      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0002" n="art0002">
        <p>BOROUGH OP WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>ELECTIONS, 1896. TO TPE LVI ELECTORS OF BEWSEY WARD. L..unEs AND GENTLEMEN.—</p>
        <p>The term for which you elected me as your Representative will shortly expire, and, in asking for a Renewal of your Confidence, I can truthfully say that the trust you then re- posed in me I have faithfully fulfilled. I have studied the test interests of the Ward. and though we still require many Sauit:ry Improvements carrying out, I have not failed to ask for them.</p>
        <p>It is far from agreeable to me to speak of what I have done, but the fact that in the last three years I have attended no less than 414 Meetings out of a possible 479 is he itself a record.</p>
        <p>I am thankful that skill in certain particulars of Municipal work has enabled me to promote Reforms and Improvements, the beneficial effects of vvh.cli my Colleagues in the Council have publicly acknowledged and all parties have assured me that they would regret if from any cause I were to leave the Council. I have therefore confidence in appealing to all the Burgesses for hearty and united support. If you deem me fit to continue the trust and duty I havo held now for nine years, I shall esteem it an honour and give the best of my ability for your welfare. I am, Ladies end Gentlemen. LYours faithfully, J. SUAW GREEN.</p>
        <p>18 King-street, Warrington, 17 October, 1896.</p>
      </div>
