      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <p>t:(; ALL'S PATENT SELF-RAISING FLOUR PREVENTS INDIGESTION.</p>
        <p>" CA .017 A CHAT.</p>
        <p>A German hosiery agent I know in the ( ity re- gards the present commercial situation as bad for German houses. Ile blesses the Emperor every day of his life for meddling in the Transvaal. Up to that time he was doing a good business. Now he can get no orders of any account in London at all. Struck by an idea that" Made in Germany" antagonism might be restricted to London, he went a tour in the provinces. There he found matters even worse, for orders could not be obtained at any price, and insults were abundant. A few jade- pendant customers continue to buy German hosiery and like it, but .there is quite a reaction against it in the metropolis. In Canada and the United States it is still possible to do business with advantage. In Australia there is a considerable falling off, as well as in Northern and Southern Africa.</p>
        <p>Lord Salisbury will be glad to have a consulta- tion with Sir Edmund Monson about the revelation Prince Bismarck has thought fit to make about the alliance he brought about batween Russia and Germany behind the back of Austria. Sir Edmund is due in town this week, and he will remain for some little time before assuming his duties as our Ambassador in Paris. Tha politics of the Continent are not developing in a way to please Prince Bismarck, and so he is bringing out some of his trump cards, though he is rather too late in the game with them.</p>
        <p>Mr. John Morley is not going to rush into the fray about the Liberal leaership with the fury and the weapons that some people expect. The line he will most likely take will be a cautions one, recommending unity, patience, and personal for- bearance. He would, as I buggestei some days ago, have preferred to remain Aleut, but he could not speak at Glasgow without referring to the sub- ject unless he wished his present silence to be ~trangely misinterpreted. Though he rather warmly supports Sir William Harcourt, he will not allow that fact to make him unjust .to Lord Rosebery, any more than respect for him will check legitimate criticism. Probably his remarks will give a new turn to affairs.</p>
        <p>As a preacher, Dr. Temple, the new Archbishop of Canterbury,has not been "popular." His sermons are always well thought out, severely logical, and full of sound commonsense. On one occasion, says the "Telegraph," he was observed sitting with the congregation in church. At the close of the service the clerygman was anxious to know bow his lord- ship liked the service and the sermon. He received cold comfort in the words, "It was an excellent text." It should be stated that he has always steered clear of partisanship. Through all the cries for re-union he has remained a staunch Church- man.</p>
        <p>The position taken up by Sir Halliday Macartney, of the Chinese Embassy. has given rise to a good deal of bitter criticism, and it has raised somt ques- tions as to the part he has played in the extra- ordinary kidnapping case whica has been the sensa- tion of the last few days. It is not known what precise advice Sir ilalliday may have tendered to the Chinese Minister, or how far he is responsible for sanctioning or lending countenance to what, has occurred. He is an English subject, and cannot divest himself of responsibility for any breach of English law to which he my be a party. But now that it is over he certainly defends the proceedings of the Chinese Legation. His English common- sense must have told him that. the doctrine that a foreign Minister may commit whatever act he pleases with a free hand,and with absolute immun- ity, in the country of his residence is the purest nonsense. Euppose a foreign ambassador, ar a mem- ber of his suite, fired at the English Sovereign or wounded the heir to the throne, or kidnapped, say, Mr. Gladstone, British justice would make short work of international fictions about the sacredness of an ambassador's person or dwelling. Nor can he be allowed to hustle a Chinaman off the streets and deprive him of his liberty. The sacredness of an ambassador's person must be dependent on his recognition of the sacredness of the laws of the land to which he is accredited. And only a dis- torted judgment could make Sir H.Macartney think otherwise.</p>
        <p>The regulations for motor cars issued by the Local Government Board are regarded as fairly sati9factory by the motorists, if I may coin a new word. They are not quite complete, but they will do very well for a start. The provisions as to brakes,tyres, lights, and width are not objected to. But considerable doubt exists as to the regulation compel ing a name and address to be written on the car. The private motor, like the private vehicle, ought,it is contended.to be exempt from this regu- lation. Only when hired, or plying for hire, ought names to be insisted upon But,in the judgment of the shrewd, this naming will gradually lead to re- gistration, and so dispose of the cycle difficulty at the same tirne,each class of vehicle being eventually taxed.</p>
        <p>Lord Northbrook has done a public service in publishing his letter to the Bishop of Winchestet on the education problem, if only for its pruden reference to the legislation of last session as it affects rating. If the largo ratepayers in rural parishes are to have half their education rates paid ter them by the State' whilst the poorer ratepayers have to pay them in full, a distinctly new griev- ance w1:1 have been created, apart from any ques- tion of conscientious objection. Whilst the Grvern- ment is relieving the occupiers of agricultural land for a period of five years and undertaking inquiry as to the burden of rating on houses and other kinds of property,it is aistinctly and obviously un- wise to impose a new general rate on the small towns and agricultural parishes.</p>
      </div>
