      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>No DIFFERENCE.—Waiter "Brown bread or white, sir !" He: "It makes no difference to me; I am colourblind." HE: "I've bought you a pet monkey to amuse you, darling." She: "Oh, how kind of you! Now I sha'n't miss you wh,n you're away."</p>
        <p>"WELL, Airy, how 'do you like your new place?" "1 can't tell yet positively; you know, the first day the ladies are always politeness itseli."</p>
        <p>CUSTOMER "is is customary to tip the waiter here?" Waiter: "Yes, sir." Customer: "Then hand over your tip. I've waited for nearly an hour."</p>
        <p>HE DESERVED TO ESCAPE.—" Have yo; been at your books, Henry ?" " Ye—yes, ma.' " Henry, are you lying to ? Your hair is wet. You've been in the brook swimming." "Ye—yes, ma; but there's books in the running brooks, you know." WIFE of his bosom (—.)ntemptuously) ; "You despicable fellow' I can't express the hatred and detestation I feel for you 1" Husband (fervently); "Thank'sh heaven ! " A CAREFUL Boy.—" Liz," said Mr. Kiljordan's youngest brother, "do you say woods is' or woods are ?'" " Woods are, of course," she answered. " Why?" " Cause Mr. Woods are down in the parlour waitin' to see yon." TOMMY'S SUCCESS.—Mrs. Cavb ker : "I am so glad that my little boy went to the head of his class this morning. How did you come to do it, Tommy ?" Tommy: "The rest of the fellows had guessed all the other ways of spelling the word." MEcfrANlcAL—Timmina : " his talk about the typewriter being a drawback to genius is all rot. I do all of my poems with a typewriter." Simmons: " You ? I had an ic.lea that you made them with a set of rubber stamps." No OCCASION FOR THOU;iIT. —" My dear daughter," said Mr. &amp;adds, " I want you to think twice before you acwpt Mr. Weevil, if he should propose." " Yes, papa," replied Miss &amp;adds., dutifully. "And you mast put an interval of ten years between the two thoughts."</p>
        <p>A MEAN LOVER. I love to make my Mabel cry, By j,.T.10n,. taunts and jeers, For then I get a chance to try And kiss away her tea.</p>
        <p>PLEASANT FOR THE NIIGILBOITIL73.—FriencI "I should think your daughter's four hours' practice on the piano would drive you crazy." Hostess: "Oh, not at all. She opens all the windows, and most of the sound gtes outside." MOTHER (severely) : "How dare you take the money from your missionary bank?" Willy: "Didn't you say I was a regular little heathen ? " Mother: "You are far worse." Willy: "Well, I was saving the money for the heathen, and first comefirst served." A POSSIBLE REASON.—Petersbe : "Why doesn't Brownkins ever send his wife to the seashore in the summer ? " Jonesly : "Don't know. I guess it is because he doesn't know what good times a fellow can have when he sends his wife there." A MATRIMONIAL MARK.—Bridget M`Gavin : " Yer owld inimy, the °widest M'Nulty gurrl, is mahrried." Mary Ann Casey: "Oi knew 't." Bridget M‘Gavin.: "how did yez know 't whin none av yez is on shpakin' turrums ? " Mary Ann Casey: "Didn't Oi mate her wid a black oye ? " FIRST Doctor: " Well, doctor, I had a peculiar case to-day." Second Doctor "What was it, please?" First Doctor : "I attended a grass widow who is afflicted with hay fever."</p>
        <p>MANsort " What was the- biggest purse you ever won on a single hand?" Stacy: "Two millions, cash." Manson, "Impossible ! Where lid you hold that hand ?" Stacy: "Here, two :f ears ago. She's my wife now." LANDLADY: " Soup, fish, joint and sweets at scven ! I told you I could not undertake late din- ners, sir !" Lodger; "Certainly not, madam. this is an early supper." Landlady ; " Oh, well, that makes a difference."</p>
        <p>Do ANYTHING 10 Papa., do lawyers ever tell the truth?" "Certainly my boy; they will do anything to win their case." IN an East-end clothing shop is a card announc- ing " Perfectly-fit ting garments. Every article sits as well as a hen." _ . .</p>
        <p>THE REASON. —Ada : "I hear that Harry Robin- son's father has started him in business,' Ida: " Yes ; I understand he couldn't get anybody to pay Harry a salary." PERMANTLY HIGH.—" Do you feel that you are wedded to high art ? " Yes; I suppose I am. I've always looked forward to moving my studio a few storeys lower; but I • guess I'll have to let ic go." :jHE HAD TOLD THAT FIB ALREADY.—Matri- monial Agent: ",Just one thing more. How old are you ? ' Miss Singler : "Twenty-five years." Agent: "Oh, well, you can easily say you are five years younger." Miss Singler : "Oh, I've done that already 1" Dowwros : "Here comes Binkers. He's got a new baby, and he'll talk us to death." Upton : "Well, here comes a neighbour of mine who has a new dog. Let's introduce them to each other, and leave them to their fate." PROFESSIONAL AMENITIES.—Dr. Killem (indig- nantly) : "What do you think? Shrouds, the undertaker, offered me twenty per cent. on all the business I would put in his way." Dr. Pillem (dryly) : "You'll be a rich man yet, Killem !"</p>
        <p>_ TURN ABOUT IS FAIR PLAY.—Mr. Upton (across the air shaft) : "I wish you folks wouldn't make so much noise; you're keeping our baby awake." Mr. Forthfloor (blandly) : Oh, we are, are we? Well, just ask your baby how he likes it himself!"</p>
        <p>_ Noitsuu..—Brown : "How is your appetite these days 1" Jones: "Fair. I can eat three meals a day onweek days, and about fifteen on Sunday."</p>
        <p>AMATEUR PHOTOGRAPHER (touring in Cheshire) : "Pardon me, sir, but would you object to my taking your daughter just as she is?" Farmer Oldun "My word, now, this is sudden! But, there, take her along and be happy." (To daughter): "Keep your eyes on him, Sal, till I scoot round for the parson." IT WAS A GOOD BUSINESS.—" Well, Jimmy, how nvich did you put in the Sunday-school box to ?" "Threepence," said Jimmy. "It was good business, too. Teacher gave me a card for being the most generous boy in the class, and I swapped it for a postage-stamp worth sixpence with Billy Wilkins."</p>
        <p>VERY STRICT.—In Germany the police regula- tions are very strict, and any violation of them is promptly punished. The people have an inherent terror of the law. Two gentlemen happened to meet in Berlin, and the following conversation, it is alleged, took place :—" Have you heard the dreadful news about Muller?" "No ; what is it?" "He was in a boat on the river. He fell overboard and was drowned. The water was too deep." " Didn't he know how to swim?" "Swim ! Don't you know that all persons are for- nolice to swim in the river ?"</p>
      </div>
