      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>ERRORS IN FOOD AND DRINK. BY A. SPECIALIST.</head>
        <p>ERRORS IN FOOD AND DRINK. BY A. SPECIALIST.</p>
        <p>It is dawning on the public to try and orevent, or at least to arrest disease. It is in prevention that .Kola olays an important part, acting solely as a first-class rultrishing food—it strengthens the system to resitt, ocpose, and overcome the attacks of disease. You'll ,i-ar someone sAy 4. Sc-and-co has a vtrAmr, eoraritu- 'inn " ; follow that up and you'll find that So-and-so ,!lows the goldon rule of being temperate in all things, aid pays attention to diet and exercise. Does he or she ep up this strong constitution by taking medicines or swallowing pills? No indeed! They have discovered that prevention is better than cure. Dr. Tibbles' Vi- Cocoa places a means in the hands of everyone to build up and maintain a sound constitution, which enables its possessor to travel his life's journey without the aches and pains which are in many cases preventible. Thus we come round again to sound comm,n-sense based on experience. Consider for one instant how the hops have played such an important part in the world's history. For hundreds of years this highly-prized vegetable pro- duct has soothed and cheered mankind. What would beer be without hops ? With or without beer (better without) it is a first-class bitter tonic and a food, and is, over and above all, what is called in medicine, a diuretic—that is, it acts specially on the kidneys. This is within the knowledge of most people. By its healthy action on the.kidneys it, in conjunction with Kola, keeps those organs in healthy working order. The amount of good done by Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa on the kidneys can- not be estimated. When you see a man or woman bloated, soft, puffy under the eyes, suspect some kidney derangement. After using Dr. Tibbles' VI-Cocoa this unhealthy dull listlessness disappears, and on trial you will see hew beneficially .Kola and Hops have acted. Do not imagine, however, because you have a pain in the small of the back that you have kidney trouble. It may be a touch of lumbago, due to a sudden cooling of the muscle in that region. which will readily yield to Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa, combined with judicious exercise and temperance. All the leading medical journals recommend Dr. Vi-Cocoa, and Dr. G. H. Haslam writes :—" It gives me great pleasure in bearing testimony to the value of Vi-Cocoa, a mixture of Malt, Hops, Kola, and Caracas Cocoa Extract. As a general beverage it excels all previous preparations. No house should be without it. " Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa is made up in ed. packets, and Pd. and is. 6d. tins. It can be obtained from all chemists, grocers, and stores, or from Dr. Tabbies Vi- Cocoa, Limited, Suffolk House, Cannon Street, London, , E. C.</p>
        <p>As an unparalleled test of merit, a dainty sample tin of Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa will bo sent free on application to any address, if when writing (a postcard will do) the reader will name the Warrington "Examiner "</p>
        <p>The Duke of Westminster has again contributed £5OO to Chester Infirmary out of the proceeds from visitors viewing Eaten. In dealing with an East End " rough " who was con- victed of an aggravated manslaughter—the victim being a woman of a certain class whom the convict had kicked to death—Mr. Justice Wright showed that he can be a terror to evil-doers. lie sentenced the convict to penal servitude for life. The convict, who is only 22 years of age, was a " strong man" who got his livelihood by having stones broken upon his chest and by lifting weights.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>MY DEAR NELLIE,</head>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE,</p>
        <p>The salient point in the new millinery is the high crown, which appears on toques as well as on larger hats. The bird of paradise plumes bid fair to oust the delicate osprey altogether; but at pre- sent these are very expensive.</p>
        <p>A i IV. very becoming to a young lady, is of a dark shadt, of red velvet, with trimming of little frills of silk-edged chiffon, black ostrich feathers putting in their appearance at the front. The straw °hat is quite of the past, although a few economical women have had their summer ones dyed, and are eking out the summer with them until the advent of winter. The boat-shaped hat is of immense popularity; it should be of felt to match the dress, ancl the trimming is very simple, just a band and side bow of corded ribbon or vel- vet, and either quills or cocks' plumes.</p>
        <p>On all the new hats and bonnets big buckles are a great feature. They should be placed in front of the high crown. If the buckle is really antiple, so much the better, but there are some good substitutes. A new tailor-made gown is of dark-green fancy canvas, the skirt having little straps of green velveteen on each side of the front breadths, and the bodice—which is perfectly plain, and should fit beautifully—has a turned-over collar high to the throat and gauntlet cuffs of velveteen, and vest of Danish red cloth or Bengaline, fastened with tiny gold and enamelled buttons set very close together. These look very smart if the button-holes are nicely worked.</p>
        <p>Another very nice dress is made in a very light shade of grey satin cloth, and has round the hem a very broad band of fine Irish lace insertion, bordered oQ either side with a hem of black velvet, whi3h in its turn is edged with a narrow band of marten. At the neck there is a square of deep red velvet, and over this, which can be worn or dispensed with at will, is a bolero of notel detail, square at the front and the back, with epaulettes and trimmed with black velvet and the Irish lace to match the hem of the skirt. On a good figure this gown would be delightful. A very striking cape may be made of steel grey watered velvet material, made to fall in pleats at the back. The collar might be of chinchilla and the yoke of scarlet satin veiled with steel and jet embroidery. A charming theatre cloak, or, rather, cape, of three-quarter length, is of cerise satin covered with fine black Jam. Steel and gold embroidery on white satin form the yoke and frontsorkle.the high collar 18 01 Thibet lamb.</p>
        <p>A sealskin doulh-breasted cape h.. large revers and an upright eolar, and the latter turns down' in front in ',pints. The new ribbons are really very charming, especially those ef moire shot with rainbow colours. Quite a novelty are flowers in the new red helio- trope shade called " aabergine," on a pale yellow ground. A fastiating ribbon is chiffon moire; quite a light soft make it lends itself well to the formation of drooping bows. The stiff erect bow, it seems, h.ls had its day, and is to be succeeded by those of the limp order. One .of the prettiest of the new iihon has a faint green ground bordered with single. violets in velvet. Quite a novelty, too, are the ribbons with 14 mother-of-pearl grounds, showered in tioy spot, of brilliant, colours. There is an immense variety of light fabrics for ball dresses, ftr,l he patterns are quite bewildering in variety. i i f the new gauzes are exceedingly pretty ; theta -, rI,2W make of silk ei4e which is quite uneruslt,',., and.very soft and dainty. The new chiffons 4re io vast variety of lovely colours, and those of water-green shades look especially well at night. Mousseline do. soie is to be had in arabesque colours, or in white with embroidered ribbon patterns. Little boys of from three to six years of age always looks nice in reefer suits which they can wear all the year round, provided a good warm sloth is selected for winter, with 'a white flannel rest up to the throat. I should always counsel the mater to buy a suit in preference to making it at home, for thick cloth is so hard to cut, sew, and press. The suits consist of the knickers and coats, with woven or flannel vest; but for house wear a little sailor blouse should be made full, with a sailor collar, and drawn in to the waist with an elastic band run into a wide hem. The blouse should be long, and loose enough to fall over the band, con- cealing it from view, and it is cut in three parts, the back and two fronts. The sleeves must be loose, drawn in with a 1.-.zat wrist-band, on which an anchor might be embroidered, matching anchors "rnrked on the corners of the broad collar. Your loving, thrum'.</p>
        <p>WHY SUFFER ONE MOMENT from torturing, di figuring skin diseases, when a warm bath with CUTICU SOAP and a single application of CIITICURA (ointment) the great skin cure, will afford instant relief, permit rest and sleep, and point to a speedy, permanent, and 400n0mica1 cure, when the best physicians and all other remedies fail. No preparations ever compounded in the history of medicine have performed the wonderful cures daily made by CIITICURA remedies. They are in truth the greatest skin curers, blood purifiers, and humour remedies of modern times, and appeal to thosa who have suffered long and hopelessly, with irresistible force</p>
