      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>'THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY OCTOBER 31, 1896 THE SUNDERED HEARTS;</head>
        <p>'THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY OCTOBER 31, 1896 THE SUNDERED HEARTS;</p>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER</p>
        <p>"WOMEN'S crur."</p>
        <p>Every Sovereign in:the world is to be formally invited to be present at the festivities ne..t Jun c in celebration of the Queen's long reign. These will be state visits,but many, including the Emperor and Empress of Russia, will be represented by proxy. The Czar and Czarina are it is said, to visit Paris incognito in the spring, but the Queen, whom the German Emperor has pressed to visit Berlin, will avoid all the European Courts, in her con- tinental holiday next year. By the way, the Queen always travels semi- incognito as the Countess of Balmoral when she goes abroad for her holiday, and the Prince of Wales fre- quently merges his identity in that of the Earl of Ches- ter, and the Empress Frederick, in the Countess von Lingen. Paris is at present wondering what title the Czar will take for his official disguise. His Majesty cer- tainly ;las a wide choice, for he has more titles and dig- nizies than any other European Sovereign. The Princess of Naples in her white satin gown and heavy train, embroidered with silver marguerites, made a very lovely bride. She is considerably taller than her husband however, who is very short. On the recom- mendation of the Queen of Italy, the Princess has had her trousseau prepared in Vienna, vhere the builders of robes and modes have been working at it night and day. It is stated that the Prince and Princess will visit Russia in the spring of next year, and that they will be the guests of the Czar and Czarina at Peterhof. . The appointment of Dr. Temple, the new Archbisho of Canterbury, has been much commented upon on ac- count of his advanced age. Dr. Temple will, in a few weeks enter upon his seventy-sixth year, but he is as active and robust as most men are at fifty or fifty-five. He is frequently to be seen on horseback taking exercise in the parks,or walking briskly along the streets. But for his gaiters and hat however, none would take him for a prelate,and he is probably the worst dressed man on the episcopal bench. His clothes are of good quality, but they always appear to have been put on anyhow. Speaking of the Archbishop reminds me of a clergy• man,who had just been given a bishopric, and was driv- ing round to leave P.P.C.cards with his old parishioners. Before starting, the unfortunate gentleman requested his new groom to go into the house and "fetch the cards,?' and each time the carriage stopped the servant was or- dered to have one or two,as the case might be. When arriving at the last house on the list, the Bishop said "Leave two cards here John." " I can't my lord," was the reply, " there's only the king of diamonds left " Many new and suitable fields of employment have, of late years opened up for women, but the latest appoint- ment which has been secured py one of our sex, is cer- tainly to be deplored. The duties of a sanitary inspec- tor are often of the most objectionable kind ; he has to go in the lowest neighbourhoods, to fri quent them at all hours, to turn out disreputahle persons of both sexes and to investigate things that need not be specified here. Only men can do this work with perfect efficiency, and wonsen should not be appointed to such a post any more than to that of a police constable. The modern-up-to-date w,,man is not supposed to look with any great favor on domestic bliss, and the cat,once supposed to be the pet of the " old maid " (a term that has long ceased to he approbrious) has assumed a posi- tion of honor to which, up to now, it has been unac- customed. More than one fashionable woman may be seen escorting her cat with a chain, after the manner of a dog, and as much attention is directed towards their breed, as to that of dogs. The Duchess of Bedford has a number of these pets with collars, and bright ribbon round their necks, who are capable of going through all kinds of wonderful performances, Apropos, while no Government, has had the temerity to put a tax on cats, most of them have looked on these particular animals with disfavour. Some of my readers may have heard of the caseiof the cat, in a Government office which suffered from the excessive red-tape habits of the officials. This office was infested with mice and after considerable correspondence, leave was given to purchase a cat, in order to reduce the pest. But no grant, was made for food, and even cats cannot live on mice alone. Another still more lengthy correspondence then took place, and finally an order for a pint of milk a day was obtained, but by that time poor pussy was dead. Whether from thirst, cr old age, history fails to relate. There is plenty of choice in thin fabrics for evening wear this winter. Grenadines, crepe de Chine, thin fabrics on the lines of alpaca,striped gauzes,plain gauzes and figured canvases are all notable for beauty of colour and texture. Russian nets too, are much en evidence, brought out in a long range of different patterns borrowed from all sources. Sometimes they have a square mesh, sometimes they are copied from Shetland knitting patterns, while every sort of variety in net weaving has been adapted to them. Care must be exercised, however, in the choice of these Russian nets, for if they are too open they are apt to catch in anything and everything to such an extent as to sorely try the temper of the most patient woman imaginable. Shaggy-haired vicuna cloths have come out this win- ter in quite a new style,they are more like the old angora shawls of years ago,soft as wadding to the touch. Many of the latest built capes of mouffian resemble these an gora and vicuna cloths, vrhile curled marabout and curled cocks' feathers are also being utilised to form this particular style of covering. It.is difficult to admire the latter mode,for these capes have a curious way of stand- ing out from the figure, suggestive of a bird. The Tam o'Shanter proves so generally becoming that</p>
        <p>it returns to us in some guise or other every season. When visiting one of our well-kno on milliners the other morning, I espied several red ones, with a close set wreath of roses of a darker shade placed beneath the brim on one side only, and surmounted by a marabout feather, standing bolt upright in what would have seemed a comical fashion s -Ins years ago. To be quite correct the Tam o'Shanter must be worn slightly on one side.</p>
        <p>Huge buckles are the feature of nearly all the latest hats and bonnets. They are placed in front, and if really antique so much the better. But a very good substitute is the new square paste and steel specimen. The stones are set apart to appear at the most advan- tage, in which respect they are decidedly preferable to the antique, whose setting is more solid. Apples are cheaper and better this year than they have been for a very long time, and first-class fruit is to be had in many shops for a penny a pound. This is partly due to the immense imports that have arrived from America. Up to a fortnight ago 600.000 barrels of United States and Canadian fruit bad arrived in Europe this season, during the same time last year imports only amounted to 36.000 barrels. One of the best methods by which to clean decanters is to take a mixture of salt and vinegar, put a dessert- spoonful into the bottle and shake thoroughly. Stains of red wine can generally be removed in this way. FIG PIIDDING.—Chop half a pound of figs and mix them with a teacupful of grated bread-crumbs, a tea- spoonful of sugar, two tablespoonfuls of melted butter, two eggs, beaten light, and five ounces of candid peel, chopped very fine. Turn into a greased mould and steam two hours and a half. Another way to serve figs is to put a pound well picked in a pan with two table- spoonfuls of Demerara sugar, one and a half pints of water and the thinly pared rind of a lemon. Simmer gently until soft, then lift out the figs and add one tea- spoonful of lemon juice to the syrup. Boil quickly about three minutes and pour over the figs. A little custard should be eaten with them when cold.</p>
        <p>" MADGE,"</p>
        <p>1100 D'S TILLS are easy to take and easy to operate is. lid.</p>
        <p>OR, A WAR OF THE HOUSEHOLD. tft •Author of "The Double Life," "The Bailiff's Scheme," &amp;0., Zto.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXI. A TERRIBLE EXPERIENCE.</p>
        <p>The Lady Barbara lingered in the shadow of the trees upon the cliff, long after Lord Champney's sail had disappeared from her view behind a pro- jecting point of land. Gradually her wild terror and excitement gave place to the cold calm of a great despair, and she sat down upon a bench, quiet and self-repressed, an infinite sorrow and apprehension brooding in her rare violet ey4s. The gardener's lad, returning from his errand to Cromer, found her sitting there when he slowly rode up the avenue of Saltair. The baroness started from her calm at sight of him, and beckoned him to approach her. He came near, dismounted, and approached her on foot, leading his horse by its bridle. "You saw the colonel ?" demanded the baroness, with a feverish anxiety. "Yes, my leddy," responded the lad, bowing his head.</p>
        <p>"What did he say 2" "He was in his room at the hotel, my leddy, and I went up to him. He read the note and smiled a queer smile, my leddy, and says he, Tell your leddy it shall be as she says,' and he gives me a note, my leddy, which here it is." He produced a dainty-looking little missive, sealed with wax, from his pocket, and gave it into the hands of the Lady Barbara. She shuddered as she took it, but said, quietly: "You have done well my lad. Here is the sovereign I promised you." "My lord came just as I was cooming away from the hotel, my leddy," said the lad, pocketing the coin. "He wor in a dreadful way, my leddy, a-wanting to know what I was a-doing there, but I didn't give un no satisfaction !" The Lady Barbara coloured a little, but managed to say, coldly, that the errand concerned Lord Champney's interests • and then, enjoining secrecy upon her messenger, dismissed him to his duties. Left alone, she tore open the perfumed note with a look of loathing. Contrary to her expectations, the letter was brief, and couched in the most respectful terms. The writer said simply that he should heed her injunctions Rnd keep out of Lord Champney's way, for that day at least, and that he should be happy to obey any further commands she might lay upon-him. The Lady Barbara's lip curled with a proud dis- dain as she tore the note into tiny scraps, and scattered them to the winds.</p>
        <p>"So far, then, Sidney is safe !" she muttered, with a deep breath of relief. "He will neither have the crime of murder on his hands, nor will he fall a victim to Effingham's murderous rage. Heaven is merciful ! Why have I doubted that mercy in my days of darkness?" She little dreamed that her own letter to Effing- jiarn had been cunningly used by her enemy to widen the gulf between her and her husband. A great load was lifted from her heart. A glad light came to her eyes—the light of a new-born hope and faith that all might yet be well. She arose and went into the house, visiting her niece in the chamber of the latter, and commenting and advising upon the bridal fineries already in course of preparation, and then she went to her own rooms overlooking the sea, _</p>
        <p>The air seemed sullenly dead and stagnant. The Lady Batha.ra opened her windows wide, and leaned out of one of them, scanning the sea.</p>
        <p>It lay before her, as far as her eye could reach, as calm and glassy as a mirror. Not a wave dis- turbed its surface. Not a white cap showed itself anywhere on the blue expanse. The wjeci to,d, died out completely. Not a breath of air could be felt. _ _ _</p>
        <p>"How stifling it is !" murmured her ladyship, fanning herself. "We B.re going to have a storm."</p>
        <p>She looked up at the sky. Already black clouds, big with menace, were massing themselves like troops ready for service. Their shadows fell heavily on the stagnant, mirror-like sea. There were no boats within view. And no sound pulsed through the still air save the wild screeching of the sea-gulls as they flew home- ward. _</p>
        <p>"We shall have a heavy storm, and soon," mused the baroness. "Sidney will be warned in time, and come home by land." With her mind quite at ease, she went into her dressing-room and made her dinner toilet. Presently she came bAck, exquisitely attired in a robe of pale blue silk, draped heavily with rare white lace, and with diamonds flashing on her neck and arms, and from the pure gold of her fair hair. Wrapping an opera-cloak about her, she went again to the window, and looked out. The sea was still calm as a mirror, but now it was black as pitch with the shadows of the menac- ing clouds, still marshalling themselves like a host in the heavens. The storm was at hand. Suddenly a zephyr faintly stirred the dead air, ruffling the sea into tiny ripples. "It is coming," said the Lady Barbara. Another zephyr thrilled the sea. A low murmur came from the dead-looking waters, and was echoed by the trees on the cliff. Another dead silence; then a gust of wind swept through the trees and about the mansion with a wild, shrill wail—the cry of the coming storm. A moment later the storm had broken.</p>
        <p>The Lady Barbara, leaning from her window, watched the wild waves leap and toss and curl themselves in a mad fury against the cliff, fling- ing back upon the sea the white froth of their anger—watched the high snowy crests far out on the inky waters, and was appalled at the sudden dal kness that seemed shutting heavily down upon the scene. _</p>
        <p>" Heaven pity any boat out in this storm," she thought. For some time she stood there, fascinated by the bitter war of the elements, awe-struck and frightened ; but suddenly she started, becoming deadly pale, and clung to the window frame in a sick terror. She had seen, in a sudden gleam of light, a little pleasure-boat out on the wild sea, her mast and sails bent, as she was, being driven toward the rocky coast before the gale. "lt is Sidney's boat 1" she cried aloud, in a frenzy. "My God! He is being driven to his death The shadows shut down between the boat and the shore, and she waited for another friendly gleam to reveal to her the positionof the endangered craft. The gleam came and revealed to her, beyond a doubt, that the boat was Lord Champney's. "He may have sent it home by a fisherman," she thought, catching at the slightest straw of hop. "Of course he has. She caught up a glass lying on the window-sill, and, waiting for the next lift in the gloom, scanned the boat with wildly questioning eyes. It was no fisherman who sat in the storm-driven boat, but Lo'rd Champney himself, as the baroness saw. His lordship had left Cromer just before the sud- den calm, had lain on the waters outside Cromer Bay during the dead lull, but had taken advan- tage of the earliest breeze to •ij homeward. He had been warned by the fish,rmen in the bay of his danger, but in his desperate mood lie had scoffed at their warnings, and found a fierce delight in facing the mad gale before which they were fleeing. The light and the darkness alternately revealed and concealed the boat and its single occupant, playing with the fears and the anguish of the Lady Barbara. At last, unable to remain longer in her safe shelter while he was exposed to such terrible peril, the Lady Barbara drew her cloak over her head, and ran clown stairs and out upon the cliff, where, clinging to the balustrade, and blown about by the storm, she watched and waited in a breathless sus- pense. The storm deepened and raged with a desperate fury, and on drove the little pleasure-boat before the storm, her sails stripped off, her mast broken short, and her hull the sport of winds and waves. "He will strike on Cranny beach, a hundred rods above here !" cried the baroness. "The Cranny rocks will be his death. Heaven help him !"</p>
        <p>She ran back to the mansion and aroused her household. Then she hurried to the Cranny beach, the butler and his male assistants speedily following with the few appliances that might be needed should Lord Ghampuey be wrecked on the Cranny rocks. The Cranny beach was a low strip of shingle, intersected with numerous low-lying rocks. Off the beach were the Cranny rocks, a stretch of half sunken black rocks, over which the inky, white-crested waters were surging, with the peculiar roar which always characterises breakers,</p>
        <p>And it was upon these dangerous rocks that Lord Cba,mpney's boat was inevitably drifting I On it came, leaping the crests like a tortured animal. The baron had long since ceased to hold the tiller, or exercise any control over the dis- masted craft, and now sat erect, his arms folded across his chest in the stern attitude his wife so well remembered, the salt spray dashing against his cheeks and his hair.</p>
        <p>The Lady thibara paced the beach, moaning and wringing her hands. At last the boat was driven full into the breakers —full upon the half-sunken Cranny rocks—and the wife closed her eyes to shut out the horrible peril of him she loved.</p>
        <p>"The boat's going to pieces I" cried the butler, excitedly. "There she goes I Oh, my poor lord I" The Lady Barbara looked instinctively toward the rocks.</p>
        <p>The boat had gone to pieces, having struck uron a sharp-pointed rock. Lord Champney was struggling in the wild yeast of waters. The wife sank on her knees on the wet beach in a voiceless prayer. The men shouted to each other and to their im- perilled master, their voices and the wind creating a horrible confusion in the ears of the anguished woman. A sudden faintness swept over her. A darkness shrouded her vision. She clutched blindly at the empty air, gasping for breath. A joyful shout at length recalled her to herself. "He's safe, my lady 1" cried the butler. "He's safe ashore !"</p>
        <p>She opened her eyes, and beheld lying on the beach, at her very feet, where the sea had cast him, Lord Champney, pale, dripping and senseless. She bent over him and felt his pulse. "He is not dead I" she cried, in a hysteric voice, "We may save him yet. Quick I Help me I" From that moment she gave herself up to his re- covery. She examined his wounds, ordered him to be taken to his chamber and put to bed, and dis- patched a servitor for the nearest physician, who was at Cromer.</p>
        <p>Lord Champney's injuries proved to be serious. His right arm was broken, his shoulder badly con- tused, and he was in a state of great physical ex- haustion.</p>
        <p>The doctor, when he came, ordered perfect quiet and absolute rest, declaring that any excitement might prove fatal to him. The baroness, who had constituted herself her husband's nurse, promised that these directions should be observed to the letter.</p>
        <p>The physician had scarcely departed, when Lord Champney turned restlessly on his pillow, moaning with pain. The Lady Barbara fancied that he was trying to speak. "What is it, Sidney ? " she asked, bending over him.</p>
        <p>The sound of her voice, low and soft and tremu- lous as it was, and freighted with love, tenderness and anguish, aroused the baron from his stupor as if it had been a trumpet sounding for battle. • He opened his eyes and glared at her. "Traitress 1" he whispered, in bitter scorn. "False wife ! Leave me ?"</p>
        <p>"0, Sidney !" she murmured, humbly. "0, my husband! Hear me—"</p>
        <p>"I know all!</p>
        <p>"Go !" he interrupted, fiercely. Go! And send me the butler." His manner was so excited and violent that nothing remained to the wife but obedience. She summoned the butler, and went to her own room. ' "It is true that he has ceased to love me," she thought, with a sense of desolation keener than any she had yet experienced. "His jealousy no longer springs from an excess of love, but from hatred. Our hearts are indeed sundered I"</p>
        <p>Meanwhile Lord Champney gave an order that Felix Warner should be telegraphed for at once, and a messenger was sent to Norwich to execute the commission.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXII. TWO WRONGED AND SUFFERING HEARTS.</p>
        <p>The Lady Barbara did not return to the invalid's chamber during the remainder of the afternoon nor the evening. Lord Cha.mpney did not at once speak his wife's name, but turned his face to the wall and moaned continually. At ten o'clock the butler, who was serving as nurse, administered to his patient the opiate the doctor had commanded, and Lord Champney sank into restless slumbers. - - -</p>
        <p>About an hour later, as the butler sat dozing in the dimly lighted chamber, a low knock was heard on the door, and the Lady Barbara stole in like a spirit. She had donned a wbite dressing-gown, and was evidently prepared for a night of watching. "Is his lordship asleep, Bugg?" she asked halt- ing sear the door and looking wistfully toward the bed. _</p>
        <p>"Yes, my lady," replied the butler, arousing himself. "He has had a sleeping powder, and won't wake up till morning." The baroness approached the bed and bent over the sleeper. His brows were contracted with pain, his face was ghastly in its pallor, and he moved restlesly under her glances. "You look sleepy, Rugg," said her ladyship, turning to the butler. "You may go to bed, and I will watch. If any change occurs in his lord- ship's condition, I will summon you." The butler gratefully accepted his release, and retired. Then the Lady Barbara, lowering still further the light, sat down by the bedside, and gathered the battered and weary bead of the sleeper in her arms, pillowing it on her breast. • " My darling 1" she whispered, kissing the thin, dark cheeks softly, and with an exquisite tender- ness. "My poor Sidney! My precious husband!" As if the loving epithets penetrated his slum- bers, he opened his eyes and looked up into the glorious face above him. But as she shrank back, fearing discovery, his el elids drooped again heavily, and his breathing became more regular. It was a long vigil, that of the proud, wronged, and loving wife over her husband, but it was not a bitter one. She bathed his face with her tears, and dried it with her kisses. All the passionate pride that distinguished her deserted her now, and she was only the loving, sorrowing, forgiving, tender wife. She whispered in his sleep-sealed ears declarations of her innocence of any wrong, and reproached him with tears for his cruel suspicions of her ; and with the swift scarlet blushes dyeing her cheeks, she told him how she loved him. If he could but have heard her! If he could but have seen the light in her radiant eyes, or marked the sweet and steady smile upon her lovely mouth! How, under all her pride and coldness and just anger, she loved him. How—if she had but known it—under all his mad and jealous fury, his ceaseless suspicions, he worshipped her. The hearts of the two were the footballs of an enemy, and neither suspected the truth. Noble, grand and truthful, well fitted for each other and for domestic happiness their lives had been a desolation and a ruin, their weaknesses turned into weapons against them by Felix Warner. The night came to an end at last, and the sum- mer sunshine, sweet and calm after the storm, streamed softly through the curtains. "I must go," thought the Lady Barbara, her tears falling. "He may soon awaken. He mus not find me here!" She bent her face to that of her husband, and showered upon his cheeks and brow a rain of passionate kisses. Then she laid the dark head back on the lace-trimmed pillow, and tore herself She touched the bell, summoning the butler, and when he came, she went to her own room. Lord Champney, however, did not awaken for hours—until near noon—but when he did he was, except for his broken arm and bruises, himself again. He lay a little while in silence, and then he</p>
        <p>"Rugg, what time is it?" "A quitrter to twelve, my lord," replied the butter, t.pproaching the bed.</p>
        <p>The. baron groan; d. "And I had an appointment at noon !" he mut- tered. "Rugg, you must go down to Cranny beach with a messege for me to Colonel I fugham, who will be there—' "I think not, my lord," replied the butler, as Lis lordship paused. "Colonel Effingham called this morning to see if you got home from Cromer safe—" He called ?" Yes, my lord. ' Did Lady Champney see him I don't know, my lord. The glitter of the baron's eyes, his labouring breath, and the feverish flush on his cheeks, all frightened the worthy butler, being signs of an intensity of passion such as hail never disturbed the peaceful current of his own existence. The baron fell back upon the bed without a word. He submitted to the ministrations of his attendant, and his toilet was made, and be was</p>
