      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>OUR REGARDS TO MR. RUSSELL.</head>
        <p>OUR REGARDS TO MR. RUSSELL.</p>
        <p>The writer of these lines hereby tenders to Mr. W. Clark Russell the assurance of his thanks and apprecia- tion. I have always loved sea stories, and those of Mr. Russell stand at the head of their class. From "The Wreck of the Grosvenor" to "List, Ye Landsmen! " I have read them all. Yet salt water, and the things thereon and therein, are not the only things he knows about; not by many degrees of latitude.</p>
        <p>lln his last book he makes a sailor talk thus: "I have suffered from the liver in my time, and know what it is to have felt mai. I say I have known moments when I could scarce restrain myself from breaking windows, kicking at the shins of all who approached me, knock- ing my head against the wall, yelling with the yell of one who drops in a fit; and all the while my brain was as healthy as the healthiest that ever filled a herean skull, and nothing was wanted but a musketery of calo- mel pills to dislodge the fiend," &amp;c., &amp;c. So much for what Mr. Russell's sailor (or Mr. Russell himself, says; an thero are p! enty of people who can testify that this is not a bit overdrawn. One fact in particular it helps us to realise—namely, that the life of a sailor does not guarantee good health. Indigestion and dyspepsia—of which liver complaint is a sequence and a symptom—is as common among sailors as among landsmen.</p>
        <p>One of the latter, however, may now tell of his ex- perience. " All my life," he says," I had suffered from biliousness and sick headaches. I would have an attack about every three weeks. At such times my appetite left me, and I could neither eat nor drink for days to- gether. I suffered from dreadful sickness and straining, and vomited a greenish-yellow fluid. My head felt as though it would burst. I had a bad taste in the mouth, sallow skin, and the whites of the eyes turned yellow. I was recommended to adopt a vegetarian diet, and did so, but the attacks were just as frequent and vio ent. I consulted doctors and took their medicines,but was none the better for it. In this way I went on year after year." Well, we shall agree that there could scarcely be a worse way to go, and it all came about thus : The over- worked stomach put more work on the liver than the latter could do. Indignnt and disgusted at this the liver refused to do a stoke more than its proper share. Hence more bile accumulated in the blood than the liver was able to remove. This surplus bile acts as a slow poison—and not so very slow either. The tongue is furred; the head aches and feels dull and heavy ; the eyes and skin are greenish yellow ; there is dizziness an nausea; cold hands and feet; spots before the eyes ; a pungent, biting fluid rises into the throat; constipatioia ; high coloured kidney secretion; prostrated nerves; irrit- ability ; loss of ambition ; fears , and foredodings, Sze., Ste.</p>
        <p>This is " biliousness," or "liver complaint" in its simplest form. When long unchecked it produces irregu- lar action of the heart, rhuematism, gout, and any, or all, of a dozen other organic disorders. There is no more certain or powerful impulse to misbehaviour ; suicide and other crimes often resulting. "What to do ? To get rid of the poison by;starting the skin and bowels into energetic action ; then to keep them going at a healthy and 'natural gait. ..A How to do this? Let our friend Mr. F. Widger, 4, Portland Square, Plymouth—whom we have just quoted—speak on that point. In his latter, dated March 3rd, 1893, he adds: "Two years ago, after all medicines had failed to help me, I first heard of Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup. I procured it prom Mr. R. S. Luke, Chemist, Tavistock Road, and began to use it, and nothing else. After hav- ing consumed one bottle! found myself vastly better, and by continuing with it I got rid of my old trouble al- together." We should mention that Mr. Widger is a tailor and outfitter at Plymouth, and well known and respected in that community. He permits us to use his name out of gratitude for his recovery. The potency of Mother Sei- gel's Curative Syrup over liver disease is due to its ability to cure indigestion and dyspepsia,which is (as we have said) the cause of liver disease. Every house on the land, and every ship on the sea, should have this remedy as a necessary part of their stockand stores. Perhaps Mr. Russell may recommend it in his next book. But no "musketry of calomel pills." Oh, no.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>AROUND THE MEDITER-</head>
        <p>AROUND THE MEDITER-</p>
        <p>ANEAN. BY DONA M. JONES.</p>
        <p>1.-ACROSS THE BAY TO LISBON.</p>
        <p>SS. Midnight Sun. I suppose no set of people ever went to sea more plentifully provided with the commodity known as Job's comfort than the party whose adventures I am about to chronicle. We were bound for the Mediterranean, and many were the dark presages as to our fate. Perhaps some people may remember the gales that immediately preceded the 29th of September, and while oaks were bending and tiles flying in every direction, and news of casualties by land and sea com- ing in by every pest, it did seem somewhat foolhardy to brave the rage of the Atlantic. In spite of all, however,our spirits,athich had been some- what damped by these rernonstrances,rose insensibly as, after forcing our way through the crowd of people at St. Pancras station who had came to speed the travellers, and securing seats in the special train which had been chartered to take the party down to Tilbury, we found ourselves speeding through the Essex flats, with their rows of pollarded willows, and red sails apparently ris- ing out of the fields, though as we came nearer we saw the river creeping between the low banks. In about an hour we reached Tilbury station and had the pleasure of perceiving the long black side of the s.s. " Midnight Sun," which was to be our floating home for so long,ris- ing above the quay side. After a sleep which not even the unaccustomed noises of a first night at sea could disturb. I was roused about half-past six by the arrival of the matutinal tea and biscuit. After breakfast the deck chairs drew into groups under the awnings, old travelling companions met -again and recalled their past adventures, and neighbours at table or in cabins improved their acquaintance, and discussed the pros- pects of the voyage. Most of us knew before we started on the cruise that Sir Lambert Playfair, who was returning to Algiers en the "Midnight Sun," would probably give us a lec- ture or two on board, and we were looking forward eagerly to the chance of getting some information on the countries we were to visit from one of the greitest living authorities on the history and antiquities of the Mediter- ranean coast. We were not disappointed. At five o'clock the large smoking-room on deck was _packed full, and Sir Lambert was introduced to his audienee by the Hon. Mr. Justice Ross. The lecturer dealt in the first place with the physical configuration of the Medi- terranean, showing the strong similarity of flora, fauna, and general contour between the northern and southern shores of the Great Sea, and pointed out that the Medi- terranean was once divided into an eastern and a wes- tern basin, separated by a barrier of land where is now the island of Sicily. He then gave a sketch of the Medi- terranean as a trade route, pointiag out that the Roman domination had ratl.er checked than otherwise the com- merce developed by the Pheenicians. The Arab invasion and the Crusades developed a trade with the East of which Venice was the chief emperium, and to which the discevery of the sea route to India round the Cape of Good Hope proved a death blow. The importance of the Mediterranean as a trade-route was revived when Ferdinand de Lessepg engineered the Suez Canal, and the only thing that could threaten it in the future would be the outbreak of an European war, in which case Ensgs..id. "the carrier of Vie world," might be compelled to revert to the longer route.</p>
        <p>We passed through the dreaded fbay, which put on its best behaviour for us. In the afternoon we ran into a shoal of porpoises,which kept up with the ship or some time,and created great amusement. leap- ing out of the water in pairs, like trained circus ponies. After dinner that evening cur indefatigable committee had an entertainment ready for us, including an excel- lent conjuring performance by the doctor in charge. The following dar,about noon,we sighted Finisterre-- low sandy hills, and then a line of purple cliffs running out to the headland and the lighthouse. That afternoon Sir Lambert Playfair lectured on the Punic Wars, the Empire of Rome in Africa, the Early Christian Church, laying special emphasis; on the great names of Augustine and Cyprian and the Vandal invasion. Going on deck before breakfast next day I found a crowd of passengers aft, with their eyes fixed on a faint, misty line of cliff on the left, which gradually took on distinctness, and assumed the outline of Lisbon Rock, crowned with its old castlQ. By-and-by we had the pleasure,which no one can appreciate who has not spent three days in open sea,of feasting our eyes on the details of the coast, red sandstone cliffs, with bare reddish. brown hills abr)ve, shading into exquisite tints of palish purple, strips of yellow beach, white churches,red-roofed villas, and patches of bright green garden. By-and-by we rounded the point, and found ourselves steaming up the wide channel of the Tagus, with its smiling shorea. Then the channel of the river widened into a spacious</p>
        <p>harbnurmay with various kinds of shipping,big steamers, an ocean tramp or two, weather-beaten and shabby,and any amount of small sailing craft, manned by bronze- coloured sailors in blue cotton. In front of us was Lis- bon, with its houses piled tier above tier to the summit of tke amphitheatre of hills which surround the harbour, red and yellow warehouses lining the quay.white domes, and cupolas, tufts of green trees and red-tile 4 houses, looking down on the harbour with its multitudinous shipping—all this bathed in a sunshine which made us realise that at last we were in the South.</p>
        <p>On reaching the quay we got into the carriages that were waiting for us, and drove through a handsome square, the Plaza Commerciale, through the business quarter of the town, and past the market. Here the houses were high and fine, and the streets fairly wide. The market was curious, with the great piles of grapes and melons on the stalls,and the women in print skirts, woollen shawls, and gay silk handkerchiefs tied round their heads, watching us with amusement in their black eyes. Then we found ourselves in another square, with exotie-looking plants and fountains and statates,and from this there ascended a very steep hill, up which we were laboriously tugged. ladeed,the streets of Lisbon gener- ally realise Dickens's description of the streets in Bath, where he says that they are like the perpendicular streets a man sees in a dream which he cannot get up for the life of him. At the top of this particular hill ot diffi• culty were the Botanical Gardens, very prettily laid out and planted with trees,in the shade of which we were glad to rest for a season, and to enjoy the magnificent view of Lisbon spread out before us. Coming down from the gardens, we halted in the middle of a more than usually precipitous street,opposite a door- way in a yellow sandstone wall, which we entered after our guide, without the least idea where we were going, crossed a courtyard, dived through several passages,and found ourselves at last in the Church of St. Roque. Outside, this building much resembles a Nonconformist meeting-house of the old style ; inside, it is square and graceless, with a gorgeous high altar, however, and elaborately decked chapels on either side. The most re- markable of these is the chapel of St. John, with its lapis-lazuli pillars, and panels of coloured marble, its gilt bronze-work, and the marvellous mosaics, which are said to have cost nearly .4.17,000,000,and to be among the finest in the world. They are copies of paintings of Guido and Michael Angelo in the Vatican of Rome. After lunch we drove along a boulevard parallel with the quay. Another terrible climb brought us to the Royal Palace,standiag in a sort of park.planted with olives, acacias, eucalyptus, &amp;c.,which had a ragged and forlorn took to our English eyes. Here, as every- where, except perhaps in the newest quarter of the town, we received an impression of melancholy and de- cay, the quays along which we passed bad a semi-de- serted look, the soldiers in their baggy canvas uniforms, the public buildings and conveyances, even the private carriages, were utterly wanting in that smartness, neat- ness.and attention to detail which form part of the Eng- lish idea of comfort. • _ _</p>
        <p>Our next visit was to the Church and Convent of Be- lem (or Bethlehem), in a poor and straggling suburb, from which a tramcar runs to Lisboa. They were buil' near the old Moorish town of Belem ti celebrate the dis- covery of Vasco de Gama. As we passed up by the side of the church we could see where the plaster had fallen off in great patches at the time of the earthquake. Coming back, we retraversed the poorer quarters of the town, and were struck with the stately carriage of the women, one old dame with a brown water pitcher on her head might have stood for a St. Anne of Murille's. We saw another sitting on her doorstep, cooking the evening meal on a little portable stove, with one arm around a black-eyed baby, to held it back from personal investition of the compound in the saucepan. Some of the women on the doorsteps were basket-weaving, but most of them were merely chatting and dozing; others were lolling at the windows with their arms on the sills. The dark:eved children were bewitching in their little brown or blue shirts, Nothing can give an idea of the quaintness of the pink and blue houses with the door and window frames badly in want of paint. Staggering up and down the!hilly streets,we saw men carrying bag- gage on a yoke, and other porters with badges. rho Lisbon porters are supposed to be exceptionally power- ful, and it is said that one of these men has bees seen to pick up a grand piano and carry it off on his head. But as dear old Sir John Mandeville is not the only traveller who has been known to diverge from strict ac- curacy, I give this statement for what it is worth. The sun was going down in a red sky and twilight coming quickly on, as it does in these latitudes, when we stopped at the gate of the English cemetery. Avenues of solemn cypresses Etood up darkly against the red flame of sunset, and between, the white tombstones glimmered. We soon found the grave of poor Henry Fielding, who sleeps beneath an epitaph crediting him with all the virtues ; but we had more difficulty with the tomb of Dr.Doddridge.which we discovered at last in a thicket of geraniums. Beth poet and novelist came out to Lisbon broken men, in the hone of gaining some benefit from the delicious climate of the place, but in vain. Doddridge died almost immediately on landing, and Fielding's respite was not much longer. It seemed strange and sad that perhaps the most typically English of our great writers should lie in a foreign laud.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>FXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 31, 1896.</head>
        <p>THE FXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 31, 1896.</p>
        <p>SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>LAST WEEK'S TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>Notwithstanding the depression in the coasting trade at Runcorn, Wedton Point, and other places in the lower portion of the canal, the volume of shipping using the port of Manchester last week was the largest this month. During the seven days ended October 23, 109 sea-going careo ve.sels, with an aggregate net registered tonnage of 50,058 tons, entered and sailed from the Snip Canal, as compared with 120 vessels of 36,588 tons in the pre- ceding week. Forty-three vessels of 32,618 tons arrived from or sailed for foreign ports, and 66 vessels of 17,442 ' tone entered or left coastwise. Cotton to the amount of 5,308 bales arrived by steamers from New York, New Orleans and Alexandria ; five cargoes sf timber, wood- pulp, and general merchandise were imported from the Balt:c; three cargoes of green and dried fruit from Spain and the Mediterranean, two general cargoes from New York, two from Rotterdam,aad one each from Hamburg Antwerp and Ghent, Boulogne, Rouen, Paris, Bordeaux, and the Levant. A cargo of ore arrived from Bilbao,stnd about four thousand tons of grain from America, whilst 242 head of cattle, sheep and pigs were landed at Traf- ford Wharf from Ireland. Three steamers and a barque cleared for Brazil via coast ports ; five steamers and a barque for .Baltic and Scandinavian ports; eight steamers for the Continent,and one each for Brunswick (Georgia), Bombay and Australia. A cargo of pitch was shipped to Cette.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>DISCOVERY AT EIGHT-A -SIX.</head>
        <p>DISCOVERY AT EIGHT-A -SIX.</p>
        <p>At Keinton Mandeville, in Sam ersetshire, lives an old gentleman, Mr. George Brooks, whose nervous system is, he says, .as robust as at any time in his life,and whose eyesight is remarkable for a man of his years. These happy conditions he attributes to quite a providential discovery. Mr. Brooks, in response to a " Somerset Express" representative, said he was in his eighty-sixth year, and for twenty years had suffered more or less from rheuma- tism. But he is now thankful to say that that pain is much less, whilst the aching round the waist which he used to experience has nearly gone. "How was this brought about 2"</p>
        <p>" Well," said Mr. Brooks, "about a year and a half ago I had a very severe illness, irt fact, I almost had a fit once or twice. I took some of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, which, in addition to greatly relieving me of my rheumatic pains, did for me what I much more value then that—entirely took away the sensation of fits, and that was brought about after I bad only taken about ten Pills. In addition to this, my eyesight has improved almost as much as any part of my body." "Flays you tried any other medicine for your rheu matism ?" the reporter asked.</p>
        <p>"Yes, every medicine brought out during the last twenty years, but Dr. Willigms' Pink Pills have given me more relief than all the others."</p>
        <p>Stronger testimony to the efficacy of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills could not he given. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills directly enrich and purify the blood, and thus it is that they are so famous for the cure of rheumatism,scrofula, chronic erysipelas, and to restore pale and sallow com- plexions to the glow of health. They are also a splendid nerve and spinal tonic, and thus have cured many cases of paralysis, locomotor ataxy,nouralgia, St.Vitus' dance, and nervous headache. They are aow obtainable of all chemists,and from Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, 46, Holborn-viaduct,London, E.C., at 2s. 9J. a box, or six for 13s. 93., but are genuine only with full name, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People.</p>
        <p>A warrant was issued at Aberdeen on Tuesday for the arrest of William Sim, solicitor, on chlrees of embezzl- ing money entrusted to him for investment. Tynan arrived at hew York on Tuesday morning on board the Saale. He boasted that his errand had been of far greater importance than dynamite, and that he had been successful beyond his wildest dreams.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER CURE OF COLD AND SORE THROAT Er DR. Lo- COCK'S PULMONIC WAFERS.—Mr. Wilkinson. Chemist, 35, Watson-street, Birkenhead. writes :—" Mr. Ravenscroft (a neighbour of mine), the other day, having been exposed on the boat, took cold and sore throat: he pro,ured a box of youi Wafers, and was quite cured by it (one box)." In Asthma. Consumption. Bronchitis, Coughs. Rheumatism, and all Hysterical and Nervous Complaints, instant relief and a rhpid cure is given by the Wafers, which taste pleasantly. Sold,byfall Druggists, at is. 11d. and 2e. 9d, per box.—Advt</p>
