      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>MARGE Al4-AINST MR. HAMPSON OF WARRINGTON. I</head>
        <p>THE MARGE Al4-AINST MR. HAMPSON OF WARRINGTON. I</p>
        <p>ADJOURNED HEARING.</p>
        <p>SOLICITORS AT VARIANCE.</p>
        <p>COMMITTAL OF PRISONER TO THE ASSIZES.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Court, on Monday morning, before the Mayor (Alder- man Fairclough) in the chair, Alderman Wright, Alderman Harrison, Councillor Pierpoint, and Messrs. F. Monks, T. Birchen, J. W. Hayward, and S. Wright, John Hampson, described as a commission agent, living in Church-street, was brought up on remand charged with haying on the 26th Sept ember,at No. 10,Glazehrook-street,inflicted grievous bodily harm upon an old man named Joseph Cowlishaw with intent. Mr. Jenkins prose- cuted; Mr. Browne appeared for the defendant Harnpson, and Mr. Percy Davies for Ntrs. Cowli- shaw. The court was crowded and considerable in- terest was taken in the proceedings, which com- menced at half-past ten and continued until 1.15. The prisoner, flampson was accommodated with a seat in the dock. Cowlishaw, the complainant,who appeared in court for the first time, was assisted to a chair between the solicitors' desks. He is rather a fine looking old man with white hair and beard. Mr. Browne said he had an application to make to the Bench,which he thought under the circumstances would be fair to all parties. It was said defendant went home with the complainant to supper, and that a struggle afterwards took place, in consequence of something said by the wife. Up to that time Hampson and Cowlishaw had been the best of friends, and Hampson did not know the wife until he saw her at the house. The com- plainant's ankle was broken. He suggested that this was a case where compensation would practically meet the case without any further inquiry taking place into the faets. The defendant was 17 years in the West Indies, in her Majesty's service,for 14 years he was Con- servative agent for Warrington, he was now in business on his own account, and for 16 days he had been in Strangeway's prison. He was injured himself on the night in question, and he thought, therefore, that corn- pensatton would meet the case. He did not think the complainant had any vindictive feeling towards Hemp- son. What advantage would it be to the complainant for the defendant to be sent to the Quarter SC3SiOII9 for trial Even if Hampson were imprisoned, what good would it do Cowlishaw ? The case had not turned out as seriously as was expected and he requested the bench to exercise their discretion in the matter. Mr. Jenkins said he hoped he should be the last man not to extend a kindly feeling to any man placed as Mr. Ha.mpson was. Mr. Browne had however, forgatten one important element in the consideration of the case and that was the duty, he, Mr. Jenkins owed the public, for it. protection. He regarded it as his duty not to give way in the slightest degree. There was, however, one thing he would do. If Mr. Hampson was committed for trial he would do his utmost to see that anything reason- able in the way of minor indictments being preferred aeainst him, and then let the responsibility rest on the Chairman of the Quarter Sessions,rr tie Judge of Assize. What would be the opinion outside if Mr. Browne's sug- gestion was agreed to? Tney often heard remarks about there being one law for the rich and another for the poor. It would be a scandal to the cause of justice to accede to the request. He felt it was his duty not to swerve one iota. If the complainant said he would do what Mr. Browne suggested, then he (Mr. Jenkins) would re- turn his brief to his clerk and leave the court. Other- wise to approve the suggestion was like compounding a felony. Xt.. Browne : I had a conversation with Mr. Jenkins before the Court and he agreed that I should make the application. Mr. Jenkins: Well, and you have made it. Mr. Browne intimated that Mr. Jenkins had agreed not to oppose it. Mr. Jenkins: Yeu must be dreaming, Mr. Browne, dreaming. The Chief Constable: I placed the charge and no one else can withdraw it. Councillor Pierpoint : I take it that we have no right to compound a felony. Mr. Browne: You have if the intent is withdrawn. Councillor Pierpoint : It is an act of condonation we ought not to be parties to. Mr. Browne: If the case goes to the Sessions or to the Assizes, the authorities will be simply in the same posi- tien as yourself. Mr. Jenkins: But they have manifestly higher powers. (To the com?lainant :) Do you wish the case withdrawn ? Complainant (excitedly) : I wish to go as far as the law will know. He did not care for my life. Mr. Jenkins then stated the facts of the case at great length. He referred to the visit of the complainant to the Ring-o'-Bills in the afternoon, after he had finished his work. Here he had a pint or two of beer. He then went home.and after tea went to the market with his wife,and ad'. in the town his wife had three glasses of whiskey a. dienplainant three glasses of ale. The wife brought back with her a noggin of whiskey for use on the Sunday for her husband, and on the way home they stopped at the Cross Keys Inn, Buttermarket street, but did not go into the house. The wife went horne,and the complainant went to the Ring-o'-Bells for a pint of beer, and at a quarter to eleven he met Flampson.who appeared to be in a merry conditi .n.He had a bottle of champagne and asked complainant to have some of it, but he de- clined. At closing time they went out together and prosecutor asked orisoner to come to supper with him. They went arm in arm to Cowlishaw's home, and at the door prosPcuter remarked in a surprised tone "Do you live here," and Cowlishaw said " Yes." They went in and prisoner told his wife to get supper ready. Hemp- son and • Mrs. Cowlishaw were under the in- fluence of drink. but Cowlishaw was the best of the three. During supper Mrs. Cowlishaw went up to Hampeon, put her hands on his cheeks, and said " I do love an old soldier," and flampson replied, "And I love you." Complainant at last got tired of that sort of thing, and told the two to desist, flicking his wife with his fingers, and telling Hampson to go home. Hampson became annoyed, lost his temper, caught hold of Cowlishaw by the shoulders, and they had a struggle. Flampson knocked him deem, fell on the top of hitn.and complainant felt that his leg was broken, but Hampson was not aware of that fact. Hatnpson at this time threatened to kill the complainant, and while he was kneeling upon him he took a knife out of his pocket and jagged Cowlishaw's throat with it. Mrs. Moston,a most respectable woman, would corroborate that in every particular. On the throat a long incised wound would be found, but complainant's strong beard prevented the injury being greater than it was. There was no mistake about the taking out of the knife and using it in such a manner that it was calculated to do serious injury. It was a pity the matter did not rest there, but Hampsen centinued to use such expressions as" I will kill you. I am going to murder you. I will murder you." Address- ing the wife, the defendant said "Bring me a rope and I will hang the old--." Fortunately, there was no rope in the house, but a strong cord was brought, and Hampson put it about the old man's neck and very nearly throttled him. It was the wife who brought the cord. Mr. Davies objected to the introduction of the name of the wife into the case. The Bench ought to know that anather charge was to be preferred against the wife. Mr. Jenkins must confine himself to what Elamp- son did. There was no charge yet against Mrs. Cowli- shaw. Mr. Jenkins: I am not goirg to allow you to know how I am going to conduct my case, or anyone else. Mr. Davies : He is acting the part of a dictator. The Deputy-Clerk: If the old man saw his wife bring the rope ho is bound to say so. Mr. Birchall : I don't see how the introduction of the name of the wife can be avoided, if the facts of the case are to be made clear. _ -</p>
        <p>Mr. Jenkins nontinued his speech, and said the com- plainant asked his wife if she was going to see him finished out and out. He also asked her to go for assist- ance, but she only laughed. Eventually the complain- ant knocked on the wall with a stick, and the attention of the neightnurs was attracted to the house. Mrs. Moston cam in, and she would corroborate the evidence of the old rnan in mast particulars. Her husband went with her, and he would also give evidence. Until P.C. Condliffe came on the scene they were unable to get the cord off the man's neck. Condliffe adroitly removed it and relieved the old man from his painful position. His wounds were washed, and he was taken on the ambu- lance, to the Infirmary, where the injury to the throat was discovered as well as the breaking of the right leg at or near the ankle. Cowlishaw was three weeks in the Infirmary, and on the 17th October be was taken to his home, where he had since been confined. The Bench saw his condition at that moment. The evidence was then called. Mr. Monks referred to the slow procedure of taking down the evidence of the witnesses in longhand. It was a legacy of the past,and he thought shorthand should be used. Alderman Harrison: But how could the shorthand be read over to the witnesses? _</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Monks: Easily enough. The reporters have to read it over. _ _ _</p>
        <p>........._.__ _ . __ _ Alderman Harrison : I foresee great difficulties in the way. Joseph Cowlishaw, complainant, said he was a wire drawer. He was married to his wife in August,lB9s. He</p>
        <p>VA I I 04.</p>
        <p>BEAUTIFUL TEETH for all who use daily on the tooth brush a few drops of SOZODONT, the pleasantest dentifrice in the</p>
        <p>world. • Cleanses the teeth anil spe,ees between them as notning else will. Sound and pearly white teeth, rosy lips, and fragrant breath ensured.</p>
        <p>Ask for SOZOUONT. 4s. 6d.</p>
        <p>ha.l been in his present empl.yment at Messrs. Rylands' works for five years. He remembered Saturday.the 26th September last. He left his work at 12 o'clock,and went to the Ring 0' Bells and had three glasses of b-er. He went home and went to bed and got up about six. His wife and himself had tea, and then went to the market. He went to the Barley Mow and had three glasses of ale and his wife three glasses of whisky. Mr. Jenkins in examining complainant was frequently interrupted by Messrs. Davies and Browne, who inter fered on behalf of their clierats,and he (Mr. Jenkins) said he was annoyed with the rude and vulgar [interruptions from the gentlemen opposite, in one case of deliberate falsehood,and he was amazed at it. Continuing, Cowlishaw stated that hie wife purchased a noggin of whiskey and he put it in his pocket to bring home.</p>
        <p>Mr. Davie : Mr. Jenkins said the wife brought it home, he is wronz.</p>
        <p>Mr. Jenkins : It is according to my instructions. Mr. Davies : Y ,ur instructions are wrong. Mr. Jenkins threw the papers across the court at Mr. Davies, saying angrily, " Read the instructions." Mr. Davies: That is an assault. (Laughter.) Mr. Birchall : This is only wasting the time of the court. Other magistrates characterised the interfer- ences and interruptions as disgraceful. Continuing, Cowlishaw said his wife was " fresh." Hampson was "a little fresh, not much." He was sober. Hampson and he sat down to supper. lie wife came and I:Qgan to love Hampson and he to love her. The Mayor : Won't it be better to let him tell his his own story P Mr. Jenkins: lam conducting this case, not Cowli- shaw. It is not the only serious case I have had the honour of conducting. The Mayor: You conduct it very funnily then. Mr. Jenkins: That is my business. You are not a lawyer. you are a miller. The Mayor: Please to go on with your case. The evidence being continued, _ .</p>
        <p>Cowlishaw said that when [lampoon was putting the cord round his neck his wife said "He is no good, he is no good." Hunpsln dragged him into the next room. He begged of his wife to call somebody. His wife did not do anything but laugh. He rolled over into the other place to get out of his way. He knocked at the wall with his stick. He knocked a second time. Mrs. Moston came to the door and " punched " it in, and his wife said "Go back, go back, there is nothing amiss." P.C. Condliffe attended to him and washed his wounds. He was conveyed to the Infirmary, on the ambulance, where he remained three weeks. He identified the shirt produced as the one he was wearing on the Saturday night. _ _</p>
        <p>In cross-examination by Mr. Browne, complainant got bothered and rising excitedly to his feet shouted "Oh let him off, let him off. I have done nothing wrong. lam telling the God Almighty truth. When calmer he said Hampson and he had always been good friends. He would not have compensation. He wanted him prosecuted. If he was offered a hundred " quid " down he would not have it. Sarah Ellen Moston said she resided at 8, Glazebrook- street. 'She rernennbered the 26th of September. Be- tween 11 and 12 she heard a sound of quarreling. She heard Cowlishaw say "It's time for yol to be going." Complainant said " Arn't I master in my own house." Witness then heard sounds of struggling and went into the street to look through Cowlishaw's window, Cowli- shaw was lying on his back. Hampson was kneeling over him feeling in his waistcoat pocket. She then saw him with a knife in his hand dagger-wise. Complainant said " What are you going to do." Hampson said " I am going to kill you." Cowlishaw said " You are not going to use the knife are you " and Hampson replied "1 am going to cut your throat." This he repeated time after time. He tried to jag the knife in Cowlistraw's throat. The later knocked his wrist away. Witness thumped at their door and afterwards Mrs. Cowlishaw nulled the blind down. Witness's husband then arrived and witness heard Cowlishaw shout " Murder and Lord help me." Witness and her husband went in and found Cowlishaw lying where she first saw him. Mrs. Cowli- shaw said "go out there is nothing amiss." Prosecutor said "See there is a cord round my neck,they have been trying to strangle me." He said his wife had given Hampson the cord. Witness's husband went for P.C. Cendliffe. Neither Flampson or Mrs. Cowlislaaw rendered the old man any assistance.</p>
        <p>By Mr. Browne : riamp4on seemed quite sliest. Mrs. Cowlishaw had had drink. She might have seen Cowlishaw come home drunk once or twice.</p>
        <p>' - Albert Moston, husband of the last witness, said just ' as he get in the house he heard knocking on the wall, and he:with his wife went to the door of the Cowlishaw's and walked in. He found complainant at the bottom of the stairs between the kitchen and the parlour. He was in a sitting posture. Witness got hold of :he cord which was round complainant's neck, and feeling his hand wet and seeing blood on his whiskers he loosed it again. He found blood on his right hand. He lit the candle, brought a damp cloth and saw a gash in his throat. While they were attending to Cowlishaw, defendant left the house. Complainant was sober. Defendant appeared to have had drink, and Mrs. Cowlishaw was the worst of the three.—By Mr. Browne : He thought it was only a drunken spree. They (the Cowlishaws) had been very quiet for six months. P.C. Condliffe said he was aroused about 12.15 on Sunday morning by Mosten. He went to Cowlislaaw'a house partly dressed. He found Cowlishaw as described and rendered him first aid. Hampson went away and witness followed him and caught him up. He said he would not go back to Cowlishaw's house. Witness and Detective Williamson took him back. In answer to the charves Cowlishaw made, neither prisener nor Mrs. 'owlishaw said anything. Witness produced Hampson's front, bloodstained. By Mr. Browne Hampson was not too drunk to charge that morning. He saw no wounds or bruises upon Hampson. _</p>
        <p>DAtective-Sergeant Monaghan deposed to also seeing Cowlishaw at his residence. Witness took possession of Hampson's knife. Dr. Battle,junior house surgeon at the Warrington In- firmary,said complainant was brought to the Institution on Sunday morning. He found a wound on the left side of the neck. It W 49 an incised wound a litta over an insh long and had just penetrated the skin. The knife produced might cause such a wound. The right leg was broken at the ankle. Cowlishaw was now going on favourably. Complainant had had drink. By Mr. Brnwne : It would not require much violence to break complainant's leg owing to his age. A man who had had drink would bleed more than one who had not.</p>
        <p>Mr. Jenkins : That is the Case for the prosecution. Mr. Browne, in addressing the Bench for the defence, said it was for them to consider whether a prima facie case had been made out to commit the defendant for trial. He undertook to say that the case had diminished from what it was four weeks ago. He suggested that it was a drunken speeeing row. What did Moston mean by saying "let them fight it out themselves." They were all drunk at the time, but absolutely good friends when the blow was struck, and that merely a drunken squabble. He ventured to say that the case ought to be dismissed ; there was not a prima facie case a jury would convict upon. The Deputy Magistrates' clerk to the bench: You can consider the case of the wife as well.</p>
        <p>The magistrates said they thought a prima facie case was made out and committed prboner te the Assizes. They did not think it any use going further with Mrs. Cowlishaw's case. Mr. Browne : The Assizes are not until the 17th or 18th November. I apply for bail. Cowlishaw said he had no objection. The Bench accepted the same bail as before, the Chief Constable remarking that it was satisfactory to the police.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
