        <p>THE CHARGE AGAINST CANON DODD</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>RE-HE k RING REFUSED.</head>
        <p>RE-HE k RING REFUSED.</p>
        <p>At a special meeting of the Chester Consistory Court held on Wednesday in Chester Cathedral, the Rev. Cha- ncellor Eipin, all., presiding, Mr. Digby Thurnana, bar- rister,London,appearing on behalf of Canon Dodd,rector of Stratton, near Warrington which living he had held for twenty-eight years,and had been canon of the c-ithe- drat for sixteen years,made an application in cor.nection with a case he ird on the 23rd ult., and in which Canon Dodd had been found guilty of acts of immorality at Stretton. Be said that looking at the position of the defendant, and the gravity of the charges,together with the effect upon the church,and not desiring to challenge the findings of the assessors in the case, he applied that the court allow the question of the defendant's state "of mind " ti be gone into. He was in a position to offer prima facie evidence. In reply to the Chancellor, Mr. Tnurnam said he was pr..-,ceeding under clause 92 of the Act.</p>
        <p>The Chancellor said it seemed to be rather a straining of the rule.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thurnam admitted that the rules were in some instances obscure, and not very clear. The Chancellor said they had not been worked out in practice on many occasions. Mr. E. S. Giles, on behalf of the bishop of the diocese, argued that he did not know bow the application could be proceeded with. Was it not an act of re-hearing or re-trial ?</p>
        <p>The Chancellor said it wag not a re-hearing. He did not inteni hearing evidence about the mental condition of the defendant.</p>
        <p>Mr.Thurnam said he based his application not entirely upon the Act and rules,but upon what he called the in- herent right or power of any court,and non the less an ecclesiastical court, of prey( nting certain proceedings re- sulting in a miscarriage of justice. Sentence had not been passed in the present case, and therefore there was no definite judgment. Mr. Giles iiaid he could not agree that there had been no final judgment. Judgment had been pronounced, but the sentence was postponed. The Chancellor said the penalty had not been specified, and explained the reason for that by saying that twenty• eight days were required to elapse as the time for ap- peal. Mr. Tnurnam suggested that the law regarding lunacy was as effective and binding upon an ecclesi- astical court as it was upon a criminal court. In the latter court it had heen shown that if the party did not know the nature or quality of the act he had done, and that if the offender was so rendered incapable of power of self-control as the result of disease affecting the mind, that was an excuse, or would be a most material fact in determining the measure of guilt and of subsequent punishment. Upon those grounds the nature of his ap- plication was sufficiently foreshadowed. He did not know how far he might read affidavits which had been made by relatives and friends of Canon Dodd as to the nature of the evidence that would be adduced at the next hearing. The Chancellor said he could not entertain the affida- vits, because it would be premature to look at the evi- dence, which in all probability belonged to a later stage of the case if it proceeded further. Mr. Thurnam said that in view of the sentence not having been passed, he asked that the Chancellor should at semi future time take evidence that he had fore- shadowed. He was instructed that no information was given on the legal question in the case for the defence as to whether such evidence could have been consi- dered.</p>
        <p>The Chancellor, after alluding to the fact that he and assessors sat in the case, and found the verdict of guilty, said it seemed to him that he could not entertain the application. Three charges were made against the defendant, to two of which he pleaded guilty, and an absolute denial was given to the third. Defendant and his wife went into the witness-box, and there was not the shadow of a suggestion of any want of his responsi- bility made througheut the whole of the lengthy pro- ceedings. It seemed that he was preclu4ed from enter- taining the question AA to whether defendant was or was not inc Table of controlling himself at the time or times which had been specified. If it could have ever been pleaded that defendant was incapable of control, it was too late to plead it that day. If in his decision he had made an error,he could be put right by a superior court, The application must be dismissed.</p>
        <p>Thurnam asked that the affidavits might bo con- sidered in mitigation of sentence. The Chancellor replied that the sentence rested with the bishop, to whom had been certified the verdict.</p>
        <p>Mr. Giles said that so far as the bishop was concerned, Ile thought it should be pointed out to his lordship,if the affidavits weie entertained, that they were made by four persons who were present at the trial on the 23r1</p>
        <p>The Chancellor : That is a matter for the bishop, and not for this court. You represent the bishop, and you must deal with the matter. I have no business to go into the affidavits at the present stage. I dismiss the application, with costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>ELLESNIERE-STREE I' WESLEYAN MISSION.</p>
        <p>The Sunday school anniversary of the above mission was celebrated on Sunday. In the afternoon a musical service was held at Bold-street Wesleyan Church, con- ducted by the Rev. W. G. Triggs. Miss Brough sang "The Lord is mindful" with good effect, and Mr. Levi Richardson rendered "Come unto Me" in a finished manner. A recitation " The Road to Heaven ' was well delivered by Miss Morrall, and the children did credit to their teachers in a rendition of Jackson's " Te Deum." The Bold-street Church Choir sang an anthem during the service. At night, Miss Bessie Dutton conducted the service at Ellesmere-street Mission. There was a large congregation. A capital address was given by Miss Dutton,whe was,as usual,well liked. Mr. Perkins sang "Lord God of Abrakam "very creditably. After Miss Dutton left Councillor Shaw Green conducted the service and closed it in the usual way. The collections during the day realised a satisfactory amount. WYCLIFFE P.S.A. SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The meeting on Sunday of this society,vs hose members now total 1,075, was well attended. The president (Rev. J. Yongo) occupied the chair, aud in alluding to the visit of Councillor Malice, J.P., the Grand Chief Tem- plar, he assured him of a hearty welcome. The temper- ance cause was one which Mr. Mali's had given his life to and it was a cause which was near many of their hearts. It was not one of those causes which in the "good old town "of Warrington went quite as well as they would like it to go.ln most towns there was always more or less enthusiasm in the temperance cause, but he was bound to say that his experience of temperance meetings in Warrington was net one of that kind. In Warrington there was a sort of atmosphere which was not favourable to this plant of temperance. They all knew that they lived under the shadow of great brewing estab- lishment ; and that was the cause of a great deal of dark- ness and the preference for darkness rather than light. He longed for the time when there should be a great awakening in the cause of temperance. The great enemy of this country was drink, and they had been told that, but they did not seem to believe it. They teeded to feel that the cause was the cause of the Master and the cause of hUM3lli!y. conclusion he urged young men. to make up their minds in regard to this question They could not. do a better or finer thing for themselves than by bocoomag total abztainers.—Councillor Malins then delivered a most interesting address. Referring to intoxicating drink he said man had adopted an artificial way of slaking his thirst, and in becoming abstainers front such drinks they were simply reverting to a more simple and natural method. Speaking of moderate drinkers, he said the drunkards were the failures in the moderate drinking school. He was an abstainer not be- cause lie wished to be called an odd man,but because he wished to dissociate himself from the drink evil which had a victina in every family in the land.—Mr. G. Lowe read the lesson, and Mr. Healey offered prayer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>WYCLIFFE CONGREGATIONAL CI3URCH.</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE CONGREGATIONAL CI3URCH.</p>
        <p>The monthly open service at the above place of wor- ship was held on Sunday evening when the building was well-filled. Tl:ie sermon was pre'ached by tia3 Rev. John Yon,,ze (pastor) who spoke from the subject of "Christ's second wOll to men and women." At the last open ser. vice " Christ's first word to men and women " was the topic of discourse, and on Sunday evening Mr. Yonge cave as the second word " Believe the Gospel." Mark i., 15 From these words the rev, gentleman preached most earnestly exhorting his hearers to adopt Christ's second word as a guide in life. He instanced the beneficial eff-ct, belief in the gospel had on the lives of all persons imbued with it. rhp gospid was the message of forgive- ness of sin and by believing in it they would be eternally saved. After the sermon the choir sang the anthem " The day is fastly sinking, etc.," acquiting themselves creditably. sr. JAMES CHURCFI, LATCFIFORD.</p>
        <p>The re-opening services in connection with the above church, were continued on Sunday. At 7.15 a.rn there was a first celebration of Hely Communion, a second celebration at eight o'clock, a children's service at 9.45 and matins (choral) at 10 30. At eleven o'clock there wis a third ce)ebration of. the Holy Communion (choral), when a good congregation attended,the music being the Missa Cantata in E fltt, which was magnifi- cently rendered. Mr. Frank Booth was at the organ. The clergy present were the linvs. F. Slater (vicar), J• W. B. Sproule (curate), the Rev. NV, J. Stanton, and the R-v. Morley Stevenson, principal of the Warring- ton Training College, who preached from the words " My bre'llren, he strong in the Lord " (Ephesians vi 10) There was on exceptionally large congregations in the evening, when the service was again of a very attrac- tive character. The preacher was Rev. R. G. Matthew, M. A. , rector of Wigan, who alluded to the re-opening of the church in suitable terms. A collection was made at each service in aid of the restoration fund.</p>
        <p>IRON MR d BRASS BRDSTgADS:—GARLAND, Iron- monger,Warrington.— Advt.</p>
        <p>X Off DEAR ME, I FORGOIT X.</p>
