        <p>A MONSTER PLACARD may be formed by jot nla- %timber of the" LEVIATHAN" SHEETS. MAMMOTH POSTERS. LARGE TYPE MAMMOTH POSTERS. FOR MAMMOTH POSTERS. THE MOST STRIKIII CI MAMMOTH POSTERS. POSTER</p>
        <p>"EXAMINER" OFFICE, ROGRAMM ES FOR CON VERSAZ I ONES PROGRAMMES Mit SOIREES. PROGRAMMES FOR CONCERTS.</p>
        <p>PROGRAMMES, BOOKS OF WORDS WINDOW BILLS. PLACARDS, kc.</p>
        <p>PAINSTAKING PRINTERS are now-a-days sought for by business raen who study real economy in ennouncements which they wish the public to notfoe ; as well as by Friendly Societies, Tr de Unions, Trading Associations, and Companies in the careful production of ReporlA Balance Sheets, Bcc.; and by Musical Unions, Pastere, and Choir Con- ductors for neatly and tastefully arranged</p>
        <p>JOHN WALKER &amp; CO., , ‘ "EXAMINER" OFEICE,</p>
        <p>MR. CHAMBERLAIN'S address at Bir- The Art of Oratory. mingham on Wednesday, on th art of public speaking should be an excellent advertisemsnt for the debating societies which are opening their sessions for the winter. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN is no depreciator of these socie- ties. On the other hand,he speaks in the highe et terms of their educational influence in free com- munities. He does not hesitate to ascribe the early development of his own remarkable gifts of oratory to his connection with the Birmingham and Edgbaston Debating Society some thirty odd years ago. Many people are of opiLion that a orator, like a poet, is born not made. But Mr. CHAMBERLAIN does not accept any doctrine of this kind, at least not without some important quali. fications. There are, as he pJints, two kinds of eloquence. He admits that there is a sort of elo- quence that is denied to ordinary men, "to all but those of the highest imaginative genius—an elo- quence which appeals to the emotions rather than to reason, which carries away the,speaker and the hearer at the same time, which transports the listener, but does not always convince him." But this, according to Mr. CHAMBERLAIN is not the best kind of eloquence, and certainly not the most useful. These fervid and resistless appeals to the emotions or the imagination may answer well for the mement,but the "eloquence of reason is to be preferred and surpasses it as a force in moulding human opinions.' Now it is just this "eloquence of reason" that, in Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S opinion, can be acquired by study and by application. He does not believe that oratory is in all instances a gift of nature. Mere long-windedaess does not make a good speaker, and there is a fatal fluency which should be avoided. Most people who are in the habit of attending public meetings are well aware that some speakers are exceeding diffuse and apparently utterly unable to "stick to a point." These defects, Mr. CHAMBERLAIN thinks, are nearly always due to carelessness, indifference, or ignorance. Mr. BRIGHT,OIIO of the greatest of English orators, set an example which might be followed with advantage by all who aspire to speak to their fellow citizens from the public plat- form. His great speeches were prepared with tha most anxious care. He would generally spend a week or more in the elaboration of his thoughts, the object being to grasp clearly himself" the central idea and main principle that he wished to impress upon his hearers, then to state it in the simplest terms he could find, so that he might be " understanded of the people," and, lastly, while avoiding every superfluous word, every unneces- sary argument, to reinforce the text by such illus- trations and arguments as suggested themselves to his mind." MT. CHAMBERLAIN'S address is, on the whole, decidedly encouraging to would-be public speakers. He speaks as a master of the art of oratory and he declares that the art can be acquired by any man of ordinary ability wh take the necessary trouble. He si</p>
        <p>that no good argument. can be idered without serious labour, that more tl e should be taken in pruning away redundancies in abandon- ing all that is not pertinent to the subject,than in preparing the language that is actually to be used, and that there is no more fatal mistake than to imagine that you can make up in length what you want in depth.</p>
        <p>THE American presidential election TheP Anaeriean will be decided next Tuesday. The resideacy. prediction that this would be the most exciting contest that has been held in the United States within the last thirty-six years are being amply verified. A few months ago there appeared to be only one opinion—in New York and the Eaatera States at all events—and that</p>
