      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>EXAMINER SATURPAY, OCTOBER 31 1896.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER SATURPAY, OCTOBER 31 1896.</p>
        <p>was that Mr. McKIN-LEY, the Republican nominee, would be returned by a stupendous majority. Now, however, the Republicans are not quite so cocksure as they were a few months ago. The Silver Democrats seem to have made a wonder- fully fortunate choice when they selected Mr. BRYAN, "the boy orator," as he is sometimes called, as their champion. Mr. BRYAN has fought with a pluck and determination which have won the admiration of his opponents even, and excited his own supporters to something like a frenzy of enthusiasm. Yet, still, he chances are, when everything is taken into account, probably on the side of the Republicans. That party, it is said, includes in its ranks nearly all the wealthy men of the States,and the power of wealth in America is enormous. The great monopolies and commercial corporations have thrown the whole of their influence on the side of MCKINLEY, in whose behalf money is being spent without any limitation. On the other hand, the remarkable enthusiasm which the working classes and agriculturists are displaying on behalf of the Dem( cratac candidate is bound to make it- self felt in a coantry whore every citizen possesses a vote. Illin ,is is described as the " pivotal" State—in other words it is the vote of that State which will turn the election. The Republican "boss "of the campaign, Mr. HANNA, claims the State as a certainty, but then the " boss" on the other side is just as confident that Illinois will be Mr. BRYAN'S trump card. Under these circum- stances mere outsiders must susp3ml judgment and patiently await the result.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>THE CONGREGATION&amp;L CHURCH, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The anniversary sermons in aid of the Bethesda Con- gregational Church were preached on Sunday, by the Rev. T. H. Spencer, M.A., of Liverpool. There were large congregations at both services. The collections were in aid of church funds.</p>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S BAND OF Ho, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday evening a service of son.' entitled "Five steps to Honour and five steps to Ruin," was given in the schoolroom, Victoria-road, Runcorn. Mr A. R. Norman psesided over a large company. A col- lection was taken in aid of the society's funds. FIRR ALARM AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Last night (Thursday) the Runcorn Fire Brigade were called out to a fire that had broken out in a chip-potato shop in Sheen-street. On arrival at the house, they found the fire had been put out, and their services were not required. The boiler became overheated and exploded. Little damage was done. NEW POOR RATES FOR RUNCORN. _ -</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday before Mr. Brundrit and Mr. H. Reynolds, the following poor rates were levied :—Aston-by-Sutton ild, in the £ to realise £239 135., Aston Gv, nge is. in the £ to realise £3975., Sutton lid, in the £ to realise £337 2s. lid.. Clifton is. in the £ to realise £207 14s. 7d.. Halton is. 3d. in the £ to realise £375 Os. 6d., Stockham IS. in the £ to realits £45 9s. 4d., Norton 10d. in the £ to realise £317 Bs. 10d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>STEAMRR STRANDED NEAR RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>STEAMRR STRANDED NEAR RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Friday afternoon, the steamer Cumberland, owned by Messrs. Mack atid Sons, Liverpool. on her journey to Mancester, was stranded in the canal. After passio2 Saltport at a fair speed she failed to answer the helm, and ran on Cyprus Island, where, when the tide went down, she was left high and dry. Everything possible was done to remove her, but without any effect. She remained there until the following aftet noon's tide,when she was successfully pulled up by tugs. AN 6' AT HOME" AT THE BETHESDA LECTURE HALL.</p>
        <p>The first of a series of social evenings—" at homes " in connection with the Bethesda Congregational Church. took place on Wednesday evening in the Bethesda Lec- ture Hall. The Rev. J. Simpson presided,and in a brief address said it was their intention to have these pleasant evenings much oftener than nad been nine!. An excel- lent tea was provided by the Mesdames Lydia, Sayd,and Hetherington. The company afterwards listened to songs,&amp;c., given by Messrs. Outhwaite,. Worral,and C. Temple; Mesdames Hetherington (2) and Temple. The song" My Little Bow Wow " given py Miss Lacey (five years) was well received and caused much laughter. Mr. Lacey was encored for an appropriate comic song. A paper read by Mr. Chadwick on " 'rattlers " was very amusing. The party broke up about 1.0 o'clock after having spent a thoroughly enjoyable evening. LECTURE ON CO-OPERLTION.</p>
        <p>A lecture was delivered on Monday evening in the Co- operative Hall, Church-st., Bunc..orn, by Mr. F. Hardern, 3.P.. of Oldham. on •• Co-operative Devel,pment." Mr. W. Wiliam, of Widnes, presided. The lecturer, in the course of his remarks, said that unfortunately the mem- bers of that society had only seen their way to contri- bute £lO to the Education Fund. The Old tam Indux- , trial Society, whose membership numbered 1,200. share !capital about £lOO,OOO, quarterly trade shout £lOO,OOO, ,devoted to education between £5OO and £6OO every three ' months, or more than £2,000 tt year. Although they acted in such a liberal manner in this department, they had for the past ten or twelve years, with one excep- tion, paid a dividend of 3s. in. the Z, and had also given handsome donations to charitable institutions. Views were shown by means of a lantern manipulated by Mr. Blinston of Widnes.</p>
        <p>TER NAUTICAL BAZAAR, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>It will be seen in another column that the Seamen's Institute intend to hold a bazaar in aid of the Institute A committee of ladies and gentlemen, with Mr. J. K Digges as secretary, are busily employed in making the necessary arrangements. The cause for which the bazaar is being promoted should ensure success. RUNCORN BOWLING CLUB.</p>
        <p>A very successful season in connection with this club was brought to a close or Wednesday evening,when the members partook of supper at the Bridge Hotel, excel- lently served by Mr. and Mrs. Riley- Mr. T. Dean pre- sidee. After the secretary had read his report a number of toasts were honoured. Song' were contributed by Messrs. Wright, Dutton, Price, Dean and Johnson. ACTION AGAINST A RUNCORN TRADIRMAN. At Watford Petty Sessions on Tassday, Mr. W. E. Lea, of Runcorn was summoned fir having on the lath September, travelled on the London and North W-stern Railway from Willesden to Watford, without a ticket, and with intent to avoid payment of tare. A fine of 10a. and costs, £2 15s. 6d. was imposed. TNR PROPOSID SECONDARY EDUCATION FOR RUNCORN. The Runcorn School Board and a sub-committee of the Technieal Instruction Committee have this week held a conference on the subject of Secondary Educa- tion, which it is proposed to introduce into the town The Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, as a member of the School Board and the Urban Council made a suggestion at both Board and Council meetings that the Technical Institute should be put to some useful purpose in the day time, instead of as at present being vacant. The suggestion thrown out by the doctor was acted upon and committees formed to enquire into the question. But the result of the con- ference has been that, the only way in which the School Board could act would be in the formation of a higher grade school, and as this would come under the Educa- tion Department the Technical Institute would not meet the requirements as to a playground, &amp;c., the Technical Instruction Committee felt that they were unable to take any steps for promoting secondary education. STALL TOLLS IN TER RUNCORN MAMMY.</p>
        <p>On Thursday a deputation it stall holdere waited upon the Market Committee of the Runeorn Urban District Council, with the view of obtaining a reduction in the charges for the stalls at the market used by them. The subject was carefully considered by the committee, and they came to,the conclusion that the request of the de- putation could not be granted. SAM HAGUE'S MINSTRELS AT RUNCORN. We call our readers attention to our advertising columns, where it will be seen that Hagne's well-knewn troupe of minstrels, is giving performancea in the Public Hall, Runcorn. The opening performance lut night, (Thursday), was creditably performed by those who took part in it. The ballads and songs were excellently rendered, and brought forth loud roars of laughter from the audience. The programme which is a rather lengthly one is to be entirely new, to night (Friday), and to morrow. There will also be a Saturday afternoon per- formance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>PRESENTATIONS AT RIINCORN.</head>
        <p>PRESENTATIONS AT RIINCORN.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening a gathering in connection with the choir of the Runcorn Parish Church, was held in the National Schools for the purpose of making presenta- tions to their honorary choirmader, Mr. James Littler, who is leaving the town. After tea bad been partaken of Mr. E. Kay handed Mr. Littler a beautifully illumin- ated address in a frame of carved English oak, which had been supplied by the Misses Mack. Master J. Howard, on behalf of the choir bovs, presents Mr. Littler with a beautifully bound edition of " Hymns Ancient and Modern." Speeches were delivered. Mr. Littler suitably respondtd. During the evening a number of songs and glees, Sze., were rendered. I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>HALTON COURT LEET.</head>
        <p>HALTON COURT LEET.</p>
        <p>A sitting of this court was held on Saturday afternoon at the Castle Hotel, Halton. The court is a very ancient one, dating back to time immemorial, and records are still in existence from 1088. which for safety were re- cently removed from the court room to the Record Office, London. The Manor of Halton was leased from the Crown to the Right Hon. George Earl of Cholmondeley in 1728, which expired in 1880. On the 27th of Decem- ber in that year, Mr. Robert Davies, solicitor, Warring- ton, was appointed steward to the Court by her Majesty the Duke of Lancaster, and since then has occupied the position. At one time the Court was entrusted with consideralbe pcwers,it having to do with tbe suppression of vice and crime, but now these have been superseded by the Assizes, Quarter Sessions, and magisterial sit- tings, so that to-day Ow duties are practically nominal. The jurisdiction of the Court extends over a very large portion of Cheshire, including 35 townships, some of them abutting n Altrinchatn. Knutsford and Chester. In the unavoidable absence of Mr. Davies, the deputy steword, Mr. Kirkcennel, of the same firm of solicitors, presided. Mr. Walker was elected bailiff. The jury were then sworn in,consisting of Messrs. J. Bate,Aston; James Lowe,Bartington ; Job Clayton,Bexton; Thomas Wright, H. Rowe, Joseph Manning, J. S. Jeacock, William Nickson, Joseph S'okes, Rev. G. D. Wray, Halton; Thomas John Weir, Keckwick ; William Thomas Brus- drett, Millington ; John Whitlow, Prestoa-o'-th'-Hill ; Edward Norbury, Toft ; William Wei . walton Superior; Henry Dodd, Walton lof,rinr ; Henry N rcott, Whitley Superior, an i T. H.Priestly, Newton-by-Daresbury. The fury then appointed Mr. Wright as forem.n. This hiving been done, the bailiff went to the outer door of • e hotel and read the proclamation ,calling upon anyone Who desired to come and make presentments to the urt. To this there was no answer. The next business was t..e election of burleymen, and on the proposition of Mr. Rowe, seconded by Mr. Manning, Messrs. Joseph Astbury and William Nicksn were reappointed to the office, which it was remarked was formerly a very oner- ous one, for they had power to do almost anything. The election of constables resulted in the selection of Messrs. Newport and Thomas Potter, on the motion of Mr. Mannin,,,seconded by Mr. Stokes. The Deputy-Steward stated that the duties of the position were practially 01-solete, and Mr. Wright intimated that his father held the office for many years, and had the paraphernalia, in- cluding the handcuffs, in his possession at the time. There being no presentments, the jury proceeded to s•gn the declaration. The company subsequently sat down to an excel- lently served dinner by Mr, aud Mrs. Walker. After this a number of toasts were honoured.</p>
        <p>The proceedings were interspersed with longs by Mr. W ,ton ; Miss Walker playing th. acc.mpanimonts.</p>
        <p>To THE DEAF —A Gentlemau who cured himself of Deaf nest and Nokes in the Head after fourteen years' suffering will gladly send full particulars of the remedy post free.— Address P Curzon, Aniberley House, Strand, London. W.O</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>FRODSIIAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSIIAM.</p>
        <p>" BAPTISMAL REGENERATION."—Under the auspices of the Church Association and National League, a lecture was delivered on Wednesday evening by the Rev. T. Sparshott (deputation secretary), in the Frodsham 'Town Hall. There was a large company present. WESLEYAN FOREIGN MISSIONS—On Sunday, at the Wesleyan Cnapel, Five Crosses, the services held were in aid of the Wesleyan Foreign Missions. At the morning service the Rev. J. R. I3argreaves officiated, whilst Mr. E. J. Guest, of Warrington, officiated in the evening. A meeting was held on Wednesday evening in the chapel in support of the foreign missions. Mr. F. Boston, of Frodsham, presided. The Rev. J. R. Hargreaves gave a very descriptive address on the missions in Jamaica. During the lecture, views of Jamaica were exhibited by ' an oxy-hydrogen lantern. Collections were taken for i the missions.</p>
        <p>FRODSRAM PETTY SESSIONS. WEDNESDAY. —Betore Mr. C. Reynolds, and Mr. T. Rigby. LICENSING.—John Vernon made an application to sell at the Belle Mount Inn,Oyerton.until the next transfer day, and the applicat on was graated, __ .</p>
        <p>_ tiltlV POOR ELATES—The following new poor rates were signed by the presiding magistrates :—Frodsham. Is est:- grated to realise £679; Frodsham Lordship, Is. estimated to realise £474; fie sby. Is. estimated to realise £406; Newton, is. estimated to reaLse £7l ; A.lvanley, Is. estimated to realise £127. DRUKK.—Samuel and John Browne. brothers, were sum- moned for being drunk and disorderly on the Northwich- road, Frodshana, on the Bth lat. aohn Browne was fined ss. including costs, and a warrant was issued for Samuel's appre hension.</p>
        <p>BURGLARY AT THE JAM Fact.°BY.—John Booth. Newton, Frodsham. was chirged with breaking and entering the Jam Factory of Messrs. Kydd and Co., and stealing therefrom" 18s. 10fd. their prop rty on the 17th October. He was sen- tenced to three months imprisonment with hard hbour. BEGGlNG.—Christopher M'earty and John Scott, were each sentenced to seven days' hard labour for begging on the 26th inst.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>PADUA"T.</head>
        <p>PADUA"T.</p>
        <p>SERVICE OF SONG.—A service of song entitled "Father, coin.- home" was rendered in the Wesleyan schoolroom on Thursday evening by the members of the Band of Hope. Mr. Frank Harris took the chair, and the read- ing w.is done in a very able manner by Miss J. Bennett, Lyndhurst. The following took part in the singing : Messrs. A. Ward, F. Stott and E. Pearce; Misses Gertrude Else, A. Pearce, L. Jowett, M. Pearce, E. Else, E. Hackney, S. Rowbottom, M. Ford, J.Ward, G.Carter, and L. Ward. Mr. Pearce conducted. There was a large attendance, a collection being taken in aid of the Band of Hope f ads. At the conclusion Mr. T. Mantle proposed a vote o f thanks to Mr. F. Harris and Miss J. Bennett which received the heartiest support of all present.</p>
        <p>CONCERT.—A very enjoyable concert was given in connection with the Young Men's Institute in the Church house, Padgate, on Thursday evening. There was a good attendance.and Mr.Beevers presided. Songs were given by Messrs. W.Finch and J.Unwin.pianoforte and violin duets by Messrs.W. Finch and J. Robt.rts,and humorous songs by Mr. Barlow. The latter created much amusement and received mueh applause. Mr. Blackwell accompanied on the piano. A vote of thanks to the chairman and to all who took part in the entertainment, nroposed by Mr. T. Steele, brought the meeting to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>MR. R. H. PEA.RSON'S CONCERT.</head>
        <p>MR. R. H. PEA.RSON'S CONCERT.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. H. Pearson is entitled to warm congratu- lations on the success of the concert given by him in the Parr Hall, on Tuesday evening. Nor should high commendation be withheld for his enterprise in engaging in the somewhat thankless task of pro- viding high-class musical entertainments in the town, and it is pleasing to record that the public response to efforts Vi this direction culminating in Tuesday's concert was entirely satisfactory, seeing that the Hall was comfortably filled in almost every part. The artistes engaged were Miss Maggie Davies Madame Belle Cole, Mr. Lloyd Chandos, and Mr. David Hughes, as vocalists,and Mr. John Dunn and Miss Marguerite Swales as sole violinst and sole pianist respectively, a concert party who, individ- ually and collectively, proved themselves in all respects capable and efficient. Miss Maggie Davies's peculiar and undoubted gifts are hardly those best suited to a successful interpretation of , Sullivan's rather trying song " My dearest heart," I and the better-chosen excerpt from the opera S' Puritani," in which she later appeared, made this clear. Miss Davies excels in Bellini's brilliant roulades, and the audience's approval of her finished vocalisation was here most emphatically expresssd. In the daintily humorous songs with which, in response to encores, she further treated her enthus- iastic listeners, Miss Davies is also at her best, and these were the items which gave refreshing variety and interest to a programme of songs otherwise net I altogether either well selected or of the highest standard. Mde. Belle Cole's reputation as a con- tralto vocalist is 80 well-established that it is need- less to say more than that in "Ruth's Song" (Gounod) she fully sustained the good opinions she has deservedly won. It is to be regretted, however, that further opportunities of enjoying this gifted artiste's singing were thrown away upon songs of such slender interest and impoverished sentiment 1 as "The Ladder of Gold" and "Daddie." The tenor, Mr. Lloyd Chandos, sang two songs of sterling merit, and therein displayed a rich mezzo voice of fine range and volume. His interpretation of "Ms Queen" and "Come into the garden Maud," showed a refinement of taste and style which mark the true artiste. A pity it is that Mr. Chandos, too, sacri- ficed an additional chance of further ingratiating himself with his audience in the common-place song " Darling ! how I love thee." Mr. David Hughes's contributions were of a high order. His robust bas' voice eminently fits him to cope with the exigen- cies of Hatton's "Revenge," and Elliett's " Hybrias, the Cretan,"—kindred songs, the superiority of which is felt in the healthy vigour of musical treatment which is characteristic of both. Mr. Hughes was deservedly applauded by a discrimi- nating and appreciating audience. On Mr. John Dunn's violin solos nothing but the highest encomiums can be bestowed. Mr. Dunn is probably the most eminent of English violinists and the mere statement of the fact therefore makes a de- tailed notice of his performances something like a work of supererogation. It will suffice to say that his interpretation af the Adagio from Spohr's 11th concerto, the " Neopolitan Airs " (Bullak), and the "Airs Russes " (Wieniawski) proved most un- equivocally Mr. Dunn's thorough mastery of the technique of the most difficult ot all solo instru- ments. Mr. Dunn's transcendent powers of produc- ing harmonic tones, of double-stopping, and of delicate bowing, were strikingly apparent in the last mentioned solo and in the encore piece "The Witches Dance" (Bazzini). Miss Marguerite S wale's piano solo" Etude" (Rubinstein).though mentioned last, was by no means the least important item in the programme. Her performance of this enacting movement was charactei ised by such so 1 e slisng-s from extreme energy to delicate pianissimo no to place her in the front rank of piano execir ants. Miss Swale also played the piano accompaniments throughout the evening with consummate taste. A most enjoyable and successful concert was brought to a close with the quartet " Mezza notte," from Flotow's " Martha," in which the several vocalists were associated.</p>
        <p>DARING TILL ROBBERY AT RUNCORN. At the Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday before Mr. J. Brundrit in the chair, and Mr. H. Reynolds, a loafer named Samuel Sinclair was charged with having felon- iously stolen on the 19th inst a purse containing £3 10s. belonging to George Moore, 9, Regent-street. Runcorn. Margaret Moore, wife of prosecutor said about quar- ter to four on Monday afternoon the 19th mat, the prisoner came into her shop and bought a metal Albert and guard for a is. He tendered for payment a half crown. Witness had reason to go to the drawer in order to procure the necess iry change. She left the sum named in:the charge,£3 10s.in a purse in the drawer.As witness was leaving the shop to go into the kitchen prisoner also left the shop and went outside. From what Mary E'en Santley told her she looked around and found the drawer open and the purse gone. Information was given to the police, and orisooer was arrested at Sale,—Mary Ellen Santley, 39, Granville-street, said that on Mon- day last, she was in Regent-street. She was passing the shop of the prosecutor's when she saw the prisoner come from underneatk the counter.—P.C. Gregory said he went to Sale in com- pany with. Mrs. Moore. He found Sinclair there and he charged him with stealing the purse and money. He replied " I am not guilty ; there is some mistaks."—The prisoner was remanded until Saturday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>4ublitations.</head>
        <p>4ublitations.</p>
        <p>T" QUIV3R.</p>
        <p>T" QUIVER. For Sunday and Gmeral Rea•iing, is permanently enlarged with the NOVEMBER PART, price 6d.</p>
        <p>ABEAUTIFUL PRESENrATION PICTURK, consisting of an exquisite repioductbm of ART SCHEOFER'S much-admired Painting of " Christ and St. John," will be issued with the NOVEMBER PART of THE QUIVER. NEW PRIVILEGE FOR READERS of THE QUlVER.—Subscribers will have the opportunity of pu; chasing for a nominal price Dean FARt,AII S world- tame(' LIFE OF ST. PAUL, uniform with "THE QUIVER" EDITION of FARRAR'S LIFE OF CHRIST ecently issued.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS, 1896. TOWN HALL WARD-TO THE BURGESSES OF THE WARD.</head>
        <p>MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS, 1896. TOWN HALL WARD-TO THE BURGESSES OF THE WARD.</p>
        <p>LADIES AND GENTLEMEN.</p>
        <p>At the desire of your Ward Committee, and with the un- ammons approval and support of the Conservatives of the Warcl,l have much pleasure in again offering you my services as one of your representatives in the Town Council. For the past three years I have done my best to promote your interests, ind, if again elected. BULL at all times be at hand and ready to hear the grievances of any of the burgesses, and so far as it lies in my power shall endeavour to have such grievances remedied. I may poifit out that during my term of office, the rates have been reduced 10d. in th s 2, and this has been effected without sacrificing efficiency in any Department. With the c,)-operation of my colleagues on the SANITARY WORKS .I.NO HEALTH COMMITThES, I have been inst:umental In bringing about considerable improvements in the sani- tary arrangements of the bonagh, and to these may, in pr t be attributed the prevailing low death rate. As vice-chairman of the ICgOOLATTENDANCC COMMIT :EK I have taken a special intereiit in educational matters, it‘ indicating my uncea,ing efforts in behalf ot the rising gz..e.a taon. I may rneation that I have attended every meeting of. the Committee.</p>
        <p>My aim has been to study the interests ef the ratepayers generally, and it you honour me with re-election I can assure you that you may rely upon me continuing to perform my iutios, haying only ONE OBJECT— ISLE GENERAL GOOD OF THE TOWN.</p>
        <p>I appeal to the burgesses generally for their votes and active support on the polling day. I am, Ladies and Gentlemen, Yours Faithfully, JOSEPti SHILLCOCK. Winwick -street, Warrington, October 14th, 1896.</p>
