      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>WOMAN AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>WOMAN AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>SERIOUS CIIA.RGE AGAINST A MARRIED</p>
        <p>At the Rufacorn Petty Sessions on Monday before Mr. H. Brundrit (in the chair) and Mr.H. Reynolds,Elizabeth Prescott, a married woman, was summoned for assisting in the management of a disorderly house at 15, Speak- man-street, Runcorn, on the 16th inst. and divers other dates. Ann Jane Prescott (daughter) ,Alice Potter,Annie Gresty, William T.ckle, David Speakman, Frederick Dutton, and Albert Taylor, were summoned for aiding and abetting Elizabeth Prescott. All the defendants with the exception of Speakman and Dutton who did not appear were represented by Mr. J. W. Knowles of Widnes. The evidence for the prosecution which was elven by Sergeant Brewster, Wylie, Nicholls, and Gregory was to the effect that, on the date named, they watched the first named defendant's house for a considerable time. Disgusting language ' and disorderly conduct were noticed during the whole of the night. When the police officers broke iate the house they found everything quiet, but all the defend- ants in the room upstairs. Mrs. Prescott in answer to questions, said the reason the men were in the house I was because they had no money to pay their lodgings and had nowhere to go, and she permitted them to remain in her house. The reason they watched the house was in consequence of receiving complaints from the neighbourhood.—P.C. Gregory spoke to watching the movements of the girl Ann Jane Prescott. Mr. Pres- cott was a respectable man and knew nothing of what was going on. Mr. Kuowles,in defence, contended that there was no case. He dwelt at length on the action of the police in lorcing entrance to the house without a warrant. The Chairman said they had come to the conclusion that the case was proved,and fined Mrs. Prescot %Land costs,or a month's imprisonment ; the other defendants a ith the exception of Taylor, 20s. and costs or 14 days; Taylor received the benefit of the doubt and was dis- charged. Warrants were issued for Speakman's and Dutton's apprehension. On Tuesday morteng,at the Runcorn Police Court, be- fore Mr. F. J. Norman,and Mr. J. W. Lightburn, Fredk. Dutton was charged with aiding and abetting Elizabeth Prescott in keeping a disorderly house on the 16th inst. and other dates. Evidence similar to that given the previous day was repeated. In explanation as to why he did not 6ppear on Monday, Dutton said he was at Liverpo )l,and he could not get to Runcorn in time. He was sent to prison tor 21 days' without the option of a fine.</p>
        <p>SHOP THEFTS BY LIVERPOOL PIRLS AT WARRINGTON. "PRACTICALLY SOCIAL RUIN. STRANGE EXPLANATION.</p>
        <p>On Thursday morning at the Warrington Borough Police Court before Alderman Sutton and Colonel Ridg- way, Kate Forrest (aged 22), and Edith Forrest (aged 16), of 16 Milton-road, Liverpool, two well-dressed and respectable-lookine girls, were charged with stealing from the shop of Samuel Fernley, jeweller, Bridge-st., at 2.20 on Wednesday afternoon, a gold bracelet, value 303. his property; they were further charged with stealing from the shop of Messrs Eustance, Sankey-st., at 2.30 the same day, a blue enamelled lady's watch-brooch, value 30s. ; another charge was of stealing from the shop of Mr. E. T. Evans, draper, Sankey-et., the gain() after- noon, a girl's woollen mantle, value 83. 6d.— The girls admitted all the offences. Both appeared to feel their position acutely. The Chief Constable said these two girls appeared up- on three aharges of committing thefts from shops in Warrington, on Wednesday. It was an exceedingly painful case ow.ng to the youth of the defendants and the Position they had held up to the present. Nothing ha i been known agsiest them before, and their friends and relatives were very respectable people in Liverpeel. For crime reason or other they left Liverpool on Wed- nesas v and arrived at Warrington between 1 and 2 p.nao At 2 30 they went to Mr. Fernley's shop,76,Bridge-street, and ask,d to be shown some bracelets. They were shown several which Mr. Fernlny got out of the window for them. They then asked to see some curb bracelets and Mr. Fernley aeain had recourse to the window and had to turn his hack to them. After examining these they left the shop telling Mr. Fernley they would con- sult their mother and let him know. Soon after they had gone and before any other customer had entered the shop, Mr. Fernley missed one of hi brace- lets, value 275. Be gave information to P.C. Clarke. who wont in search of the two girls. The officer saw them enter two drapersl shops. He went up to them and questioned them but as their answers were unsatisfactory he took them to Mr.Fernley who identified them. They were then taken to the police station and enquiries made. It was found that a few minutes after the theft of the bracelet it was pledged at the Tower pawnshop with Mr. Dalton, for 15s. along with a watch which formed the subject of the second eharee. They gave the name of Williems, Senkey-st. The seenrel case was almost identical with the first. After 2 o'clock the defendants went to Messrs. Eustance's shop and examined bracelets there. Mr. Devonport, the manwer,had to torn his back upon them once or twice. They did not purchase anything,and after they had gone a man Came with a Case which had been upon the ennntsr.and had contained the watch and hro,cla. Mr. Deynnpert then di7covered that, the case was missing and sent a note down to him (the Chief Constable) abort it, which arrived jolt As he was interviewing the two 'flies. arlmlfteci the thefts to him. In the third ease, the defendants went into the shcp of Mr. Evanr, dr.n,r 17 Sankey-street. at 2 n m. and asked for some eelf caps Micv WhitP,thA essistent,was base and left them to look round the showrooku. They went out making no rmrcimae. When PpprAended a eirl's mantle was found in their possession. This was identified by Miss White a. having been stolen that afternoon. He (the Chief Constable) did not think they bad any ex- pination as to why they committed these thefts. It was practically s,eial ruin to the girls. The money the defendants tamed by pledring the bracelet and watch way on the eider girl. Beyond that their capi- t only amounted to Is. 10(1. They had return tickets to feve..nenl. Rate Forrest, the elder of the girls, said : I have never done auoh s t',ing before. I Wag induced to do this to, pet money for rn•essing things at home. I was not in d'ffieuleie. The Chief Constable then said the explanation the girls had given to him was that their mother had su3tained serious finaneiil losses recently, and that their elder sio.er w,e at home in an almost dying state. She had heen oidsred change of air and the girls adopted the ides of committing these thefts to raise money to send their eiek sistr awsr. The mother was here hut not in a fit state to enrne into Court. The Chief Conatable added that he had sent an nffieer to Liverpool to the girls' home and he load found them highly respectable people. Their father once had st ennd Tiosition in Dale-street. There had boon no previous charge against the girls. The mother was a widow. It WAR (Mite true :about their sister being ill. No one would softer financially by the thects ss the money would he returned to the pawnbroker and the e Oa to their owners. Tue rhei”man It is a painful and thing to see two suet, girls here on such a serious charge. The Chief Cenatebie said they fell' the serinusnpsi of th'ir position. Thring the night they had been in of a respectable matron and had not slept At all, _</p>
        <p>Th. Chiirman enquired if the defendants had been rParling ,inht• end tra‘hy literature. _ .</p>
        <p>Kat,7FarrAqt said they were n-o-C/iccustomed ta reading lipht litPra,nre.</p>
        <p>Th. m.gistr-ites dealt with defendants tinder the first offenders' Act and recorded a conviction against them.</p>
