        <head>PARR HALL.</head>
        <p>THE PARR HALL.</p>
        <p>IMPROVED SEATING ACCOMODATION,</p>
        <p>Eve?: -sine-e—the erection of the cnmmodi-uq Ball in Palmyra Sqnare, Warrineton, its chief detraction has been the lack of comfort in the seats. In fact there was no d;fferenee between the acenmodation provided for those who paid the top prices and those who were present on the lower charges. The Corporation has wisely determined that such a serious fault shall be absent dur- inr the forthcoming wagon and afterwards. Since Mon. day, lase week. great improvements have been effected. Downstairs. 192 tip-up chairs (Dean's patent) have been prcyiripd, makiafe eight rows of 24 each. end upstairs in the ce,tre gallery. 84 similar chairs I are Trrmanently fixed. Tb,se. on the ground floor are movable and can be quickly removed whtn neceesarc. The chief advantage in those chairs is that the Reel; and back awing autnmatically when unoccu- pied, into a perpendicular pncition, leavirm a wide and clear passage between each row. They are ell uphols- owed in red plush, and exceedingly comfortable. Six pairs of eurtaina, rich in colour, have been hone over the doers. and :01 possibility of discnmfottirg draughts is precluded Dnwn the passages thick, red, cocoa-nut matting deadens the sound of fnntateps, and harmonises with the chairs, etc. The bslenny in front of the centre gallery has alsn been plush covered. Overhead tine wires have been stretehed to break the sound wavas,and Nolen the echo which detracted fr m the effect of musical per- formanre. in the building. The Hall new looks warm and'er mf(,rtitt le. nd in apnearanee ple,sing to the eve. MPS.III. F. Ponclbrvalry and Co. have supplied the chairs, eurtAins. ere., and their work has been performed ex- peditiously and well.</p>
        <p>THE RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL OF RUNCORN, SUTTON WATER. WORKS.</p>
