        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED THEFT OF £5,</p>
        <p>Last Fridday, before Alderman Wright, Councillor Roberts. and Dr. Gornall, at the Warrington Borough Police Court, Samuel Pomfrey, Wright-street, Sankey Bri iges, was charged on a warrant that being a bailee of £5, the money of John Cross, he feloniously stole it on October 21st,—John Cross, stated that on Monday morning he gave the defendant, who was in his employ, a bag containing £5 in gold to take to his wife, and he did not deliver it. He did not tell defendant what was in the bag. The latter went in a cart and had only to drive from the Market-place, where he was given the money, to Liverpool-road opposite the Wesleyan Chapel. When he came back he deniEd to com- plainant that he ever received the money.— Defendant reiterated this statement in court.—Edward Molyneaux deposed to seeing Mr. Cross hand the bag containing something which he did not know. He was close by the cart at the time. Complainant told defend- ant to give it to the " missis."—Sergeant Monaghan stated that he apprehended defendant in Sankey Bridges and he said he never saw the money, in answer to the charge.—The case was remanded until Tuesday for the police to make enquiries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>XINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>XINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before Alderman Writzht (chairman), Dr. Gornall. and Connelllor Roberts. Ac INcoRBIGIBLE TRUkNT.—Patrick Cavey, 347, Winwick- road, was summoned again for neglecting to send his boy, Dennis. to school.—Mrs. Cavey appeared —Mr. Jones, school attendance Inspector prosecuted, and said the boy had not been In school more than twice dun i,g the last six months. Defendant had regularly appeared before the bench and he asked that the boy be sent to Father Nugent's Boys' Refuge. Liverpool.—Mrs. Carey asked for another chance, but as it was her 29th appearance, and 12th for the boy Dennis. the magistrates sent the latter to the Refuge until he is 16 years of age. WIFE DESERTION.—WaIter Clarke 2 Ashton-et.. was sum- moned for deserting his wife Mary Clarke.—Defendant did not appear, and complainant was represented by Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins.—Mr. Jenkins said that defendant had deserted his client several times and Mused her. Practically she had been &amp;Betted from September 1894. In October 1895 defend- ant was prosecuted by the Board of Guardians for neglect and sent to gaol for a month. The summons WAS taken out for desertion since September 26. when defendant relused his wife anything for her maintenance. -Mrs. Clarke corrobo- rated, -The bench granted a separation order giving com- plainant the custody of her three children, and allowing her Bs. a week maintenance. A Lon4mG-HouBx LICENSE CANCELLED.—BIiza Marshall, lodging-house keeper, 3 Turner-street, was charged with having her lodging-house dirty.—lnspector Norman stated that he visited the lodging-house on Tuesday and found the rooms, staircases, bedding, and landings very dirty. He had cautioned the woman repeatedly and she was not fit to keep a lodging-house. The police records showed that she had been prosecuted before for the same offence.—The magis- trates inflicted a penalty of 10s. arid costs and cancelled the license.</p>
        <p>MoNDLY.—Before the Mayor (Alderman Fainlough, in the chair), Alderman Harrison. Alderman Wright, Councillor Pierpoint, Messrs. F. Monks, J. W. Hayward, S. Wright, and T. Birehall. AN OFFENDER FROM ORFORD EARRACICS.—WiIIiam McGane, a militiaman from Orford Barracks, was charged with hav- ing stolen certain articles of clothing from John Handley on the 21st ult. Defendant was arrested by the Present police, and handed over to Detective Meacock.—A remand was asked for and granted till Friday. BEGGING.—James Dunn, last from Manchester, was charged with begging in Irlarn-et. on Saturday night.—Ser- geant Chandler proved the case, and defendant was sent to prison for 14 days. A DESERTER.— John Mannien. 2, Webster's-yard, was charged with having desPrted from the South Lancashire Regiment, stationed at Irermoy, since the 19th August.— Detective Meacook proved the arrest of the prisoner., who was remanded to await an escort.—The usual award was made to the officer. DRONE AND DISORDERLY.—John Finnerty. 2, 17,-rnon's- yard. Was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in the Market-Place on Saturday night.—P.C. Rylett proved the case, and as it was the def,•ndant's first offence the case was dismissed. THEFT PRISM A SnoP.—John Oxley, Rose and Crown-yard. was charged with stealing a pair of child's boots from the shop of Messrs. Heywood. Bridge-st., on Saturday night.— The Chief Constable, in detailing the facts of the case, said the boots were missed from the shop at 9.50 on the evening in question. It transpired that the defendant went to a pawnshop and tried to sell the boots. The manager. seeing they were new and had beeffirecently rubbed in mud, asked him for a satisfactory account, of their possession. which he could not give. He sent for P.C. Fisher, and the de'end int tried to implicate a respectable woman, against whom there was no suspicion.—Defendant was sent to prison far 14 days' hard labour. A CONSTABLE AstiauLTED.—Charles Henderson, Pose and Crown-street, was charged with assaulting P.C. Plumb on Sunday morning and with being drunk and disorder:y.— P.O Plumb said he arrested defenHant at 1 a.m. on Sunday and as he was drunk and disorderly brought him to the police station. In the charge office he kicked witness on the legs.—P.C. Marsh corroborated and defendant WAS fined 10s. and costs.</p>
        <p>TUE3DlY.—Before Mr. Illbert Garnett (in the chair), and</p>
        <p>Mr. E. J. Guest. THEFT FROM A. BOOT-SHOP.—Annie Whittaker, a middle- aged woman, was charged with stealing from the shop of Arthur Booth, 29. Horsemarket-street.at 6 o'clock on Monday evening, a pair of men's boots. value ss. 11d., his property. The Chief Constable said that at the time in question Mr. Booth was in the back part of his shop. About 10 minutes to 6 Mr. James Craik observed the prisoner without shoes and stockings, enter Mr. Booth's shop and soon after return with the pair of boots produced, under her arm. She put them under her shawl and went away. Mr. Oraik informed P.C. Evans. who followed the woman and brought her back. Mr. Booth identified the boots. When charged the prisoner said " I don't remember anything at all about it, I was drunk." which woe, the Chief Constable added.the stereotyped excuse. —She was remanded until Friday for enquiries to be made into her antecedents. THE A.LLEGED THEFT OF £5.-9amuel POinfrOF was brought up on the charge of stealing £5, the money of John Cross.— As the wife of the latter, an indispensible witness was not present, the case was remande i until Thursday to allow her to attend.</p>
        <p>TIIIIRSDIY.—Before Alderman Sutton (chairman), and Colonel Ridgway Tir: ALLEGED TI U' or £s.—Satnuel Pomfrey, Wright- street. Sankey Bridges, again appeared to answer the charge of stealing £5. the money of John Cross, butcher, Warring- ton.—Defendant still denied having received the money.— ' r A . Browne prosecuted, and stated the facts of the case as alremly reported.—Mr. Cross corroborated as to giving defend- ant the gold in a bag.—Mrs. Cross stated that defendaNt cltry, to her from Mr. Cross. He saidinanswer to a question bat he had bought " nowt Mrs.Cro,s thought that her hue- tand must have forgotten to send the money, but shortly rtf`r.r a note arrived from him asking for a collar and whether -oney had been in time. She nen thought. sornethit,g wrong and sent a note back by defend • :v... —nuttier evidence was given to show that ,:eft,ndatit had received the money.—Mr Browne</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>CARD</head>
        <p>A CARD</p>
        <p>L('NOWN as ev. Joseph Holmes' Mexican Prescription LI- is the only original guaranteed Remedy for all those wh,r suffer from nervous weakness, exhausted vitality- ii'dney or bladder diseases, gravel. prostate. spinal weak- brain fag and kindred complaints. It has stood the test for twenty years. Send a self-addressed stamped nvelope to Joe. HOLMES' Remedy Co. Bloomsbury Man dons, Bicarnsbury Square4London, and get full particulars I,f his great specific'. free of charge. Mention whether for znaie or female. Bewareicf fraudulent Imitators.</p>
        <p>said that some weeks since, defendant left his home in his shitt sleeves and was away several weeks. They thought he must be wrong in his head.—ln answer to Nlr Sistton, Mrs. Cross said defendant came to her and said he had brought nothing.—Detective Sergeant Adonaghan said no trace con d b- found of the money.—Defendant was com- mitted to the County Quarter Sessions for trial. FIF ra oF No v EMBER P REP IR LTIONS. —Edwin Ellison and Arthur Ellison. two boys of Cambria-place. were charged with stealing from the boot shop of Mr. W. Haynes. Flag-lane, a crate, value ss.—They pleaded guilty.—The Chief Constable said the crate was placed outside the shop by Mr. Haynes and afterwards missed. The police found portions of it in Ellison's house. Ho thought the coming sth of November was the cause of the boys taking the crate.— fhe elder boy. Edwin, received six strokes of the birch, and the younger was discharged.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>DARESBURY PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>DARESBURY PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—Before Mr. J. C. Parr (chairman), Messrs. W. Long, C. H Broadbent, H. Nield, and Harvey Talbot. Tan DRUNKEN LlST.—James Bostock, of Wellfold, Stockton Heath was charged with being drunk and disorderly on the 7th October.—Sergeant Gosling stated the case.—The defendant denied being drunk and his wire corroborated his statement. The magistrates discharged h m with a caution. —Henry Shaw. a labourer, who appeared in court the worse for drink, was fined 10s. and costs or fourteen days imprison- ment for being dru k and disorderly at Apple on. DOG OWNERS IN TROUBLE.—AIfred Croughton, joiner, Walton Inferior was surnm-med for keeping a dog without a license. P C. Brew proved the case and a fine of ss, and costs was imposed.—Albert Owen, Stretton was summoned for having on the 4th September allowed a dog to be at large in Warrington-road. Lower Whitley, He was fined 2s. 61. including costs.—William Bailey, Acton Grange, was mulc,ed in a similar penalty for allowing a dog to be at large at Little Leigh. DRIVING AN ENGINE WITHOUT I LICEKSE.—Robert Hud- son, of St. Helens was charged with being the owner of a locomotive engine and unlawfully did drive the same on the highway without having in force a license. P.C. Robinson state-I that he stopped the defendant at golre in charge of an engine drawing some chemical plant, and asked him whether he had a license, He replied that he had been in communication with the County Council but the license had not come to hand and he had auth rity to proceed in the meantime.—Superintendent O'Kell stated that the defendant was reported for using the engine in the Runcorn district. and the previous day the Runcorn magistrates fined him 103. and costs for haying no license, and 2s, 6d. and costs for having no address on the engine. Communications were read from the County Council Clerk (Mr. Potts) showing that the defendant had not received permission to travel in Cheshire,—A fine of 20s. and costs was imposed. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Mr. J. Brundrit, and Mr. H. Reynolds. LICENSING.—Mr. T. Jones made an application fir the transfer of the Navigatien Inn, Canal-street, from W. Faulk- ner to himself W. Rhodes applied for permission to sell at the Travellers Rest, Heath-road. Both opplicatious were granted. A TRACTION ENGINE DRIVER AND MIS LICI7NSE.—Robert Hudson was summoned for havi,lgon the 13111 inst.obstructed a free passage at Astmoor Bridge. Halton, by having a trolly thereon ; also with being owner of a locomotive did use the same before a license had been granted, and also having used the same, without having his res Pence affixed thereon on the 14th inst. at Halton—Mr J. W. Knowles of Widnes appeared for the defence.—Sergeant Lee proved to seeing a traction en- gine on the 13th inst. in charge of the defendant. There was no address on it.—P.C. 14regory said on the 14th inst. he saw the defendant in charge of a traction engine at Halton. The detendant said he had got no license. ut he had got. permis- sion to drive the engine from the Standing Joint Committee. He examined the engine and fond it had only defendant's name on, but no address -P.O Carrington said at about 11.16 p.m: on the 14th inst. he saw the engine in question on Astmoor Bridge at Halton. TI-ire were no lights attached to the engine.—Superintendent Okell said he saw the engine. There were no lights. There was room for a cart to pass, but it w3uld have to go past very carefully. He left a policeman in charge of it that night.—Mr. Knowles. in defence, admitted that a techaical offence had been com- mitted. The defendant came from St. Helens and was travel- ling ta the Castner Chemical Company's Works at Weston Point He travelled allright until he reached Astmcor Bridge. The engine was on the hire system and some bolts had broken belonging to the machine. The defendant couldn't write, and so he got his wife t 3 write about a license for him There was some mistake and the latter went to the Town Clerk of Chester, and of course his license was delayed—The mavistrates fined defendant 10s. and costs for not having a license, and 2s. 61. and costs for not having his residence affixed on the engine. The charge of ob- struction was dismissed. A SUPPO+ED POACHER —Abraham Bratt was charred with being suspected coming from land at Sutton Weaver on the 20th inst.—Sergeant Richardson said about 10-30 p.m. on 20th inst. he saw the defendant on a field belonging to Mr. Chadwick. He saw him shoot at something. and when he got in the road he tried to conceal the gun prodaced (which he was carrying at the time) behind the h.sdge. He searched him and found three cartridges on him.—A of 2s. 6d. and co ,ts. 13a.. was imp3sed, and the gun ordered ta be for- feited. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Assemas.—Mary Ann Feeney was charged with assault- ing Margaret Mulmearn on the 17th October. The complain- ant said Mrs. Feeney threw a jug of beer at her in a public- house, She gave her no provocation whatever. After hear- ing other evidence the magistrates dismissed the case Margaret Critchley, Egerton.st., was charged with assault. ing Polly Jones on the 20th inst. The evidence in this case showPd that the comolainant had made some complaint to the owner of the hnuses or which the parties were tenants in reterence to the defendant's children. On the date named she was standing near her door when Mrs. Critchley struck her ii the lace—Evidence for the defonee was called,and the magistrates decided to d Isnaiss this case also. DRUNK. —George Furnival was charged with being drunk and disorderly on Saturday night in Heath-road. P.C. Kent proved the case, and a fine of 10s. and costs, or in de- default 14 days was imposed. TUESDAY.—Before Mr. J. Norman and Mr. J. W Ligktburn. FREQUENTING.—WiIIiam Crosby, of Widnes, was charged with frequenting Church-street, the previous night with intent to commit a felony.—P.C. Gregory said he saw the prisoner in Church-street, and he was actin; in a very aus- picious manner. A woman shouted to witness, and told him that Crosby had been trying to induce her daughter to steal a shawl. He took him into custoiy. Sergeant Brewster,spoke to seeing prisoner begging in Halton-road the same day. He was committed to Kuutsford prison for 21 days hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>INTERESTING LOCAL WEDDING.</head>
        <p>INTERESTING LOCAL WEDDING.</p>
        <p>ROBINSON-GREENHOUSE.</p>
        <p>A very large amonnt of inter(st was taken in the wedding on Tuesday.of May Harding,second daughter of Mr. W. El, Robinson, Wilson Patten-street (the popular president of the Warrington Cricket and Bowling Club), to Mr. Walter Greenhouse, of Stockton Heath. The ceremony was performed by the Rev. T. Bridge.vicar of Poynton (Cheshire), assisted by the Rev. E. C. E. Carle- ton,vicar of Holy Trinity, Warrington, at which place of worship the wedding took place. Inside the building there was a large gathering of relatives and friends of the parties,and in Sankey-street, slso,a alrge crowd was as- sembled. Everything passed off smoothly and happily, and the marriage was one of the prettiest which has been solemnised in Holy Trinity Church for some time. Mr. Charles E. Bennett,Latchford,actei as `• best man," and the bridesmaids were Miss L. RobinsOn (sister of the bride), and Miss E Hayes (cousin), Southport. The bride was becomingly attired in white Duchesse satin, trimmed with Brussels lace and orange blossom, white felt hat and plumes,and she carried a handsome bouquet of white chrysanthemums and lilies of the valley. She also wore the bridegroom's presents. Her attendant bridesmaids looked very pretty in dresses of white surea silk, trimmed with pink silk arid chiffon, and Ducbesse hats wish ostrich feathers. They also carried bouquets of white chrysanthemums and pink carnations, and wore the presents which they had received from the bridegroom. Mr. F. Handall,organist, played a selection of appropriate music as the wedding party entered and left the church. After the ceremony the breAtfast was held at the residence of Mr. Robinson. The following is a list of the presents, which were valuable and of a useful character : Bridegroom to Bride, opal ring and pearl pendant brooch ; Bride to Groom, travelling case; Bridegroom to Bridesmaids, gold bracelets ; Mr W H Ito inaon, cheque; Mrs W H Robinson, house and table liner.; Miss L Robinson, paintings ; Mrs Greenhouse,dinner service; Mr It I` Greenhouse, dinner cruet ; Mr C and Mrs Hayes, picture ; Mr C B Bennett, silver fruit dishes •, Mr and Mrs Bonnell, claret jug ; Ass A Bonnell tidies ; Mr. and Mrs A. J. Robinson, tea rosy and holder; Mrs. W. Young,cetery dish; Mr and Mrs R.H Pearson,sifted sugar dish;Mr and Mrs Briggs, afternoon tea spoons ; Mrs Bonsell, bed spread; Mr and Mrs J. Heeketh, afternoon tea service • Mr and Mrs Greenough actor carvers ; Mrs Alit, biscuit box ; Miss Alit, tidies ; Mr and Mrs Ed Eustance, dessert knives and f.,rks ; Mr Aspinall, silver tea and coffee set and lcwt. sugar; Mr Ainsworth. vases; Mr and Mrs Taylor, (Northwich). cut spirit bottles ; Mr and Mrs Dutton. (Runcorn), hot water jug; the Miss ftimmer, ornament ; Miss Allmond, ivory paprr knife; Mrs Skinner, cameo and gold brooch and chatelaine; Mr and Mrs nrowlie, silver sugar dish and salt cellsrs ; Mr and Mrs F Goldsmith, serviette rings ; Mr and Mrs A Sutton, pep- perettes ; Mr Brown, copper jardiniere ;Mr and Mrs 'loft, toast rack ; Mrs Speakman, silver tr_st sp^ons and sugar tones; Mr and Mrs A Browne, fish slice Mr A Down, silver butter knife; Mr and Mrs ; Williams, cake basket ; Mr and Mrs Walker, album ; Mr and Mrs Holland, celery dish; Mr and Mrs B Caldwell, capper kettle; Mrs Smith (Liverpool), oriental tablecloth; Mr and Mrs A Paul, pickle bottle, ; Mr and Mrs Higginbottom, flower vases; Miss K Brundrett, doyleys; Miss Isiainwaring; pair plaques; Miss Briggs, case and comb bag; Miss Paul. duc'aess cloth ; Mrs and Miss Evans, breakfast crust; Mrs j Wright, jarctineres ; Miss Ross, picture ; Mr and Mrs Gill, toest rack ;Mr and Mrs J Lightfoot, painted mirror ; Mr W H Robinson, junr„ waitor ; Mr. Lennie Robinson, plaques; Mr and Mrs ssr 8 llowarci,arawing-room tab'e: Miss E Green, salt cellars ; staff from Greeuall Whitley, and Co„ clock ; Mrs J Sampson, serviette rings; Miss's Hayes, tea service ; Mr Birtfes, picture ;Mr and Mrs A 0 Robinson, fish knife and fork; Mr and Mrs Chidlaw, Wrexham, ladies companion; Dr and Mrs Sherratt, silver mounted scent bottle ; Messrs. Crosfield and Soils I cwt of soap ; Mrs Nicholson, trinket set. Mr. and Mrs. Greenhouse will be at home at " The Lynden," Stockton Heath, after Nevernh,r 17th.</p>
        <p>Uziamplefara STALE TOBACCO. 'Condy s Fluid V • t•;: instantly removes all Taste and Odour f..cra Mouth and Breath, - ,2?</p>
        <p>and prevents sore lips and tongtv which often cause Cancer. Use CONDY'S FLUID Asi Mouth-wash. Bead instructions with the Bottle. Sold Everywhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>EXAMINER SATUR 'AY, OCTOBER 31, 189 g.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER SATUR 'AY, OCTOBER 31, 189 g.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON Locos. v. STOCKPORT RANGERS.—This match was played at Stockport on Saturday last. Dur- ing the first half play was of an even nature, a dropped goal to the Rangers being the only point scored. In the second half the Locos playing down the slope crossed the Rangers lines twice and succeeded in converting one of the tries. Towards the close the homesters dropped another goal and the game ended in a draw. 8 points each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>FOOTB ILL.</head>
        <p>FOOTB ILL.</p>
        <p>Ll_BO. G. BUSSBY CO., are makers of High Class appli- ances for Football, Golf, Hockey, Lacrosse, and at Sports and Games, also for Gymnasia and Physical Exercises. Illustrated Catalogue may be obtained from local De tiers or the Sports Manufactory, London, S.B. Trade Mark G.G.B.</p>
        <p>RUGdY. WARRINGTON v. ROCHDALE HORNETS.</p>
        <p>The above match was played on the Wilderspool enclo- sure on Saturday, before 5,000 spectators. There was a strong breeze blowing towards the railway, and the day we.s dull and damp. Both teams were in full strength and a tough struggle was witnessed. For Warrington the players were: Heesom, full back; Radfnrd, Barber, Cross, and Lawless, threequarters ; Bate and Houghton, half-backs ; Barrett, Thornton, Nevins, Hill, Swift, Roby, Meecock, and Taylor, forwards. The Rochdale Hornets team included, Rigg, full back: House, Davies, Hickey, and Wightman, threequarters ; Janes and Evans, half-backs; Fletcher, Lancaster, Uttley, T. Gibbons, Melledew, Simpson, Gorman, and Tetiow, forwards. Warrington won the toss and played with the wind in their favour, that is from the pavilion end. Simpson, the visitors' captain, kicked off, and Cross returned cleverly. A line out got the ball loose and Barber kicked. Wightman secured but was tackled by Radford before he could get away. This put the homesters off the offen- sive and in dangerous proximity to the Hornets' use. Jones relieved, but Lancaster got off side and Warring- ton were given a free kick. Gorman failed to return the kick, and Nevins, picking up, kicked in touch. Some smart exchanges took place among the Hornets' backs, and Wightman relieved in touch. Hickey was upset by Barratt and Hill in attempting to r n, and the Hornets almost got away through Cross missing a pass. Bate was safe, however, and Cross put in one of his useful kicks. Radford, Barber, and Wightman all went for the oval, but the latter ar- rived firet and kinked dead. Evans and Jones got away only to allow Radford to kick in touch. Jones again got possession and gained a little ground. Warrington tried a forward rush and almost on the line Hickey re- lieved by a neat kick. Another penalty kick was given Warringtcn, and Taylor made an attempt at goal, only to see the ball kicked in touch. More loose play gave Lancaster a chance. With the ball at his feet he broke away and helped by Melledew transferred play to the home 25 before Heesom blocked the way. The Hornets now put in all they were worth, and Houghton,Bate,and Barber were successively downed before getting in a kick. At last the home captain took the ball from the scrimmage and kicked well in touch. A display of exchange kicking between the teams ended in Cross planting the oval near the Rochdale 25 flag. The homesters now gave a Hornets' line a hot siege. Passing between, Nevins. Taylor, Bate, Barber and Lawless, ended in the latter being knocked in touch. A moment later Lawless made a splendid drop at goal, which just failed. Bate got round a scrimmage and was held on the line by Hickey,who similarly stopped Cross. Hough- ton now secured and transferred te Bate, who in turn gave the ball to Barber. The latter had a clean run in when he was tackled b 3, the feet. Cross picked up the ball and passing like a shot to Lawless, the latter ran over. Hill failed at the kick. A pass forward nullified a good chance for Barber. Taylor mulled another free kick. Cross, Lawless and Bate all stopped some smart movements by their opponents. Radford got in a use- ful run and Jones getting offside Cross was given a kick. He essayed to drop and made a good attempt.Barher put the oval in the corner again,and Taylor utilised a penalty by kicking across the field. Lancaster returned but Warrington still kept is front of the visitors' goal.Crosa attempted another drop, which faikifi. Half-time arrived with Werrington one try ahead. Hill kicked off again and after some open play the Rochdale backs got possession. A brilliant passing bout looked promising but House dropped the ball, which rolled in touch. Rigg defeated Heesom with a kick and the home custodian was compelled to fall on the ball over his own line. Bate. Houghton, Thornton, and Cross re- lieved but Rigg kicked again, to some advantage. Law- less took a lightning pass from Bate and gained ground. Wightman and Evans were prominent, ard another grand passing movement almost sent House over the line. Cross tackled well however. Warrington were forced to concede a minor and were twice penalised. A good kick by Lawless changed the play and the same player almost- got over from a pass from Bate. Wight- man tackled Heeiom with the ball, and then Batt broke away. Being overhauled he kicked across to Lawless who had the line at his mercy when Rigg came up with a rush and kicked dead. Two more free kicks proved useless to Warrington. Evans started another move- ment which was spoiled by Davies. Rigg almost equal- ised with a free kick and the Hornets pressed hard. For a number of minutes they were scarcely off the home line, which was defended superbly. It was an anxious time, but when the whistle blew Warrington had re- entered Rochdale territory once more. The game ended in a fourth consecutive victory for Warrington by one try to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>RUNCORN v. MORECAMBE.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN v. MORECAMBE.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the cup-holders journeyed to Morecambe, this being the first time the clubs have ever met. Need- less to say the visit was looked forward to with interest, bet unfortunately the weather was of the vilest des- cription,rain and hail failing heavily at intervals.attended with sudden squalls, which rendered the somewhat primitive ground a perfect quagmire. Both sides made alterations in their teams, Robinson going half-back,and Myers taking his plact amongst the threequarters. It was intended to rest Y. Faulkner, who is suffering from an injured ankle, but unfortunatley Pendlebury met with an accident on • Friday, which necessitated J. Faulkner having to fill the vacancy, although far from being fit. Morecambe placed their crack wing three- quarter, Holmes, in the centre position, which he filled with distinction. The following did duty for Runcorn : —Houghton, back; Myers, Butterworth, Warder, and Richardson, threequarter backs; J. Faulkner, and G. Robinson, half-backs; W. Faulkner, Farmer, Lang- ley, Walker, Shepherd, Lightfoot, Hughes, and Gayter forwards. W. Faulkner started and Run- corn at once took up the attack and several pretty passing movements carried play close to the home lines, both Richardson and Robinson being grassed within a yard of the line. Blacow relieved momentarily with a screw kick, be the ball was soon back, the Run- corn forwards getting possession from nearly every pack Robinson got away cleverly on one occasion, and it seemed any odds on a score, when Holmes intercepted his pass and kicked clear. However, do what they would the seasiders could not clear the visitors out of their quarters, Runcorn at this junction having all the play. The home defence was very keen, and aided by a lot of luck they kept their lines intact. Houghton took a penalty kick, but the ball fell short. By short stages the seasiders got near the centre line, and Swain racing away from a scrimmage passed to Baguley, who threw wide to Holmes, and the latter putting on top speed scored a fine try close to the posts. Blacow kicked a goal. Stimulated by this success the homesters played up surprisingly, whilst the Runcorn combina- tion seemed to fall to pieces. The Morecambe forwards used their feet judiciously on the wet sloppy ground, whereas Runcorn every time they broke away tried to pickup, with the consequence that many op- portunities of gaining ground were lost. Just before the interval, from a kick by Warder, Elkin was at fault,'and lost the ball close to his own lines, and the visiting for- wards rushed up, but experienced "hard lines," one of the forwards hitting the corner flag in grounding. Half- time arrived with the score. Morecambe, one goal; Run- corn nil. Upon resuming, it looked as if the visitors would soon wipe off the adverse balance, Robinson, Farmer, and Myers being prominent, whilst Shepherd had an easy opening, but he preferred te pass to Myers, who " knocked on. A moment later J. Faulkner made a strong dash, but misfortune again dogged the steps of the Runcornians, be knocking the corner flag down when grounding. Judicious kicking by the home backs and quick following up by the forwards saw the Runcorn lines assailed, Houghton only saving in the nick of time. It was now evident that the seasiders were playing a winning game. The dash with which they followed up was a marked con- trast to the listless fashion of the Runcorn van-guard, who gave the home custodian •‘ yards of room" to get his kicks in, with the result that the moment the ball got amongst the visiting backs they were invariably "swamped" Once the Runcornians did make a burst, and a splendid sequence of passes saw the ball landed to Warder,who took a drop at goal,which went wide when a pass would have seen Richardson over the line. This was followed by a rush of the home forwards,where from a scrimmage near the centre line, Cornwaithe dribbled from the pack and screwed the ball across the Runcorn goal mouth, and Holmes, rushing up, outpaced all oppo- sition and added his second try. Blacow again improved. This raised great jubilation amongst the home suppor- ters,it being the first match this season that the Runcorn lines had been crossed. Up to the conclusion the homes- ters held the advantage, their forwards playing with untiring energy. The final result was, Morecambe, two goals (ten points) ; Runcorn, nil.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A" V. ROCHDALE HORNETS " This match was played on Saturday at Rochdale, before about 2,000 spectators. The Warrington team was: Boscow,full back ; Cooper,lcherwood,Scott,and Mr,nres, threequarters ; Higginbottom and Fairhurst,half backs ; Hughes. Jolley, Templeton, Scholtze, Andrews, Bailey, Wilcock, and Savage.forwards. Warrington kicked off, and the ball was returned well into the visitors'. quar- ters. A scrimmage took place near the line and one of the Rochdale halves getting possession he-tricked Fair- hurst with a sham pass and scored near the posts. The try was easily converted. After this the game was warmly contested, neither side having any advantage. On one occasion Isherwood secured and raced down the field. Ins pad of dodging the lull back himself he passed to Savage who missed the ball, and the only chance of equalising was lost. The game ended in Warrington being defeated by a goal (5 polnts) to nil.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON ST. MARY'S V. RUNCORN " A " TEAM.— The above teams met on the Runcorn around on Satur- day in dull weather, Runcorn "A" team winning after a splaadid game by a try to St. Mary's nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON GRAMMAR SCHOOL V. HI:IMM GRAMMAR SCHOOL.—This match was played last Saturday at Man- chester. The visitors, who lost the toss, kicked up hill and against the wind. The opening exchanges were rather in favour of t ,e Warrington Grammar School,but the state of the turf prevented accurate shooting. Ulti- mately the visitors gained their first point by the aid of R. Pierpoint. Not to be denied, however, the homesters scored two goals in quick succession, and led at half time by two goals to one. Immediately on resuming Hulme Grammar Schocl added a third point. But the visitors aroused themselves, and Wallace scored their second goal. The third one was obtained in fine style by Kidd, and from a splendid centre by Price,the fourth and last point was registered by Davies. Victory finally rested with the Warrington Grammar School by four goals to three. Riley,at outside right,was several times conspicuous with clever runs and centres._</p>
        <p>STOCKTON REATH V.LYMM BRIGADE.—PIayed at Lymtll on SAturday,Stoekton Heath winning after a hard game by three goals to two.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH V. PENKETH.—These teams met for the first time this season, on the ground of the former, when a keefily contested game was played. Penketh gained the coveted circle repeatedly, but their attempts to score were frustrated by the excellent defence of Gar- nett in goal. Stockton Heath, however, proved the bet- ter team, and won by three goals to one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>REMARKABLE INCIDENT.</head>
        <p>REMARKABLE INCIDENT.</p>
        <p>It is a scene in a lecture-room of a medical college. The professor is lecturing before an intelligent class of medical students. He is describing the human body,its defects and the danger by which it is surrounded. In order to illustrate it he has fluids from the human body, which he is subjecting to chemical tests. "Gentlemen," he said," I have described to you the appearance of the human fluid in a diseased state ; I will now show you how the same fluid appears in a healthy state," and he subjected his own to the test. As he held it up to the light for a moment, his hand trembled, he caught his breath,he paled and exclaimed,'•Gentlemen,l have just made a most horrible discovery ; I myself have Briglat's disease of the kidneys." In less than one year he was dead. The above dramatic and strictly true incident shows the terrible danger and mysterious nature of this modern disease, which may well be called the demon of the present century. It steals into the system like a thief, manifests its presence by the commonest symptoms and fastens itself upon the system before the victim is aware. It is nearly as heriditary as consumption,quite as common and fully as fatal. Entire families,inheriting it from their ancestors, have died, yet none of the number knew or realised the mysterious power which was removing them. Hundreds of people die daily by what is called heart disease, apoplexy, paralysis, spinal complaint, rheumatism, pneumonia, and other common diseases, when in reality it is Bright'a disease nf the kidneys. These are solemn facts, but all the more serious because of their solemnity. There has never been but one remedy known for the cure of Bright's disease, or even for its relief, and that remedy has become the most popular of any preparation known to the world. It is Warner's Safe Cure. It has taken men, and women, too, who were in the lowest stages and restored them to perfect health and strength. It will invariably check the first stages of this terrible disease, if taken in time. It is endorsed by physicians, approved by scientists and used by the beat people in the land. It is a scientific preparation and owes its popu- larity wholly to its power.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>NOTES AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>The announcement of the South-Eastern Railway Company that it intends to introduce the American sys- tem of railway carriages on c.rtain plrtions of the linel beginning with the service to Hastings, marks an epoch in the history of the development of accomodation so distinct as was that of the use of the Pullman carriages fdr first-class passengers. In all American carriages of the ordin try class there is a stove at each end for sup- plying heat, and a receptacle for water for the use of the passengers. This is filtered and, it need,hardly be said, iced, since no Anaerican ever dreams of drinking water, even in winter wlaich has not first ben artificially cooled. There is also a lavatory with a constant supply of running water and clean towels, all supplied without charge. It is to be hoped, therefore, that the arrange- ments on the South-eastern will include a supply of pure water.</p>
        <p>At the London South-western Police Court on Satur- day, John George Owens, aged 30, a traveller, formerly living in Beauchamp Road, Battersea, was charged on remand before Mr. Francis with having obtained money by fraud from Henry Russell Bignall, a traveller, of 28, Harbut Road, Wandsworth. The prosecutor had known the prisoner in business, and eight weeks ago met him by appointment at Charing Cross: The accused then told him that he had been in the diplomatic service, and that he had just received instructions from Lord Salis- bury to proceed to Constantinople with a special war- rant to arrest the Sultan of Turkey, and also that Mr. Chamberlain had offered hint the post of British Consul at Algiers, the salary being £lO,OOO a year. Tbe prose- cutor was invited to be his secretary at £l,OOO a year. He slw prisoner on several other occasions and, believing the representations, gave him, as he was short of money, several sums, amounting to £3O. The prose- cutor subsequently ascertained from inquiry at the Foreign and Colonial Offices that the statements were false.—The prisoner, on being arrested, said "Just wire to Mr. Chamberlain, and it will be all right. "—Mr. Cockerell. the head of the Consulate Department of the Foreign Office, proved that he had no knowledge of the prisoner.—The Clerk: Was he ever appointed Consul at Algiers P—The witness : Certainly not.—The Clerk : Or that he received £lO,OOO a year.—The witness : That is not the salary.—(Laughter.)—Mr. Francis : Nor have you given him any instructions from Lord Salis- bury to arrest the Sultan P—(Laughter,)—The witness: No—The prisoner, who made no defence, was committed for trial.</p>
        <p>Some idea of the enormous cost of the Presidential election in the United States may be gained from the fact that early in October the single item of franked envelopes which are supplied free of charge to members of the House of Representatives and Senate had cost the Government through the public printing office 140.000 dots. Over five million envelopes were printed for tho free transmission of one representative's speech. The "Evening Post" of New York also says that the ap- propriation for folding public documents at Washington has been exhausted owing to the great strain, and clerks are now working on faith of future rsmuneration. Measured by weight, from sixty to seventy-four per cent, of the mail matter that leaves Washington goes under frank. Many taxpayers, the Post adds, object to seeing their money diverted in this way to the uses of a campaign fund,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>BURNING ACCIDENT AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>BURNING ACCIDENT AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Harriet Platt, eight years of age, and residing at 75, Howard-st., was on Monday admitted into the Warrington Infirmary suffering from serious burns to the face. It appears that while the child was sleeping on a chair in front of the fire she by some means slipped off,and fell face forward on the bars of the fire grate, thus severely burning herself.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>GREAT SANKEY SCHOOL BOARD.</head>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Great Sankey School Board was held on Tuesday evening itt the Board School, there being present:—Mr.T. Halton (in the chair), Messrs. A. Bate and J. Kilshasy ; Mr. W. D. Jeans, jun. (clerk). and Mr. Geo, Clark (schoolmaster). The Clerk read the accounts, which showed a balance of £8 us. id. in hand. He also stated that no part of the call for £lOO had yet been paid but he expecced that a portiop of it would be paid by the 31st inst. The schoolmaster's report for the past month was read, in which he stated that on the 3rd ir.st, there were on the books 385, present 338, average 319 2 and the fees amounted to 183. 4rl ; on t he lOr ii 354 on the books, present 339, average 305.7 fees 14s. 41. ; on the 17th on the books 352, present 336, evrag, 308.1, fees 13s. 2d.; on the 24th 345 on the b3oks, present 329, average 308.0 fees 133. 21.; total amount of fees £2 14s. Several children had left the lower standards owing to the families hav- ing gone out of the district. The report of the drawing examinanion was read and favourably commented upon. The Clerk stated that in answer to his application for an estimate for the lighting of the school by gas he had received a communica- tion from Mr. Taylor, of the Warrington Corpora- tion Gas Works' to the effect that the school would be fitted up entirely with fittings for £l5 33. ; and the school-house for £3 7s. 61. On the motion of the Chairman, seconded by Mr. Bate, it was agreed that the work be proceeded with at once. Two applications had been received for the post of mistress, which will soon become vacant, and it was decided that the chairman and the master see .11. is Sykes, of Halifax, and report to a future meet- ing of the BoarA. The meeting then concluded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The last meeting of this Council previous to the Nov- ember election was held on Tuesday evening in the Council Chamber at the Town Hall. The Mayor (Coun- cillor Fairciough) presided, and there were also present —Aldermen Greensll, Sutton, Wright, Hutchinson, Harrison, Francomb ; Councillors Shaw Green,Shillcock, Smethurst, Bennett, Ellison, Furness, Tinnion, Rogers, Woods, Baird, Garner, Heaton, Lee, Monks, Longton, Davies, Cannell, and Messrs. J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk), Stanley Wilson (deputy town clerk), J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer), T. Longdin (borough surveyor),Dr. Gornall (medical officer). THE LATE MAJOR PICSMERE.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR, in moving a vote of condolence to the widow and family of the late Major Pickmere, remarked on the deceased gentleman's connection with the Cor- poration, and said it must be a consolation to his widow and family to witness the respect that was shown to his memory at the funeral. He wouid not .say anything further, as they would see from the newspapers the esteem in which the deceased was held. He then moved the following resolution :—" That we, the Town Council of the borough of Warrington,receive with deep regret the announcement of the death of John Richard Pickmere, Eq., honorary freeman and a justice of the peace of tae borough, and formerly an alderman and five times Mayor of the borough, and desire to record our high appreciatic.n of the valuable public services rendered by him to the borough, and the respe3t and esteem in which he was held by all classes of the com- munity for his invariable kindness and beneficence and his honourable and unblemished life. And we request the Town Clerk to convey to Mrs. Pickmere and her family our sincere sympathy with them in their bereave- ment."</p>
        <p>Alderman Sorrow seconded the resolution. The lamented death of their good friend Major Pickmere had as be (the Mayor) said, called forth many expressions of regret from the numerous friends he had and the public institutions he was associated with. It was difficult tl find wads to adequately express their deep sympathy with the widow and family at the loss they and the town generally had suffered. The fact that Major Pick- mere was elected on no less than four or five different occasions as mayor of that borough was convincing proof that he was regarded with affection and esteem by all classes, and that he was one of the most popular mayors Warrington ever possessed. (Hear, hear.) He begged to second the resolution.</p>
        <p>Councillor DAvrEs said he had known Major Pickmere as well as anyone in that room. He was a good friend to the Warrington Dramatic Society, and no one in that Council Chamber was more respected than the late Alderman Pickmere. No matter what society he was in he was a favourite. He was an old friend of his father. They had now both gone, and he hoped that future mayors would emulate their example. (Hear, hear.) Alderman HARRISON said if proof were required of the estimation in which their late friend and colleague, the late Major Pickmere, was held, the scene enacted last 'ruesday supplied it. The long line of carriages, and the orderly and well-dressed people, mostly in mourning habiliments,that followed his body to the Parish CLaurch, the listeners to the memorial service,and who afterwards followed his body to the cemetery to see the last of him, and the wealth of floral tributes that adorned his grave showed their lasting regret for the loss of one whose cherished memory awakened the acute sense of a more than ordinary loss. Major Pickmere possessed some sterling qualities of both head and heart, and none so conspicuously as that faculty of forgiveness which one of the deepest thinkers of the age had described as one of the most refined and generous points of virtue to which human nature could attain. He could not con- clude without referring to a pnrase from the Roman Catholic breviary, requieseat in pace, or in other wor is "may he rest in peace." and there were few who would not say" Amen" to so sweet a prayer. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Alderman GREENALL and Alderman HUTCHINSON also spoke in praise of the late Major Pickmere, the latter speaker referring more particularly to the deceased gen- tleman's connectien with the volunteers. The resolution was carried unanimously. - - MI THE WATCH COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This Committee reported the appointment of six new constables all except one being residents of Warrington. The Committee also reported that the chief inspector and clerk, William James, having given notice of his desire to retire from the police force after upwards of 31 years' service, it was resolved that the Town Clerk be instructed to convey to Mr. James the high appreciation by this Committee of his long service in the force. The Chief Constable having eartified the service of Inspector James as approved service for the purpose of the Police Acts, 1839 to 1893, it was resolved that there be paid out of the Police Pension Fund to Mr. William James a pen- sion for life equal to two-thirds of his annual pay. It was also resolved that P.C. Beacham he promoted to the rank of a first-class constable. The minutes were confirmed on the motion of the Mayor.</p>
        <p>STREET IMPROVEMENT AND LIGHTING COMUITTEE.-NEW</p>
        <p>HOUSES, &amp;C.</p>
        <p>The minutes of this Committee, in addition to those given last week, were as follows :—That thc plans of the following buildings be approved, five houses in Longford-st. for Mr. John Appleton; six cottages in Hardy-st. for Mr. James Astley ; new street off Grove- st., Latchford, for 11r. T. H. A. Pilkington •, six houses. a dairy, and two stables in Grove-st., for Mr. Wibber- ley and Mr. Walter Tw;ss. It was resolved that the Borough Surveyor be iastructed to mark on the Ord- nance Sheets of the Borough all such streets as in his opinion are of a secondary character as distinguished from the principal streets, to enable this Committee to define the building line in such streets. Alderman Stravoli moved the adoption of these minutes, which were agreed to.</p>
        <p>S&amp;NITARY WORKS COMXITTEE.-.REDUCTION IN TEES PRICE _ OF M&amp;NIIRE.</p>
        <p>At the request of the committee the agents for the sale of concentrated manure attended a meeting of the above committee, and discussed the question of price charged for the same, when it was resolved that until further notice the price of manure be reduced to £5 per ton,and that a discount of 10s. per ton be allowed on all ac- counts paid on or before the 31st August in each year. Councillor MONKS, in moving the adoption of the minutes of the above committee, said that perhaps the most important minute was that relating to the reduc- tion in the price of manure. The matter had been be- fore the committee for a long time, and had had a great deal of consideration given lb it, and naturally, too, be- cause it meant a loss of revenue to the extent of about £3OO per annum, and the result was that there would be an additional expense on the borough fund. As to the question as to how this loss is to be made up, he might say that experiments had been carried on recently at Longford the principal object being to reduce the cnst of manufacture,ie which way they hoped to make up the loss of £3OO. He did not think it necessary to go closely into the matter at the present time, but the object they wereaiming at was the concentration of the manure by means of a mechanical process instead of the evaporative process, the one adnpted hitherto. They had up to the present spent about £5O, and it had been demonstrated that the raw sewage contained seven per cent of solid matter. They had found five per cent, in suspension and two per cent, in solution. The five per cent. they were able to extract from the liquid by the mechanical process. The two per cent.had still to be treated by the evaporative process, and it looked as though in the future they would have two processes instead of one. The committee were of opinion that they would be able in twelve months from now to reduce the expenditure sufficiently to make up the £3OO. As to the reduction in the price of the manure,it was due to the competition of other towns and to the fact that su:phate of ammonia is cheaper,which induced the farmers to take that in preference to the manure the Corporation had to dispose of. In regard to the experiments, a chemical ques- tion arose, and they had engaged Mr. Haddock who had taken a large number of samples, and was preparing an analysis. He had received an interim report from him, which he (Councillor Monks) would read to the meeting :—" The results of the analysis of the samples I have taken at Longford fully justify the committee in going on with the experiments, and I hope to present my first report at the next meeting.' The outsile cost of these experiments would not exceed £lOO, and that they proposed to pay out of the current year's account. Councillor EvAps seconded, and the minutes were adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>PAVING AND SEWERAGE COMMITTEE--.THE FLUSHING OF</head>
        <p>PAVING AND SEWERAGE COMMITTEE--.THE FLUSHING OF</p>
        <p>THE SEWERS.</p>
        <p>It was resolved by this committee that the following final apportionments of the cost of forming and paving the unmentioned streets be approved :—Passage behind Parker-street. £35 us. 4d. ; passages behind Arpley- street, £25 163. 6L ; £29 163. 4d., and £24 14s. 11d. The committee visited the Bewsey district, and in- structed the Borough Surveyor to prepare plans for the paving of a large number of streets end passages. It was resolved that the sewer in Manch 'ter-road be con- tinued from Bruche House to the borough boundary ; that the tender of Mr. Thomas Stringer for that work at £285 be accepted, and that the sewer in Padgate-lane be continued to the new property erected by T. J. Rigby.— Other apportionments of the cost of street paving were as follows :—Passage behind Arplev-street £l6 is. 9d.- and passage behind Hanover-street,£46 123. 41.—Alder, man Wright moved, and Councillor Heaton seconded, the confirmation of the minutes.—Ceuncillor Bennett sad in case that might be the last opportunity he would have of addressing the members of the Council—(laugh- ter)—he wished to ask the committee if they could see their way to make an improvement in the ventilation of the sewers. Ventilating shafts were in Use in other towns and he thought the committee might make in- quiries as to the result of their use. He was also of opinion that the sewers should he better flushed. The sewers in his part of the district had been a danger and a disgrace to the town.</p>
        <p>THE PUBLIC ARE WARNED against the use of Foreign cocoas containing alkali, producing highly coloured prepara- tions offered as " pure eocoa." Cadbury's Cocos is absolutely pure and unadulterated, and is therefore the best. The " Lancet describes it as " the standard of.highest.nurity at present attainable,"—Advt.</p>
        <p>Alderman WRIGHT was surprised to hear the com- plaint, as the sewers had been better flushed than ever during the last twelve months. As to the question of ventilating shafts, he had spoken to the engineer from time to time, and up to the present he had not been able to advise him as to a better system than the one at pre- sent in force.</p>
        <p>Councillor BENNETT : nave you any data from other towns?</p>
        <p>Alderman WRIGHT replied in the affirmative, and said that he would be pleased to kt Mr. Bennett see it. The minutes were passed. SCHOOL ATTENDANCE IN WARRINGTON.—BETTER RESULTS</p>
        <p>Councillor SHAW GREEN, in moving the confirmation of the School Attendance mieutes, alluded to the annual school report, and to the tables at the end of it, which were of an interestin, but voluminous character. He observed th it ti.ey had now acoommodation for 12.558 children, and that there were 10,957 on the books. The average attendance for September, although that was a wet and miserable month, was 9,698—a considerable in- crease upon twelve months ago when the number was 8,861. (Hear, hear.)—Mr. Shillcock seconded, and the motion was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>TOWN BALLPARK AND BATHS COMMITTEE</head>
        <p>TOWN BALLPARK AND BATHS COMMITTEE</p>
        <p>The minutes of this committee contained a report of the bathkeeper, from which it appeared that the income from the baths for the past tour weeks was £2 18s., and for the corresponding period last year. £4 us. 2d ; de- crease, LI 13s. 2J. It was resolved that the tender of Mr. F. &lt;;* DAL for painting the outside of Bank House,at £lO is. 91. be accepted ; and that the best thanks of the committee be given to the Chairman and Deputy-chair- man for their valuable services during the past year.— The minutes were passed OR the motion of Councillor Dr. Cannel!, seconded by Coun. Smethurst.</p>
        <p>GAS COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This committee's minutes were of a routine charac- ter. It was reported that the average illuminating power of the gas was equal to 184 candles at both works.—The minutes were unanimously passed, the mover being the MAYOR, and the seconder Alderman W RIGHT.</p>
        <p>RIVERS COMMITTEE —COUNCILLOR HEATON'S LITTLE JOKE. At a meeting held on the 12th October, the committee considered the drawings of the preposed wharfs and railway sidings and the Warrington dock entrance, to which they had no objection provided a convenient cart nad be made from the lay-by to connect it with Arpley New-road. The committee also considered the drawings of the proposed culvert and penstock through the dam at Arpley, when it was resolved that the company be requested to proceed with the work. Councillor HEATON moved, and Councillor BENNETT seconded, the adoption of the minutes, the latter re- marking that the Council would be pleased to see that they were nbw in sight of the wharf, and he hoped be- fore long they would see the ships there. (Hear, hear, and laughter.) Alderman HARRISON referred to a speech delivered last week at a ward meeting by Mr. John Crosfield, on the Manchester sewage scheme. It was,he said,of a very doleful character,and it appeared that there was hanging over the Corporation— The MAYOR : It was decided at the last meeting that we should not discuss the matter, because it is not wise to show our hands.</p>
        <p>COURCiiiOr HEATON : The remarks made by Mr. Cros- field were in the nature of an electioneering speech, and as such must not ba taken very much notice of. (Laugh- ter, and cries of " Oh, oh.") The minutes were then agreed to. FIRE) ENGINE COMMITTEE.-THE FARM FIRE AT PADGATE.</p>
        <p>The cost of the attendance ;of the brigade at the fire at Mr. Thomas Bennett's farm, at Padgate, was. at a meeting of the committee, reported to amount to £4l 16s. 10d. Alderman HUTCHINSON moved the confirmation of the minutes of the cornmittee,and referred to the recent con- flagration on the premises of Mr. Thomas Bennett. He said the brigade worked satisfactorily to all con- cerned.</p>
        <p>Councillor SMETHURST seconded ard the motion was agreed to.</p>
        <p>WATER COMMITTEE.-THE PENICETFI WATER SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the above committee, it was resolved that notice be given to the London and N-irth-Western Railway Company of the intention of the Corperation to drive a shaft in the line of the highway under the Sankey Canal for the purpose of carrying the new mains re- quired for the Penketh water supply. The minutes were approved, on the motion of Alder- man PRAwords, seconded by Councillor LEE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>MISCELLANEOUS.</head>
        <p>MISCELLANEOUS.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the following committee were adopted without discussion i—Fioance and Estate, Health Corn- mittee,Museum, Library, Arts and Technical Instruction Committee, Cemetery Committee, Markets Committee, and General Purposes Committee. VOTE OF THANKS TO THE MAYOR. Alderman HARRISON moved :—"That tbe best thanks of this Council be presented to Mr. James Fairclough, Alderman of this Borough, for the satisfactory manner in which he has discharged the responsible duties devolv- ing upon him as Mayor and chief magistrate of this Borough during this his second year of office, and for the unvarying courtesy and impartiality displayed by him when presiding over the deliberations of the Coun- cil, and in its intercourse with its members." He said it was a very pleasing duty, although he should be very brief. He was sure that the resolution was one which would commend itself to the consideration and the ap- probation of every member of the Council. He had not had the pleasure of drawing it out himself, but he must admit that that had been done with great skill and abil- ity, leaving him very little to say iudeed. If he were to take a word from or add one word to it he would spoil its force and effect, and, therefore, he would content himself by submitting the resolution, which he did with much pleasure. (Hear, hear, and applause.)...</p>
        <p>Councillor &amp;Taw Glutnn seconded. He said he had never received anything but kindness at the hands of the Mayor, and the generous manner in which he had discharged his duties had marked the two years during which he had occupied tile chair. He was also conscious of the great amount of time he had spent in the service of the town in the multifarious duties connected with the office, and he believed he had a sincere desire to promote the interests of the ratepayers generally. They had sometimes felt a little difficulty under his rule, because he had brought down the hammer pretty sharply, but he had never shown any partiality and he believed he had honestly endeavoured to do his duty. (Applause.) Councillor DAVIES said he could not allow that oppor- tunity-to pass without adding something to what had been already said as to the manner in which the Mayor had discharged his duties during the past two years. He had known his Worship for .35 years, since he (the speaker) was ten years of age, and during the whole of that time he did not appear to have changed a bit. (Laughter.) He was not speaking in a flattering manner at all. His Worship was a little balder, but that was all—(laughter) -,-and that preservation had been brought about by his generosity, goodness and kindness. (Hear. hear.) Councillor Davies alluded to the Mayor as a cricketer and sportsman generally,and remarked that a good cricketer generally made a good business man. He spoke in very complimentary terms of the Mayoress and in conclusion expressed a hope that the Council would see its way to take down the shop at Bridge-foot, a matter of which he had referred from time to time in the Council Chamber. He hoped when he left the Council the Corporation would buy a ten-ton gun from Daintith's and blow up the (Laughter.) He was sorry he was severing his connection with the Council, and that he had to say " good-bye " to them, but he would rise again some day as an independent member of the Council. Councillor BENNETT, speaking as one who had occas- ionally been stopped by the hammer of the Mayor, said his Worship had no doubt pulled them up from a sense of public duty. They had learnt to respect each other in that Council, no matter how much they differed, and to feel that all of them were animated by disinterested desires for the public good. (Hear, hear.) Councillor WOODS, referring to the Mayor's ruling said he had, of course, to use the whip occasionally. He was sorry they were parting with some of their nuisances— (laughter)—colleagues—but he hoped some day to see them back again, re-varnished and re-gilded. (Laugh- ter.) He hoped the Mayor would not be allowed to rest in peace, but that he would be in office again. (Applause.) Alderman Surrom also supported the resolution, and said they all felt that his Worship had done his duty faithfully and impartially during thm past year: and they hoped to see him in offi7,e next year. (Hear, hear.) The resolution was th,n put to the meeting and agreed to.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR, in reply, said he was very much obliged to the members of the Council for the kind vote of thanks which had been given to him for his past services. If he had presided over the meetings of the Council and the Bench to their entire satisfaction he was amply rewarded. At the same time, he should like to say that if he had been at times, a little too severe with any member, it was not because he entertained any ill-feeling towards him, but because he wished to conduct the business of the Council in a proper, orderly, and expediticus manner. He thanked them again for their kind vote of thanks. (Applause.) The Council then rose.</p>
