      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>ST. PETER'S CHURCH SALE OF WORK. The proceeds of the three day,' sale of work amount to £lO7, which is considered very satisfac- tory. A WARRINGTON HAWKER FINED AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>On Monday, at St. Helens, Charlotte Stanley, of Mersey-street, Warrington, was fined ss. and costs for hawking cheese in the street at Sutton without having a market permit. FAIRCLOUGH V THE MANCHESTER SHIP CANAL COMPANY. In the Chancery Division of the High Court of Justice,on Monday,Mr.Justics Kekewich mentioned the action of Fairclough v. the Manchester Ship Canal,which is understood to be a heavy actiontand which had been fixed for hearing on the 3rd of Nov., stating that he had reason to believe that the date would or might be altered. A CATHOLIC CHURCH MISSION. A fortnight's mission was opened at the above church on Sunday morning before a large congre— gation. After the mass, the Rev. Father Fabian preachel the opening sermon, and exhorted the congregation to take the opportunity now offered to them of gaining the salvation of their souls,for a mission was a special grace Riven to them from God. The mission, which has since been well at- tended, is in the hands of the Pasionist Fathers. FREE LIBRARIES AND TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION.</p>
        <p>At the annual meeting of the librarians of the Mersey district, held at sale on Friday, Mr. Madeley (of Warrington) remarked that libraries could do something in the matter of the provision ot works on technical subjects for readers, but he would call attention to this, that it was just that particular class of books which it was most expensive to pro- vide. Unlike all other books, such works were out of date immediately, and they constituted a class of books that needed constant renewal. He hoped the technical instruction authorities would always bear A that in mind, and remember that whilst the library was an organization they could use for the distribu- tion of technical literature,the powers of the library, as long as its rate was limited as at the present time, would only in a limited degree be able to per- form such work of distribution. GOOD TEMPLA.RY AT LATCHFORD. The weekly session of the Bonehill branch of the 1.0.G.T. was held on Monday evening in the Tem- perance Hall, Latchford. Bro. Councillor L. Ellison, Chief Templar presided. There was a good attend- ance. Various communications were read, and lodge business transacted. The programnie for the evening consisted of a discussion on opinions on lodge programmes. There was a lively debate. Bro. W. Pearson opened the discussion, and was sup- ported by Bros. J. Ditchfleld, A. Ford, H. Blease, E. Cliff, H. Fletcher, T. A. Jenkins, A. Hampton, and the Chairman Each expressed his desire to make the future programmPs interesting and instructive. SOCIALIST Si EETIN4II. There was a good attendance at the fourth of the series of socialist meetings held in the Druids Hall on Sunday eveninw,wben Mr. James Johnston, J.P., of Macclesfield, delivered a lecture on "Social Con- trasts." The lecturer called to his aid a number of lime-light views which added interest to his lecture. Be contrasted the slum life with the life of the aristocracy, and showed pictures on the screen of the style of living in both classes, and also showed some pictures depicting the effects of Christian socialism. He commented on the duty of socialists and co-operators combining together to do some practical work on the lines of profit-sharing schemes, etc. The chair was occupied by Mr. F. Stott. Several labour hymns were sung. THE RESULT OF A FALL. An inquest was conducted by Mr. F. A. Jones at the Workhouse on Saturday morning concerning the death of Jane Price,aged 71 years, who resided at 12,Stamford-street. Deceased died at the work- house on Wednesday.—Victoria Wheeler, 13 Oliver- street,stated that the deceased was her mother. She last saw her alive on Tuesday week, and at that time her mother complained that she had fallen on the stairs. She said that there was no latch on the bedroom door; they used a bit of wire instead. She got hold of the wire which came out of the door, causing her to fall.—rrancis Simmons, general labourer,Stamford-street,gave evidence as to seeing the deceased fall on ths stairs. Dr. Braide having given evidence, a verdict of " Accidental death" was returned.</p>
        <p>THE DROWNING OF A Boy IN THE MERSEY. Mr. F. A. Jones, deputy-coroner, held an inquest at the Workhouse on Saturday,on the body of a boy about eight years of age, which was found in the Mersey on Wednesday night, and which up to then had not been identified.—John Bennett,flat-captain, said he was on the river at Howley Quay about 815 p.m., on Wednesday. He saw the body floating in the water near his flat. He at once caused in- formation to be given to the police. Inspector Tay- lor said that in consequence of information received he went down to Howley Quay. He assisted in getting the body out of the water. It was the body of a boy, and was in an advanced state of decom- position. It appeared to have been in the water six or eight weeks. He found a quantity of horse chestnut' in the pockets The jury returned a ver- dict of" Found drowned."—The clothing has since been identified by Mr. Martin Lee, contractor, 27, Albert-street, Didsbury, as having belonged to his son Martin, who was seven years of age. The lad had been missing since the 26th September. He was last seen following the Didsbury Workhouse Band through the streets of that place.</p>
