      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>Kettero to tin 04itor,</head>
        <p>Kettero to tin 04itor,</p>
        <p>RELIEF FOR ARMENIANS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Having seen the oft-repeated appeals of Lady Henry Somerset and Miss Willard in several of the cur- rent papers, for help in the noble work (which they have undertaken) of succouring the Armenian refugees, who are continually arriving in large numbers at Marseilles, in a very destitute condition, the women of Earlestown and district have formed a comrnit,ee for the purpose of raising money and clothing in the immediate vicinity for the aforementioned object which yill be afterwards sent direct to Lady H. Somerset for distrilaution. Contribu- tions of money or clothing (men's chiefly) will be thank- fully received by the treasurer, Mrs. Dobson, 46, Lec,h- street. Earlestown ; the secretaries, Miss E. Marrow,l34, Market-street; Miss Matthews, The Vicarage, Market- street, or any member of the committee.—yours, E. M iRROW. M. MATTHEWS.</p>
        <p>MR. KENYON AND THE MARRIAGE LAWS. Sir,—ln reply to Mr. Kenyon's query, " What has my being a Christian or not being a Christian to do with the marriage laws," I maintain that it has everything to do with the question. If Mr. Kenyoc is a Christian, he is bound by the laws at Christian morality; and in re- pudiating the binding nature of the sacred hoed of matrimony, he is sinning against one of the most essen- tial laws of the Christian faith. From the earliest ages, the Christian Church has always regarded Christian marriage as a sacrament. Of this we have abundant proof in the writings of the Fathers, the decisions of the great Councils, and from the liturgies of the Western and the Oriental Churches, including the Coptic, Jacobite, Syriac, Nestorian, and other bodies of Christians. The gospel, which was once upon a time, alleged to be the rule of faith for Protest- ants, forbids a man to have more than one wife, and a wife to have more than one husband. " Have you not read," says our Saviour, "that Ho who made man in the beginning, made them male and female? And He said, for this cause shall a man leave father and mother, and shall cleave unto his wife, and they shag be two in one flesh. Therefore they are no more two, but one flesh." Matt. xix 4-6. Three of the evangelists, as well as the apostle of the Gentiles, proclaim t'ae indissolubility of marriage, and forbid a wedded person te engage in second wedlock during the life of his spouse. There is scarcely indeed a moral precept more strongly enforced in the gospel than the indissoluble character of marriage validly contracted. The Catholic church, following the light of the gospel, forbids a divorced man to enter into second espousals deicing the life of his former partner. Therefore,from a Christian standpoint (and it is as a Christian, and not as a mere citizen that I oppose diverce a vinculo), nothing can put an eon to the bond of a consummated Christian marriage, except the death of one of the par- ties ; but in some countries the State refuses to recog- nize this principle, and takes on itself to dissolve " marriages.by a decreee which purports to put an end to the bond. Such a decree is absolutely valueless in con- science,and it does not give liberty in conscience to either party to marry again,so long as the other lives. In order that we may understand what Mr. Kenyon does really advocate in this connexion, he will please observe that dissolution of marriage must be carefully distinguished from what is called separation front bed and board, or judicial separation, which justifies the parties to a mar- riage living apart, but does not purport to enable either to marry again during the life of the other. For it is often the case that nullity,dissolution,and separation are often confounded under the name of divorce. Will Mr. Kenyon kindly inform your readers whether he so far disregards the explicit teaching of Christ and the teach- ing of the Catholic Church, as to uphold divorce a vinculo ?</p>
        <p>Mr. Kenyon is clearly in error in stating that the marriage laws prevent people from separating. But he is not candid enough to acknowledge his error! He is also in error in attributing so mady direful calamities to the strictness of the marriage tie. We have proof of the disastrous effect of polygamy upon society in the fact that bigamy is punished as a crime in all states whose civilization has been derived from the Gospel. Referencel has been made above to dissolutidns of marriage granted by a civil court. This altogether un-Christian practice originated in England in the seventeenth century, since which time it has spread widely, with ruin. ous consequences to society. The first dissolution of marriacos by authority of Parliament occurred in the year 1665. A letter has survived which tells by what disgraceful means the passing of this disgraceful measure was secured. An agent of the petitioner writes that he got six-and-forty of the members of the House of Commons to the Dog Tavern at Westminster, and gave them a dinner; and as soon as they had dined, they were " carried "to the House and passed the Bill without amendments. Mr. Kenyon has in a general way (he seems to despise particulars) attributed certain unfor- tunate occurrences to the nature of the laws of marriage. But what has the Christian sacrament done to lift woman to the exalted position she now holds in society? What innumerable blessings has it bestowed upon humanity! We have only to consider the position of woman under pagan civilization, to understand fully the nature and the extent of these blessings. The history of woman in Pagan countries has been,with 'rare exceptions, an unbroken record of bondage, oppres- sion,and moral degradation. She had no rights that the husband was bound to respect. In many of the ancient empires of Asia,notably in Babylon, India,and Lydia,the wife was bought like meat in the shambles, or like slaves lin the market place. And Mr. Kenyon would have us go back to Pagan customs, and disregard the sacredness of the holy bond of the holy Sacrament of Matrimony, which alone cured the evils of the Pagan system of bondage.—Yours, &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>SURSUM CORDA.</p>
        <p>IMPROVEMENTS IN FIRE-EXTINGUISHING</p>
        <p>AND FLUSHING SEWERAGE ARRANGEMENTS. Sir,—Would you oblige by space in your valuable paper for reference to the advice of the late expert with reference to the increase in the size of the water-pipes, and also the nunaber of hydrants at a time when the Corporation are supplying with water the neighbouring villages which will eeriously reduce the pressure. After very seriously considering the above, I think it would be more advantageous to provide water-stations. I would recommend that the first station be at the large bath which contains 105,000 gallons capable of being discharged in 3-1 hours. The arrangements are such that no cost would be incurred, and would present advan- tages for fire-extinguishing purposes within a 300 yards radius:which includes the following principaljbuildings : Town Hall, Infirmary, Drill Shed, London and North- Western Railway Goods Station, Williams' and Clay's, Conservative Club, Post Office, Guardian Offise, Town Offices, King-st. Brewery, Baptist Chapel, Methodist Chapel, and Heath-side Schools. One of the small baths is also available without any outlay for most of the above imparta.nt buildings, both engines being utilized at the same time. (N.B.—All these stations will be provided with Appleton water which requires no pumping, and which is continually running to waste in the Ship Canal). No. 2. or Market Hall station, would be available by taking .one of the cellars in the North West corner of the Market Halhand converting it into a tank, by means of which a great deal of valuable property would come within the 300yds area, viz :—Bridge-street, Horse- market-street, Sankey-street, Butternaarket-street, and all adjoining streets. No. 3, or Cockhedge station, would be available by tapping one of the hot water tanks, and bringing it by means of a twelve inch pipe to the edcp of the footpath in Orford-straet, and would supply the following build- ings:—Cockhedge milln,Borax works, Greenines, rheatre Royal, Stubb's file works, Parr's bank, and all property within a 300yds radius.—Your, No. 4 or Gas-street station would provide fire ex- tinguishing purposes for the following important werks, viz, Tinsley's, Walker's and Reynold's tanneries ; For- shaw's mechanical works ; Richmond and Co's ; Castle Rubber Co's ; Hut&amp; inson's basket works, rewlish and Griffith's tool works ; Gas works ; and the lower part of Bridge-street.</p>
        <p>No. 3. or Crosfield's station, will supply the following: —Crosfield's worka,Crosfield's mission, Robinson's, Skin- and Co's. glass works, Catholic Church, Whitecross wire works, and Bank-quay passenger station, besides other adjoininE property. No, 6, or Workhouse station, will provide for the workhouse, where they have a pond which only requires deepening to make it available for the following :—ln- fectious Diseases Hospital, the new Infirmary which is about to he erected, Wilkinson's cutting shop, Plump. ton's tool works, the higher portion of Whitecross works, and the Cheshire Lines goods station. No. 7, or Ryland's Station,which will be at the top of Howley-lane, will provide for the following :—Rylands' Wire Works. Parish Church, Training College, Naylor's Joinering Works, Ship Canal Wharf, Howley Mills, National Schools. and adjacent property. No. 8, or Bewsey-road Station. will provide for the fol works :—Wire Rope Works, Bewsev Forge, Walker's Brewery, Tanner's-lane Tanning Company, St. Paul's Church, Wesleyan Chapel, Wycliffe Chapel, St. Alban's Catholic Chapel, Collin' s and Wallington's Build- ing Works, and property within 300 yards area. No. 9, or Winwie.k-road Station, will provide for the new Tannery, Blue Cat School, Hallam Brewery, Barker's Engineering Works, St. John's Church, Guest's Tannery, Flepherd's Malsters, Tanners'-lane Tanning Company,St. Ann's Church,Co-operative Stores, Central Station, and other works in the vicinity.</p>
        <p>WILLIAM DIGGLE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>EXAMINERS SATURDAY OCTOBER 31 1596</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINERS SATURDAY OCTOBER 31 1596</p>
        <p>TEMPERANCE MEETING IN WYCLIFFE</p>
        <p>HALL.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening a temperance meeting promoted by the Independent Order of Good Tempters was held in Wycliffe Hall, Warrington. There was a good attend- ance. The chair was occupied by Mr. E. J. Guest, J.P., and he was supported on the platfOrm by Bros. Colin- cillor J. Melina, J.P., Grand Chief Templar of England, Rev. Joseph Lee, chief chaplain of England, Councillor Luke Ellison, Revs. W. Potts, G. Harbottle, W. G. Triggs, J. Finch, J. S. Hughes (Hill Cliff), Colour. Sergeant Mantle, Messrs. A. Ford, J. W. Roberts, F. A. Jenkins, A. Newell (Liverpool), G. Clarke (Earlestown), and S. B. Stringer. After singing, and prayer by the Rev. W. G. Triggs, The CHAIRMAN said he believed they were on the win- ning aide. (Hear, hear.) It might not seem so at the moment. Those who were opposed to the views they held in regard to temperance might point to the last election, and to the wealth and power and influence of the drink traffic as it was to-day. But greater forces than these had been against great and wondrous move- ments in the days gone by, which had been triumphant. They might think they were moving slowly but be be- lieved they were moving surely to certain victory. In every church to-day the great majority of the ministers and the best workers were teetotalers and were in favour of that great movement. The great Liberal party was committed to their cause,and when the party came into power they believed it would give them their help. (Applause.) Public opinion was influeecing the public magistracy and it was a harder thing to obtain a license now than it was some time ago. (Hear, hear). But they were hoping the people themselves would de- cide, how many licenses there should be. (Applause.) Some people called teetotalers fanatics,weuld be tyrants, and Utopian. He wished they were more fanatical than they were. He should be glad if he deserved the name. It was also said that they were tyrants and that they would thrust down the throats of people the tenets they held. They did net wish to thrust anything down people's throats but they wished to persuade people to accept their views. Those were the tyrants who thrust their gin palaces into the midst of people who wished to live peaceable lives, as everyone of those places was a centre of quarrelsomeness, riot, and poverty. (A.pplause.) He had it on the authority of the Mayor of Warrington that the part of Warrington where there were the most nublichouses was a very hell upon earth. (Hear, bear.) He (the chairman) did not think the Mayor was very far wrong. (Applause.) The Rev. W. POTTS said he was glad to hear the speech of the Chairman's because he believed it had the right ring about it. The presence of himself and the other Wesleyan ministers at that meeting was to show which slim they were on. They were on the right side. (Applause.) Twenty-five years ago when in Scotland the Grand Chief Templar spoke in one of his (Rev. W.Potts) chapels,and he believed the temperance movement must become more and more a question with the churches. (Applause.) He was sure there was great cause for a revival of the temperance movement in Warrington,and he hoped they might live:to:see a larger gathering than the present. (Applause.) Mr. A. Mtwara, (Liverpool), read letters of apology for non-attendance from the Revs. John Yonge, Charles Andrew, and W.H. Drummond. The Rev. J. A. .Lau said that in several parts of the district the Good Tempters were making inroads upon the people. They had some large lodges and some of the best men in the order were to be found in South West Lancashire. He hoped the lodges would receive some stimulus and encouragement from the visit of the Grand Chief Templar. It was urged against them that they intended to rob the poor man of his beer, but in re- ply they ceuld say that the drink traffic had robbed the poor man of his bread. (Applause.) Councillor JOSHPH Marans, J.P., said the Good Tern- plars recognised that the Preston pioneers inaugurated the modern temperance movement,but they claimed that the practice of total abstinence was as old as humanity. They were prepared to prove that tested by any stan- dard, whether by powers of endurance,tength of life, or capacity for enjoyment, the teetotalers stood head and shoulders above the moderate drinker or drunkard. (Hear, hear.) Proceeding to speak with reference to licenses,the speaker said it was the duty of the magistrates ' to consider the needs of the neighbourhood. He believed were it not for the fact that men had been granted lic- enses over and over again by way of custom there would not be half of the licenses granted each year that there were now. He thought the whole of the people should be allowed to say whether they needed a publichouse or not. (Hear, hear.) The temperance movement was not going to stop beeause one election went against it. They did not expect to work a great reform in five minutes. But for the benefit of those who stated that the temper- ance people had gained nothing, he might say that they had had twenty-five clauses or acts of Parliament passed in the House of Commons. (Applause.) They were always winning something or preventing something from being passed. In 1888 they succeeded in defeating the Compensation Clauses and that was worth more than any Act they got passed. They might not be quite so powerful as they wished in constructive leeislation, but they were demons to obstruct. (Laughter.) They had been agitating in England in • this way while their Colonial dependencies had done the work. Councillor Matins proceeded to speak of the progress of temperance in the Colonies, the United States, and in Sweden. Referring to the Good Tempters Order he said they had gone on from strength to strength. In Great Britain they had 1,400 full branches and 700 junior branches. He showed how English soldiers and sailors had been missionaries on behalf of the Good Templar Order, by forming branches at places where they had been staying. In the South West Lan- cashire district there were 48 ledges. Their movement was growing and they would win. Great as the drink bill was now it was seven million pounds less than it was twenty years ago when there was a smaller population. Bad as the condition was now, what would it have been but for the efforts of temperance folk? He asked them to join hands in this great work. There were a great many who bad discontinued working in the temperance cause. He asked them te join again. The Good Temp- ters Order was by far the largest temperance organisa- tion in existence. They thanked Gad for having assisted them thus far and having the confidence of His favour they would continue this good work. (Applause.) The Rev. G. HARBOTTLM in proposing a vote of thanks to the'Chairman, said he believed more harm was done to the bodies and souls of people between six o'clock and ten o'clock on Saturday night than in any four other hours in the week. He rejoiced that they had that Saturday night meeting. He wished that they could have some meetings on Saturday that would keep people out of the public houses. (Hear, hear.) Councillor Enrason seconded the proposition, and said it was 24 years since he first joined the Good Templar Order in Warrington. He thought that meeting would stimulate them to go on with their work and fight against the demon of intemperance. (Applause.) The Rev. C.Fneert put the proposition to the meeting, and it was carried unanimously. The CISAIRM4N acknowledged the compliment, and proposed a vote of thanks to Councillor Matins. This was seconded by Mr.Newann,and carried with acclama- tion.</p>
        <p>_ During the evening Master Wallington played a violin solo, and Mr. Griffiths contributed a song. Mr. W. H. Payton was the accompanist.</p>
        <p>THE REV. JOHN YONGE AND THE MUNICIPAL</p>
        <p>ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>A Gospel Temperance meeting was held in Wycliffe Church on Sunday evening, after the ordinary service. A stirring address was given by Councillor Joseph nMalice,apathetic J.P , in whoth tei rtgeemd peel rl apnrcees ecnatu steo. b err he ne edrrgi entki c d s e enrs were not apathetic, but fought to strengthen their trade, and it would .not do far them to be apathetic. (Hear, hear.) Warngtnn, he said, was largely domi- nated by beer, which -affected everything. It entered in politics and interfered with the governing of the town our and the administration of justice. (Applause.) Administration was corrupted. (Hear, hear.) The Politics of drink must be crushed and the town governed in the interests of tlse people and not In the interests of those whose only purpose was to amass wealth regard- less of producing the impoverishment of the people. (Applause.) He was thankful that all the Wesleyan ministers in the town were teetotal. There was hope ' for Warrington. (Applause). Let them not be idle. The Temperance movement had cost thousands. of pounds to attain the position it now occupied. . Mr. W. H. WALLINGTON gave a capital h. ren-i.en of " Callto arms,arm vs brave." The Rev. Joan Yoraos said there was a municipal election coming on directly,and he thought all Christian and temperance people in Warrington should be true to their colours in that election. There was no doubt what- ever—it was no use mincing matters—that the liquor traffic had had an influence on the Town Council injuri- ous to the town. (Hear, hear.)lt was in the hands of the municipal voters to put an end te that on Monday next. (Hear, hear.) He was not going to say a single word against individuals. The gentleman who retired from the ward they were in might be an exceedingly good and , worthy person but he (Mr. Yonge) was dead against his business. Were he the biggest Radical possible he would never vote for him. If the temperance people would be true to their princinles and not do as he had heard of temperance men doing in the elections, canvassing for publira no, but if necessary sacrifice their pertv,then they might hove for something to be done. (Applause.) It was time they were up and doing. (Hear, hear.) That business had got to he checked and to be put down, hut only the temperance and Christian people in the town had the power to do it. (Hear,bear.) He hoped every- body present was prepared to vote right from a temper- ance point of view in the forthcoming municipal elec- tions. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>AN ARMY PENSIONETt FOUND DEAD IN BED.</head>
        <p>AN ARMY PENSIONETt FOUND DEAD IN BED.</p>
        <p>Robert Robinson, 63 years of age, an army pen- sioner, who lived in lodgings at 19, Bank-st., was found dead in bed at 4-30 on Sunday afternoon. He was left in bed on Sunday morning by another ledger,who heard him cough during the night. No inquest will be held, as death was from natural causes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of this Council was held on Tuesday at the Dutton Workhouse. Mr. J. Mclntyre, J.P., presided, and there were also present—Messrs. J. Knowles (vice-cbairman), T. Lewis, T. Wright, E. W. Davenport, J. M. Williams, H. E. Heywood. J. Darby- shire, W. Pickering, J. B. Chadwick, E. Bratherton, T. Darlington, H. Talbot, 1.P., S. Woodward, T. Baxter, C.C.. C. Roylance,J. Brandreth, J. Parr, W. Bradford, J. Stokes, J. Ashton (clerk), G. Ashton (deputy clerk), Farrington (nuisance inspector), Swinton and Diggle (surveyors).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>APPLETON PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>APPLETON PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The minntes of the meeting of the Appleton Parochial Committee were read. They showed that the Clerk re- ported on the provision for the construction and lighting of Twenty Steps Bridge, Stockton Heath, and that a sec- tion of the Act of 1890, provided for the construction of a bridge, near Twenty Steps Bridge, bat said nothing about lighting the same. It was resolved that the clerk write to Messrs. Grundy, Kershaw and Co., and point ut that although according to the letter of the A," of 1890,the Company might not be compelled to it the Midge, the committee think very stronel. .At the pre- sent bridge was practically the one r'' -• led by the Act of 1885, and therefore should be dek.:t with in the same way,as that bridge would have been, as in fact it had been up to a recent date.—The minutes were approved on the motion of Mr. Davenport.</p>
        <p>SUTTON WATER SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from the Local Government Board sanctioning the loan of £BOO for works of water supply to the township of Sutton. The letter added that the Local Govornment Board had learned that the village of Sutton, is badly drained and that many of the houses have cesspools which are seldom emptied, and requested therefore that the District Council would take the mat- ter into consideration with a view to formulating at an early date a scheme for the efficient disposal of the sewage of the village.—Tho Inspector (Mr. Farrington), said the assertion respecting the cesspools, was contra- dicted when the Local Government Inspector was mak- ing his enquiry. There were not more than two cess- pools in the village, except those outside on the roads, to receive the quicksands from the L.and N.W. Bridge and Sutton Park.—ln order to get the works of the water supply executed as early as possible it was de- cided to advertise for tenders at once.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>COMPLAINT FROM TNELWALL.</head>
        <p>A COMPLAINT FROM TNELWALL.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from Mr. W. B. Cook, Clerk of Thelwall Parish Council, calling the attention of the District Council to the state of the drain in Bell-lane.— Mr. Swinton eonsiderhd the drain was a private one from the farmhouse.—The subject was referred to him for enquiries. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>TEM ROADS AT H&amp;LTON</head>
        <p>TEM ROADS AT H&amp;LTON</p>
        <p>A letter was read from Mi.. A. Woodier, clerk to the Halton Parish Council, calling the attention of the coun- cil to the bad state of the road past Mr. G. Leathwood's house, and also to the very dangerous state of the pit near to Hallwood ; these the Halton Council considered the District Council ourght to attend to. It was also unanimously resolved that the District Ccuncil's atten- tion be celled to the road past Halton Lodge, from the top of the old fountain gate to the parish of Clifton. This road, it was considered, should be adopted by the District Ceuacil.—Mr. Diggle, referring to the road men- tioned, said it certainly required attention. A year ago a committee was appointed to meet Mr. Evans to see what should be done.—Mr. Stokes moved that a committee consisting of Messrs. Baxter, Woodward and Wright, be appointed to visit the spot and report the result.—This was seconded.—Mr. iteylance moved an amendment that the matter be referred back to the Halton Parish Council, they being the proper authority to look after footpaths. The amendment was put to the meeting and carried. This concluded the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of this Board, was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday afternoon. Mr. J. Knowles presided, and there were also present, the Rev. J. Simpson, Messrs. Taylor, J.P.C.C., A. R. Norman, and T. Lea; the members of the Rural District Council, with Mr. Ashton (clerk) and Mr. G. Ashton (aeputy clerk).</p>
        <p>THE WORKHOUSE. The Master reported, that the Medical Officer had pointed out that scarlet fever prevailed in the district and recommended that the children attending school outside be confined to the House for the present. The Master also reported thst one of H.M. Inspectors visited the House on Wednesday, and inspected the schoolboys. He reported that they hed passed the elementary sub- jects and had done very well. The girls, although they fiad been without a teacher since May, had not declined in proficiency.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>HOUSE COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>THE HOUSE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read copy of correspondence between Mr. Cunnah, the Workhonse master and Mr. Stokes, schoolmaster of Dutton School Board, with reference to sending the children of the Workhouse, other than in- fants, to the Board schools, for elementary education. Mr. Cunnah said he would send three girls on the 19'h inst.—This committee recommrmded that a schoolmis- tress be appointed at £3O without beer money.—alr. Norman asked whether there had been any reply from the Education Department re the school question.—The Clerk : No.—Mr. Norman proposed, as an amendment, that the matter of advertising for a schoolmistress .be deferred for a fortnight, and that the Clerk in the meantime write again to the Education Department and ask for same definite statement in regard to the matter. He thought the Dutton School Board ought to make accommodation for their children. It would be a pity to go and advertise for a schoelmistress.—Mr.Woodward: At the Poor Law Conference held at Chester a short time ago, the Chairman of that Conference,denounzed Work- house education as " Bad, wholly bad, and nothing but bad."—Tho matter was dropped and the Master con- tinuing his report, said the matter of asphalting the Workhouse yard had been left to him (the master). He thought he could manage the work himself.—Mr.Stokes moved the adoption of the minutes —Mr. Williams seconded.— Mr. Norman moved an amendment that the advertising for a schoolmistress be left in abeyance until next Board day.—After some discussion the propesition that the minutes as a whole bepassed, was put to the meeting and eight voted in favour of it. The amendment that the adver- tising for schoolmistress he left in abeyance for a fort- night, was put to the meeting and 12 voted for it. The amendment was therefore declared carried.</p>
        <p>ASSISTANT MATRON.</p>
        <p>Miss Cunnah was appoint-d assistant matron.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>EXTENSION OF THE DINING noom</head>
        <p>EXTENSION OF THE DINING noom</p>
        <p>Mr. Heywood moved a resolution that a committee be appointed to reconsider the plans for the extension of the dining-roona. He proposed that the Chairman of Visiting Committee, vice-chairman of Finance Com- mittee, Chairman of Relief Committee, Admiral Perry and Mr. C. W. Davenport, be appointed as a committee to consider the plena. The proposition was carried.</p>
        <p>LADIES' VISITING COMUITTRP2</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Ladies Visiting, Committee was held on the 21st October. They visited the inmates, and found everything in order. They suggested that the men should be provided witti some amusements, such as dorninoes,draughts and other i nexpensive'games. (Laugh- ter). They also suggestrd that the children should go to some outside school for instruction • also that the in- mates should be encouraged to attend' some other ser- vice during Sunday. They had only one service and that was at 10 o'clock in the morning.—Mrs. Linaker proposed that a tea with a concert be provided for the inmates at the expense of the ladies.—The Clerk said he didn't know whether they had any power to pay for these amusements. But any private individual could subscribe and buy the thins.—Mr. Parr suggested that they subscribe and buy billiard tables. He would give a subscription for a billiard table. kLaughter.)—Mr. Williams said the Rev. J. S'mpson would come to the Workhouse on Sunday afternoon if he could be of any assistance. No doubt other clergy would be glad to come.—The Master said 'chat another concert was pro- mised for the 14th December.—The Clerk was instructed to ascertain their position as to the provision of amuse- ments for the inmates, and also as to the chaplaincy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>MARRIED COUPLE'S QUA RTE R S IN WORKHOUSES'</head>
        <p>MARRIED COUPLE'S QUA RTE R S IN WORKHOUSES'</p>
        <p>The Clerk of the Guardians of St. John, Hampstead, wrote enclosing a petition which they intended to send to the Local Government Board relating to the occupa- tion of married couple's quarters, by inmates, married whilst in the workhouse. The petition set forth the facts if inmates of Hampstead Workhouse ae 66 years, marrying another inmate age 61- and than the couple claiming possession of one of the rooms erected for married couoiPs. After quoting 10 and 11. Victoria, chap. 103. sec. 23, the Guardians expressed the opinion that the objects on the above enactment were to enable an aged couple, who in their declining years were obliged to seek the shelter of tile workhouse, to live together and to thereby mitigate the severity of their misfortu no. They sugges'ed that a measure of legisla- tion which would enable Board of Guardians to grant, or refuse the use of a room in the married couple's quar- ters to persons above the age of ICI years, who had been married during residence in th *,,orkhouse,or within six months of such residence. wa: desirable and necessary. They adod the Runcorn Oasrdi sql,' to support them in the matter.—lt was propoiod this t the Board prepare a petition in support of the rnsmoPfal. This was all the businesa.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>1.0.G.T. TEMPERANCE MISSION LuvGE.</head>
        <p>1.0.G.T. TEMPERANCE MISSION LuvGE.</p>
        <p>The usual weekly esion of this lodge was held in the Mission Room, Academy-street, on Wednes- day, Bro. Sullivan, C.T. presiling. Two candidates were admitted into nie,hership. After the initia- tory ceremony, Bro. Nurse gave a report of the first monthly entertainment which proved very satisfactory. Bro. O'Brien proposed, and Bro. Hey- wood seconded, that the report be adopted with thanks to the committee, and this was carried un- animously Bro. Price gave a recitation, entitled " A True Christmas Story." After arrangements had been made for the next concert, the lodge was closed in the usual way by the Chief Templar.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>EARLESTOWN &amp;ND NEWT3N.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp;ND NEWT3N.</p>
        <p>BURGLARY AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>Between half-past ten on Friday night and seven o'clock on Saturday moraing the residence of Mrs. Edge, of Legh Terrace, Crow-lane, Newton, was burglariously entered, and articles to the value of .£l7 stolen. Miss Edge, schoolmistress at St. Mary's B.C. School, lives with her mother, and when she came downstairs on Saturday morning she found that a gold watch and chain, a jacket and other articles of wearing apparel were missing. Investi- gation showed that the thief or thieves had got into the back yard through the ash box place, and finding the back kitchen window unfastened effected an entrance to the house by this way. Their depredations were confined to the lower regions, including the kitchen and sitting room. Another house in this same terrace was broken into about 12 months ago, and there is a suspicion that the present theft is by the same hands. The police, so far, have no clue.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>DRUNKENNESS.</head>
        <p>DRUNKENNESS.</p>
        <p>At the Town Hall, Newton, on Saturday morning, before Mr. R. Stone,Alice Emmett was charged with soliciting in Earlestown on Friday night, and W5B committed to gaol for 7 days.—James Cannon, of Woolton, for being drunk in Newton on Friday night was fined 2s. 6d. and costs.—On Monday morning before Mr. C. B. F. Borron, at the Earles- town police station, William Marshall, of Stockton- on-Lees, and James Mitchell, of Manchester, were each fined 2s. 6d. and costs for being drunk in Earlestown on Saturday night. ST. JOHN'S CHURCH CHOIR, EA.RLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>Ihe first of a series of social gatherings pro- moted for the purpose of bringing into closer mutual contact the members of St. John's Church choir during the winter months, took place on Monday evening in the Mission Hall. There were about 30 present, and the evening was spent in a very pleasant manner in games of various kinds, interspersed with singing. The arrangements were satisfactorily carried out by Mrs. Latimer and the committee.</p>
        <p>IMPORTANT PROPERTY SALE AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>At the Wellington Hotel on Wednesday evening in the presence of a large company of tradesmen from Earlestown and the surrounding district, Messrs. Burchall and Son brought under the hammer several important lots of shop and cottage property under the trust deed of Mrs. Johnson, late of Earlestown. Mr. R. W. El. Thomas was present as solicitor for the vendors. There were in all six lots, and the whole were disposed of in a little over On hour, the competition being keen and brisk. ;hot 1 consisted of the shop No. 8 Market-street, occupied by Mr. Samuel Duncalfe under a lease for five years from February 1895, at a yearly rental of £52. The bidding began at .£5OO, and closed at .C 1,005 being £5 above the reserve price, and dis- posed of for that amount to Mr. C. Reis cq Bridge- street, Earlestown. Lot 2 was No. 10 Market-st., occupied by Mr. P. MacMahon at a yearly rental of £35. The bidding opened at £6OO and closed at £852, the purchaser by Mr. MacMahon. Lot 3 was the shop No. 12 Market-street, occupied by Mr. K.T. Evans under a lease for 14 years from April 3rd, 1893, at a yearly rent of £45. The competition for this lot opened at £7OO and after some brisk bidding Was purchased by Mr. Evans for £B5l. Lot 4, the shop, 16 Market-street, occupied by Mr. Joseph Stringer under a lease for ten years from Ist Jan., 1894, at a yearly rent of .C 27 68., opened at £4OO, and was bought in by Mr. Reise for £625. Lot 5, 18, Market-street, occupied by Mrs. H. Walton under a lease for five years from 23rd March 1893, at a yearly rent of .C 26 was purchased by Mr. R. Jones, Market-street for £625. Lot 6 consisted of five cottages, Nos. 1,3, 5,7, and 9 Grafton-screet (formerly Back Market-street) yielding an annual rental of £5B 10. This started at £4OO, and closed at .C 507 the purchaser being Mr. E. T. Evans.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>EAIELESTOWN MISSIO N.</head>
        <p>EAIELESTOWN MISSIO N.</p>
        <p>A musical evening in support of the above, of which Mr. G. Dring is at the head, was held on Saturday night. There was a large attendance of the public, and the following programme was very efficiently rendered under the chairmanship of Mr, P. M. Beesley, of Warrington :—Song," Life is a river," Mr. T. Taggar ; song, "The children's home„' Miss Stronach ; song," High tide," Miss Mee; song, "The anchor's weighed," Mr.A.Banton ; recitation, "The land of gold," Mr. T. Taggar ; song, "Daisy song," Miss Mee; song, "Death of Nelson," Mr. Banton, and in response to an encore "The Holy City " ; song, " Uncle John," Miss Mee; organ solo. Mr. S. Dring ; song, "Killarney," Miss Stronach, encore" Ora pro nobis." Miss Crawford very ably accompanied. A vote of thanks was accorded the chairman at the close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>ODDFELLOWS' ANNIVERSARY AT</head>
        <p>ODDFELLOWS' ANNIVERSARY AT</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>The anniversary dinner in connection with the Loyal Viaduct Lodge of oddfellows No. 2686, M.U. (Earles- tcwn) took place at the Wellington Hotel, on Saturday night. There were about 80 cresent and the meal which was provided by r.u. J. W. Therpo, was much en- joyed. After the cloth had been removed,the subsequent pro- ceedings were presided over by Councillor Wiliam Gil- moor, P.P.G.M., the vice-chairman being G.M. James Lawe, amongst the visitors present being Bros. John Tunstall, Ie.P.G.M., Liverpool, and James Brockbank, P.P.G.M. (St. Helens). After the usual loyal toasts had been proposed,and suitably honoured, that of the" Man. cheeter Unity" was given by P.P.G.M.P. Connor,and re- sponded to by Bro. John Tunstall who, in the course of a lengthy speech, referred to juvenile work in the order, and the extent of its operations. He thought it was time that the district undertook juvenile work and he appealed to I all to bring their children in. Addressing himself to I Oddfellowship as regarded females, he dwelt on the necessity of lodges for women. There were a certain number of working women,who for various reasons, did not get married, and it would he an immense boon to them if their way of life could be rendered easier by combination of that character. There were now 31 female lodges,and he wished there were 3,000. Speaking particularly with regard to the Viaduct Lodge,Bro. Tun- stall sail he regarded it as being in a very good position especially when the class of members the lodge included was taken into consideration. He quoted statistics, and pointed out what a vast amount of money was disbursed in sick pay and alluded to the great work the ' society was doing in that respect, and the beneficial in- fluence it was wielding on behalf of the working classes. As regarded the burning question of old age pensione,he said he had always felt that when men became old something should be done for them, and he contended that there should be some assistance f.om Government to help the scheme through. Anything received from Government under those circumstances would not be charity, and there would be no stigma attached to the recipient. He deprecated the system at present in vogue through which men when they became old and indigent often had nothing. but the workhouse before them, and concluded by impressing upon the members of the lodge to do their duty and let it be a pleasure to them to assist their fellow men, and at the same time to help themselves. (Applause.) The toast of the Grand Master and Board of Direc- tors" was given by Bro. P.P.G.M. Tnomas Tunstall,was responded to by Bro. P.P.G.M. James Brockbank, of St. Helens, who said that he sympathised with the Board under existing circumstances, and believed they would pull themselves through the difficulties that were surrounding them at Northampton. The Boari of Directors, they would un- derstand,was the head and front of the whole Order. He pointed out that a Board of Directors at any M.C. was net like the Government of a country, compelled to re- sign when there happened to be an adverse vote, and he considered that had the directors been moved theA.M.C. would have made one of the biggest mistakes it possibly could have done. The MX, was a progressive body, though built upon ground' that had already been made stable by actuarial calculation and scientific thought, and they had to go on from time to time making pro- gress as fast as it was possible under the circumstimces. In the hands of the directors he thought the welfare of the M.U. was well cared for, and that no appeals their ruling was generally considered to be is tut-, right direc- tion. As regarded the whole movement, they must go on ; stagnation would be retrogression. (Applause.) Responding to the toast of the "Viaduct Dodge," Bro. J. S. Thane (secretary) feelingly referred to the loss sus- tained by the lodge in the death of their late Secretary, Br. W. Ball, and regretted that his predecessor had not been longer with them. Speaking of the finances and progress of the lodge, he said they commenced the year 1890 with a capital of £2,400, £2,300 of which was I invested in cottage property. They had 375 members on the books at present, and 55 members of other lodges paying into that lodge. A pleasing feature was that the membership of the lodge was steadily increasing, for during the last five years there had been a gain of 98 members, or twice as great a gain as in any preceding five years, during the last 20 years. This year. as far as initiations were concerned, they were still up to the average of the last five years. The income of the lodge in 1895 was about £6BO, and the expenditure £610; sick pay £360, funeral benefits £loo,with various other items making up the expenditure. Other toasts were proposed and honoured, and during the evening songs, &amp;c., were given by Bros. G. Wright, J. Marsh, William Mawdsley, J. Ryan, James Appleton, James Phillips, A. Limond, R. Lythgoe, J. Fitzpatrick, &amp;c.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <p>THE MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>FURTHER NOMINATIONS.</p>
        <p>The following further nominations were received on Saturday : ST. AUSTIN'S WARD.—Mr. R. B. Matthews (C), pro- posed by Mr. Robert Pierpoint,M.P., seconded by Alder- man Sutton,and supported by Messrs. H. B. Melville, T. Beesley, J. McDowell, R. Jo'inson, G. Wright, H. R. Horobin, E. Potts, and W. Hands. He is further pro- posed by Mr. J. Tilley, seconded by Mr. F. Bennett.— Mr. James Hepherd (C) malster, SSocktori Lodge, is pro- posed ny Mr. Robert Pierpoint,M.P., seconded by Alder- man Sutton, and assented to by Messrs. H. B. Melville, W. Johnson, T. Beesley, J. McDowell, G. Wright, B. R. Horobin, E. Potts and W. Hands.—ln another paper Mr. Hepherd is proposed by Mr. John Tilley seconded by Mr. Fred Bennett, Mr. William Burton (C), farmer, Mill-lane,has the Same proposer and seconder, and the same agsentors, as Mr. Matthews. BEWSBY WARD .—Councillor Shaw Green (L), account- ant, 6, Winmarleigh-street, is proposed by Mr. T. Morris, seconded by Mr. G. M. E. Cunnington, and sup- ported by Messrs. T. Evans, E. Healey, W. G. Stephens, B. Gill, J. Poole, J. Parkes, sen., S. Holding, and C. Biddlestone, whilst in a second paper his proposer is Mr. Joseph Owen, his seconder Mr. Samuel Gough, and his assentors Messrs. W. Ford, J. Wright, T. Trow,J. Quinn, T. Field, J. Poole, B. Wycherley and T. Moody —Mr. E. Aukland (C) is further proposed by Dr. Nuttall,seconded by Mr. Henry Taylor, and supported by Messrs. J. Morrell, J. Ashton, R. W. Collins, J. Bush, G. Plumb, J. Knight, P. White and W. Appleton. FAIRFIELD WARD.—Michael McCarthy (L), 36, Man- chester-road, physician and surgeon, is proposed by Mr. Wilkinson Hough, seconded by Mr. J. W. Jeffery, and assented to by Messrs. J. Corry, W. Unsworth, J. Phoenix, Edward Heckle, J. Iw3ns, P. Garrity, R. Davies, and J. Ford. Mr. Henry Johnson Longton (C), the Alders, Cross-lane, Newton-le-Willows, has fer a pro- poser Mr. R. A. Naylor, for a seconder Mr. Thomas Joseph Hulse, and for assentors Messrs. W. Dooley, J. T. Whittle, G. T. Brimelow, J. D. Brirnelow, A. M. Brund- rett, W. H. Harrison, and John Rigby. In a second paper is proposer is Mr. Thomas Moores, his seconder Mr. Robert Butterworth.</p>
        <p>HOWLEY WARD.—Mr. Mounfield (L) is further pro- posed by Mr. John Gandy, seconded by Mr. J. S. Hill, and supported by Messrs. J. Drury, E. Pickavance, jun., J. Williams, T. S. Carter, G. Stephenson, A. Horrocks, G. Howard, and J. Albinson.—Councillor Arthur Ben- nett, (C), chartered accountant, Salisbury-et., is pro- posed by Mr. John Evans, seconded by Mr. R. W. Antwiss, and supported by Messrs. W. Lee, W. Clare, P. White, P. Makin, W. Jarman, F. Brown, G. A. Howard, and J. Whitfield. In a second paper Mr. Bennett is pro- posed by Mr. H. J. Longton, seconded by Mr. William Nelson. Tows HALL WARD.—Councillor Shillcock(C) is further proposed by Eliza Whittaker, seconded by Maria Bar- tour, and assented to by Alice Holbrook, Annie L. Williams' Elizabeth Truslove, Eliza, Andrews, Margaret Powell, Hannah Rigby, Ann Kershaw, and Sue Bal- shaw.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD WARD.—Mr. Alfred Jolley (C) is proposed by Mr. T. Ireland, seconded by Mr. Thomas Norman, and supported by Messrs. J. W. Marsh, T. Hankey, S. smith, T. Nixon, T. Bate, F. W. Greenwood, J. Sher- lock, and J. Boscow.</p>
