      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>PEN ICETII.</head>
        <p>PEN ICETII.</p>
        <p>Cramer CLUB.—On Saturday night, the members of the Penketh Wesleyan C.C., at the invitation of Mr. C. E. Parker, met at the Wesleyan school for their annual ' re-union. The Sunday school teachers and members of the young men's and young women's Sunday school classes were also invited. A substantial tea was pro- vided, the guests being timed to arrive at 5.30 p.m. After tea was disposed of, the reom was cleared and games were indulged in for a short period prior to the concert. The programme of this was as follows :—Songs were ably rendered by Mrs. Parker, Miss Hilda Garnett, Messrs. W. T. Wilkinson "The Holy City," A. Appleton "Golden Guineas," G. E. Smith "Allan Water." A violin solo was well rendered by A. Watson accompanied by Mr. Wilmot Gandy on the pianoforte. A. Seed also contributed a recitation. During the course of the even- ing Mr. A. Seed, the secretary, read a report of the late season, which spoke well for the club. Mr. J. A. Smith, the treasurer, also read his report, which showed a slight deficiency,due to the heavy expenses incurred dur- ing last season for pavilion, attending to ground, &amp;e. the following gentlemen addressed the gathering, and thanked Mr. Parker for his kindness :—Messrs. R. Gar- nett, J. Watson, J. Lewis, A. Appleton. Mr. C. E. Parker responded. The following ladies prepared the tea : Mrs. Wilson Mimes A. Hobson, A. Hughes, A. Boardman, C. Wilkinson, J. Wolversen, H. M. Maiuley, M. Ashton, and L. Bowled. HOMEY AND FOOTBALL.—rhe Park Hockey Club were away at Stockton Heath on Saturday,trying conclusions with their old rivals there. Both clubs were formed about the same time, and a friendly rivalry is felt as to which shall make the better progress. The two previous matches resulted in a win for Stockton Heath the first time,and a draw the second. Penketh hoped to gain the day on Saturday, but although they pressed hard and forced many corners, they were unable to score as well as they ought to have done, and the acme stood at the call of time Stocton Heath 3 goals,Penketh one goal. H. Mainley scored for Penketh.—The football team had a fixture at Haydock,but went two men short,and as two more had to leave the field shortly after the start, the game was abandoned with the scere—flaydock Reserve 2 goals, Pinketh nil. BILLIARD lleeinicar.—A billiard handicap in connec- tion with the Liberal Club has just come to a close, and has proved very interesting throughout. The games ev,re very even, the winner unexpectedly turning up in the person of H. Mainley whom the handicapping com- mittee had injudiciously placed on the lhiit mark. 'there were three prizes, the winners being :-Ist, H. J. Main- ley receives 40 ; 2nd, W. Woods, scratch; 3rd, F. Wilkinson, scratch. MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT ASSOCIATION.—The weekly meeting of the above was held in the Wesleyan school- room on Wednesday night. In the absence of the ap- pointed lecturer, Mr. W. Heale gave a most interesting paper on " Characteristics." Considering the short time for preparation, the paper was extremely geoel,the subject being dealt with in a very able manner. An interesting discussion ensued at the close of the paper. Mr. A. Appleton occupied the chair.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-31_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>ST. JOHN'S PRESBY fERIAN CHURCH</head>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S PRESBY fERIAN CHURCH</p>
        <p>List Sunday was the anniversary day of the Sunday schOol in connection with St. John's Presbyterian Church. The service in the morning was well attended, and the Rev. J. Warnock (pastor) delivered a thought- ful sermon. A children's service occupied the afternoon and an address was given by the Rev. J. H. Scott, of Manchester, who is a well-known writer in the "Pres- byterian." Mr. Scott also preached in the evening when his sermon was based on the words "When the Almighty was yet with tee, when my children were about me."' Job, 29c. sv. The rev, gentleman's discourse was lis- tened to with deserved attention. There was a large congregation, and, the collections during the day were regarded as satisfactory.</p>
