        <p>MUNICIPAL ELIICTION, 1396. TO THE BURGESSES OF ORFORD WARD. ' LADIES AND GENTLEMEN.— Iraving received a hearty and nruutimons itwitation from a large representative body of the Ratepayers, to offer myself as a Candidate to represent you in the Town Council, I have great pleasure in consenting to place my services at your disposal. I have alreadylreceived promises of support frorn'influential and highly respected members of both political parties, which is especially gratifying. because I am most anxi us to do my utmost for the benefit of the working classes, irrespective of party. Having the time at my disposal, I shall, if elected, make it my special duty to find out what is required to improve the condition of the inhabitants of Orford Ward, and if there is anything which demands Immediate attention you may depend upon my using every effort to carry what I may con- sider to be for the best interests of the Ward. It is my intention to personally wait upon every I voter during the election, and I shall esteem it a favour if you will kivdly reserve your votes until I have had the pletsure of sailing upon you. I am, Ladles and Gantlemen, Your Obedient Servant, HENRY THORNTON. " The Laurels," Fearnhead, Oct. 3rd, 1898.</p>
        <p>BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON. AVIJNICIPAL ELECTION, NOV. 2Nrr, 1896. AIL TO THE ELECTORS OF LATCHFORD WARD. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN, I have much pleasure in complying with the numerous requests which I have received during the past few weeks and beg to offer myself as a Candidate for your suffrages at the forthcoming Municipal Election. As you are aware. I have a very substantial interest in the Ward, both in regard to my business and as a property owner and having also resided for a quarter of ;‘, century amongst von, I claim to be thoroughly acquainted with your needs and the special requirements of the district. Seeing how rapidly all vacant land is being acquired for building purposes, I would strongly support any scheme which would provide open spaces and play grounds for children in every Ward of the town. In regard to the repayment of the cost of street making, which bears so heavily upon property owners, and, therefore. also upon tenants. I am of opinion that the system of deferred payments might be at once extended to a period of seven or ten years. I am glad to 'see that the opposition to the provision of wharfage accommodation on the river has been overcome, and I shall support a still farther extensioq thereof, so that every assistance may be given to manufacturers and others who desire to take full advantage of the great natural facilities which the Mersey affords. I think it is quite time that we were supplied with bettor and cheaper gas and that eleAric lighting should be intro- duced without delay. If you do me tho honour to elect mu you will find me, as your friend and neighbour, always ready to investigate any well-founded complaint and to do my best to remedy it ; and at the same time most willing to assist in promoting any scheme for the improvement of the town and for the reduc- tion of our expenditure. I am, Ladies &amp; Gentlemen, Your obedient Ser.,ant, JAMES STRANGE.</p>
