        <p>BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON. UNICIPAL ELECTION, NOV. lwr, 1896. 11l TO TUB BURGBSSIIS OF ST. JOHN'S WARD. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN,— A large and representative 'number of the Ratepayers of this Ward are anxious that I sh mid represent them on the Town Council, and I have at their earnest request decided to contest the Ward and now seek your support. During the nine years I was in the Town Council I endeavoured to serve you to the best of my ability. I always, both by my voice and vote, opposed what I believed to be extravagant and unnecessary expenditure, &amp;nil will not hesitate to do so in the future. At the same tim‘, I strongly supported every movement which in my judgment was conducive to the best (interest of the ratepayers. I cordially supported the freeing of our Public Library, the erec.lon of the New Baths, and you will doubtless remember the very active part I took in advocating and co:npleting the purchase of the Water Works (and but f3r my action in the matter it might still have been in abeyance; and if the pur- chase had to be made now it could only be done at a greatly Increased cost, which would have meant another £53,000 out of the ratepayers' pockets) which has proved 60 be a great and increasing advantage to the Borough of Warrington. I, therefore, appeal to you for your support on November let, believing that I shall command your confidence and approval. I am, la lies isnd Gentlemen, Your Obedient Servant, JOHN HENDERSON FLEMING. Springfield Street, October 7th, 1893.</p>
        <p>BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON. M UNICIPAL ELECTION, NOVEMBER, 1896. HOWLEY WARD. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN.— I have much pleasure In complying with the numerous requests which I have received during the past few weeks and beg to offer myself as a candidate for your suffrages at the forthcoming Municipal Election. As you are aware, I have a substantial interest in the Ward, both as an employer of labour, and as a property owner, and having also resided in the Ward for upwards of thirty years, I claim to be thoroughly acquainted with your needs, and the special requirements of the district. The land which was secured in the Ward for an open space or playground has so far been quite useless, owing to its not having been laid out for the purpose intended. I should sup- port any well-considered scheme for its improvement. I am of opinion that true economy and efficiency go hand in hand, and I should support a policy of steady progress, and should oppose the expendltnre of large sums of money upon fanciful or impracticable objects. • The River Mersey affords great natural facilities to the town generally, and to this Ward in particular, which as yet have only been very slightly utilized, and I would support any proposals by means of which the business et the town would be improved, trade encouraged, and the public benefited. There are other matters of vital Importance involving serious responsibility upon the town which will shortly have to be dealt with, and these shr.il receive my most careful consideration. Should you favour me with your confidence, you will find in me the friend of el and enemy of none, always ready to assist in promoting any scheme for the welfare and prosperity of the town, and prepared to do my duty at all times with- out fear or favour. I remain, Ladies and Gentlemen, Your Obedient Servant, JOSEPH MOUNFIELD. Walton, October 21st.</p>
