        <p>BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON. u-NICIPAL ELECTION 1896. TO THE ELEC- TORS OF FAIRFIELD WARD. LADIEE AND GENTLEMEN. I have pleasure in responding to the nnmerous invitations which I have received, and beg to offer myself as a Candidate for your suffrages at the ensuing Municipal Election. My intention, if you elect me, ls to act upon thoroughly Independent lines, and to apply myself to the consideration of the business of the Borough without regard to personal or private interests. I have resided in your midst for a sufficient time to enabl e rue to thoroughly understand the special requirements of the Ward and what is necessary to alter the surroundings so as to improve it from a hlih and sanitary roint of v ew. It is quite time that an Open Space for a Children's Play- ground was secured for this Ward, and I shall do my utmost to secure one without delay. The proposals of the Manchester Corporation in regard to their sewage disposal scheme are of such vast importance to the future health and prosperity of this town that I shall be prepared to give my utmost assistance and professional know- ledge to such a. solution of the problem as will prevent any disastrous effects resulting. and secure the advantages which belong to the town aud are priceless in value. Living amongst you as I do, if you do me the honour to elect me I shall at all times be ready to personally investigate any subject which you wish to bring before my notice, aid will on all occasions do my utmost to serve your interests. I am, Ladles and Gentlemen, Your obedient servant, AicCARTRY. 36, Manchester Road, Warrington, October 14th, 1895.</p>
        <p>BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON. MUNICIPAL ELECTION, 1896. TO THE BLECTORS OF TOWN HALL WARD. LADIES AND GNSTLEMEN,— At the requestof numerous ratepayers in the Town Hall Ward II have ioonsented once more to place my services atyour disposal as a Candidate for the representation of the Ward in the Town Council. lam the more wiling to do this because last year you showed so large an appreciation of my candidature as to place me within four votes of success. This year, I am convinced, my success is assured. I need hardly remind you of my qualifications for the re- presentation of the Ward in the Council. Suffice it to say I have lived for upwards of thirty years in the Ward, and nearly the whole of that time have been: actively engaged in business therein. During the next few years the Council will be largely occupied in the consideration of schemes for the benefit of the town, involving the outlay of very large sums of money. Whilst in favour of improving to the utmost the appearance and resources of our Town, I shall always be anxious to save, as far as possible, the pockets if tbe ratepayers. In this direction I believe there is plenty of room for the exercise of care and vigilance, and as a considerable ratepayer myself I shall scrupulously, safeguard the interests of the rate- payers. I believe that in various respects a great improvement is required in the direction and control of the Town's affairs, and it is on that account, and not from any motives of merely personal ambition or personal interest, I feel called upon to offer to devote some portion of my time to work on behalf of the town, and if you will do me the honour to elect me, I shall try faithfully and conscientiously to bring about that improvement. Whilst deprecating the introduction of po.itics into muni el _ pal affairs, I yet feel that all right-minded persons must be shocked by events of recent occurrence in the Council, and must be anxious by their votes to express their sense of the ill-treatment which has been accorded to one of the most useful members of the Council. If you elect me I shall always protest against such behaviour as that referred to in the name of decency and fair play. As the time available is short, I may fillet it imoossible to call on many vaters. I feel confident I shall none the less receive very large and very kindly support from you. Relying upon the favour of your votes, I am, Ladles and Gentlemen, Faithfully yours, GEORGE CLAY. Park Buildings, Crosfield Street, Warrington. 13th October, 1896.</p>
