        <p>VISIT OF THE .CZAR, AUTOCRAT OF ALL THE RUSSIAS, TO " VIE PANTHEON." HIS IMPERIAL MAJESTY, the CZAR OF RUSSIA, during his vi-it ei the French nation, took the opportunity of visiting " TIH PANTHEON,' at Paris. We ityite the people of Manchester, not to imitate his Majest 3 example by paying a visit to "The Pantheon." at Pa. a," but to emulate his Imperial Highness by paying a visit to "THE PANTHEON," GAYTHORN, 'MANCHESTER. TIIE PANTHEON. A visit to " The Pantheon " will prove both instructive and remunerative It is the Finest Furnishing Warehouse in Manchester, has an immense frontage of 110 feet. five ' storeys high, with a capacity of over 1.0 0,000 square lest of showroom, most brilliantly lit throughout with eloctrict light, has Bite to all the floors, n contains ten times the stock of furniture of any other house in Manchester. You can pay v33lt to" The Pantheon.' walk through it with perfect COttl- fort and pleasure, see if there is anything you consider a bargain, entirely at your ease, and in perfect libere.pleasing yourself whether vou buy or not. If you we h to furnish a house for £lO or £5,000, WORSLEY'S can execute the order with marvellous rapidity, and you will effect a saving of quite 50 per cent. One visit to "The Pantheon," at Man- chester, will amply demonstrate the truth of these asser- tions. CARPET D EPA RTMENT. This Department has had special and careful attention. All that experience and accurate knawledge could acquire in all the markets of the world has been realised. Most carpet departments after a lapse it years des elop a quantity of soiled stock, which is very objectionable. The whole of the stock at "THE PANTHEONeloweveres spotlessly cleeteperfectly new from the loom, the best ef patterns,the newest and most unique designs. The product of the best manufacturers In Kidderminster and Yorkshire are now on show at "THE PANTHEON." Ineian,Turkieleand English Carpets in great variety, at keen, cutting prices. Immense quantities of Per- sian, Egyptian, Turkish, French,and English Rugs,and every form of Skin Rug All carpets will be sold at old prime. We may indicate briefly the lines upon which this Department is worked by the following prices,for.despite the recent advance in ea-pets, WORSLEY'S low prices for standing qualities re- main the same :- Best 5 Frame Brussels, 38 9d per yard. Special 13russl s, 2s 9cl per yard, British-made Axminsters, 3s lid per yard. Ditto ditto, 5s 6d per yaiel. Ditto (Wool Back), 5s lid per yard. t Turkey Carpets,though dearer owing to the disturbances in the East. are offered at old prices. WORSLEY'S being direct importers of these Carpets are able to give exceptienal value. Vinest Deccan Rugs. 6ft by 3ft Pin, 12s 9d. Houle Rugs, Es 9d, 10s 9d. 15s 9d, 21s 6d. Finest Mirzapore Rugs, 6ft by 3ft, 21s. Extraordinary Cheap Seamless Carpets in a wadi ty of new makes from 14s 9d. A magnificent sh w Space has been allotted for the display of well seasoned Linoleums aud Oilcloths,all the newest pat- terns in colouring end design, at prices from letel to 3s 6d per yard. CABINET DEPARUIENT. This Department, which is the largest, and harms the Chief Feature at WORSLEY'S, contains, without fear of can- tradiction, the largest, the most complete, and the most beautiful stock outside Loudon. 30.) Bedroom Suites from £3 17s. 6d. to £l5O are on show. 150 Dining-room Saites and 150 Drawing-roorn Suites, from £4 10s. to 85ge., are also on show. This will give you an idea of how varied and ex- tensive a stock of furniture it mast be. Of course', accom- panying these chief features in the display there are all the thousands of articles which are necessary for fume eing. The grandest display of Bedsteads in the North of England, chiefly by these celebrated makers Messrs. Hokin and Srwell, of Birmingham. are artistically shown, fitted complete with the usual accoutrements. BEDROOM SUITES. The show of Bedroom Suites comprise the following leading lines :- No. 1. A Very Pretty Enamelled Suite, suitable for girl's bedroom. Price sgs. No. 2. Solid Walnut Bedroon Suite, very beautifully carved, suitable for young lady's bedroom, containieg a very pretty wardrobe of unique design, with ceystal bevelled mirrors ; dr&amp; ming table, artistic in skape, and fitted with every convenience ; washstand, with Minton tile top and back; pedestal cupboard, three chairs, acid towel rail. Price 7igs. N.8.-We wish it to be clearly understood that the phrase Bedroom Suite means, in every case, Wardrobe, Oreseing- tab.e, Washstand, Pedestal-cupboard, Three Chairs end Towel Rail. No. 3. Bachelor's Bedroom Suite complete, exquisitely carved in bag relief, very prettily fitted with crystal bevelled mirrors, and ,handsomely chased brass work. Price 10gs. No. 4. Special Bargain, well worth examination. The " Lonsdale Bedroom Suite, in satin-wood. This suite is exceedingly pretty, and forms the most elegant decorati n for a lady's bedchamber. It. is solid throughout, anti polished In the new artistic green shade. It is a quaint Mediaeval style and very highly comm. nded. Price 14gs. No, 5. The " Wliarnecliffe " Bedroom Suite, bo richly figured pollard oak. This wood is well-known as being the most beautiful, smooth, and decorative of any wocei extant. The colouring of this suite is richness itself, rnellcwed and beautified by ripe old age, Price, £2l. No. 6. " Prince Edward " Bedroom Suite, in solid American Walnut, 6ft wardrobe, 4ft dressing-table and washstand. This bedroom suite is a mass of excellent carving, in the latest has relief style. It is fitted throughont with every modern convenience, the crystal bevelled mirrors are very quaintly arranged in " Saracenic " style. Price, £25. No. 7. Egyptian Bedroom Suite, handsomely decorated, 6ft wardrobe, 4ft dressing-table and washstand. Price, 28ga. No. 8 Massive Pollard Oak Bedroom Suite, large 6ft wardrobe, 4il 6in dressing-table and washstand cemplet,e ; made throughout of the finest figured Pollard Oak, so univer- sally admit ed teroughout Manchester. Price. £35. No. 9. Inlaid Rosewood Bedroom Suite, exquisite in style, beautifully finished, and original in design ; inlaid with ivory and tuerwood. Price, £45. No. 10. Chiepeneale Bedroom Suite. An exact reproduc- tion of the famous Chippendale style ; 61t wardrobe, 41t drassinges.ble and washstand. Price, £55. Nt 11. Pollard Cak Bedroom Suite. A fine, handsome massive suite ; well worth £lOO. Pri..e. £65. We have given this brief list in order that our readers may ' form a faint idea of the immensity of the stock at WOES- , LEY'S. and we should rec mmend all intending purchasers to pay a visit to' Ti. E PAN THEON"tiefore deciding:the visit will certain ybe interesting and instructive, and cannot fall to give satistact ion. .• THE PANTHEON "is worth a visit, as it contains the finest exhibition of furniture to be seen out- side London. Everybody has perfect liberty to inspect the lain figures, The prices are stock, %tech is all marked in P fixed prices, and will not be altered for enybody. All goods delivered carriage paid. Purchasers of any of the bedroom suites may have their money returned if not satisfied. Our next advertisement will cotitain a list of a portion of the Diniugeoom Suites, succeeded by one conttining Draw- ing-room cuites and other miscellaneous furniture. ECOND-HAND DEPARTMENT, Mr. Worsley wishes to draw special attention to this de- partment. which forms one of the most attractive features of " THE PANTHEON." In this department there is every' variety of ferniture for Drawing-room, Dining-room, Bed- room, Library, and Office. 'The most astonishing bargains can be procured. Mr. Worsley has agents purchasing second- hand furniture from the le taing business centres of Great, Britain, and the stoc thereby obtained will enable the work- ing man to procure a House of Furniture Complete for £2O, worth double the money. It will also enable the merthant to furnish with high-class, good old hand-made furniture by makers of great repute at prices which pulvet ise the com- petition of the new London furniture. Be Cautious not to Purchase New London Furniture, There cannot possibly be any satisfaction in purchasing the new jerry-veneered onstage- boxes, which the makers have the effrontery to call furniture, and which are knocked together in the East End of London. Second-hand furniture by good old makers having stood the test of time can be depended upon to give satisfaction. WOKS- LEY 'S have quite an army of cabinetmakers. French polishers, and upholsterers whom they chiefly employ In renovating the finest specimens of second-baud furniture, sine, if necessary, remodelling the same. They have an enor- mous stock of Antique Furniture, and exhibit the most in teresting collecten ever placed on show in this district. As a sample of the bargains that they can offer in this depart- ment they cite the case of a Sideboard by Doveston. Buffet and Hull cost £B5. Price, 18gs•• CoNCLUSION, WORSLEY'S give notice that their terms of business will be cash, at the market prices, but will have no objection to allow part of the purchase money to remain over,or to be raid by instalments, subject to a charge of 10 per cent for the convenience) This will sweep away the unequal aid pernici- ous effects of the Hire System, which perhaps is responsible for more misery to young people than any system that was ever invented. The Hire System has ;been denounced over and over again by Ceenty Court Judges throughout the land as being little better than the money lending system, Pur- chasers have to pas through the eyes and nose for everything they have to g t on the hire system, and then they are liable to lese every puny they have paid, if they miss one of their monthly or weekly payments. WORSLEY'S will try to help the public in this matter, and instead of having to pay high prices they will sell them the goods at the plainly marked cash prices, ai d charge them straightforwardly in a percentage upon the money unpaid. They have seen cases where chairs have been charged 7s. 6,1. each on the hire system, which they sell at 3s. 61. WORSLEY'S will be delighted ii you call and inspect their stock, to prove the undeniable truth of their assertions. We H. WORSLEY, THE MAMMOTH HOUSE FURNISHER, " THE PANTHEON," GAYTHORN, MANCHESTER; and at "TILE PANTHEON," WESTG-ATE ARCADE, BRADFORD. (Established 1841.) All City-road cars pass Business hours-- WORSLHY'S. 9 to 7. • •</p>
