        <p>dressed and carried to a low, easy lounge, still without speakin,. Rugg brought his breakfast, and he ate it 'silence, then becoming sternly thoughtful. About the middle of the afternoon he aroused himself, inquiring: "Is not that the sound of wheels? Isn't it dine for Mr. Warner to arrive ?" "Just about time, my lord. But I don't hear wheels, and there's no cab on the avenue, n2i lord." The baron stirred uneasily. "I had a strange dream lagt night, Rugg," he said. "A remarkable dream! You watched bY me all night ?" "No, my lord. My lady came in and sent nie off to bed. My lady watched you." Lord Champney started. "Could the dream—vague and unreal as it seems —could it have been reality ?" he asked himself, fe a tone too low for the butler's ears. "Was it Bar' bara's kisses I felt—Barbara's tears that bathed nli face? Impossible 1" He tried to recall the experience which seemed to him a dream. He thought of his wife, putting from him the remembrance of yesterday's expert- ences, and a strange softness relaxed his sterit features, a strange light glowed in his eyes. "I was very near death yesterday, Rugg ?" be said, softly. " Yes, my lord," said the butler. "Poor Lad? Barbara saw your boat out half a mile, I should think, and she roused the men, and had us all at Cranny beach waiting for you. My poor lady ! She looked like death, my lord. She just sunk on her knees and prayed there, and when she fainted I thought she was dead, if you had perished, it would have killed Lady Barbara, my lord." The baron looked at his servitor in utter amaze' ment. "She grieved for me, then? She was anxious?" "She was almost frantic, my lord." Lord Champney covered his face with his hand. "Was she acting?" he thought. "Is she so double-faced as this? Was she making a show of grief, hoping in her heart I would be lost? Or can it be, under all her faults, after all her stray- lugs, she has conceived a love for me 7 There Ii some mystery here I cannot fathom. Those kisses last night—those tears !—I must see her! I must know ? " He uncovered his face. "Rugg," he said, "go to the Lady Barbara and tell her I desire her presence." "Yes, my lord," said the butler' arising. "And there comes a cab. Mr. Warner has comet my lord." "He has? Then you need not go to the Lad? Barbara. I will see Mr. Warner first." His lordship little knew what that decision, ar parently so trifling, would cost him. A few minutes later, Felix Warner, all anxietl and excitement, hurried into his presence. (To be continued).</p>
