        <p>f'l OLD MINING AS A PROFITABLE INVESTMENT. Parties with a little spare cash at their command would do writ to ponder over the following remarks. It is a well known fact that many worthy families in this and other counties laid the foundation of their fortunes by investing in legitimate mining enterprises. What has beeff done Lefore can be done again, but investors ehouid make strict and careful enquiries before embarking in undertakings of this description. There can be n., doubt that if a judicioul selection be made of the various public companies now work- ing, the shares in which (owing to the great depression) are quoted at merely nominal figures, and an investment =ado say in half a dozen, we dare sate'y say that in the course of a few months, an investor would see his money doubled or even trebled. We do not advocate gambling or take prices by the so-called " Cover System," but advise our clients to ealculate the aunt- ber of shares they can take up and pay tor, to loci them up and watch the price lists. When a profit is shown, wire or send orders to sell, unless trustworthy advice is received fro centrary. Not to be alarmedat a slight fall in price—in manY instances prices are put down by market mtnipulation not because of any falling off in this intrinsi vatue of the mine—but wire for information,and if there is any legitimatro reason ior the fall, sell out sal cut the loss. By following the tr.b.ive faithfully, risks are reduced to S minimum and large profits can be wade. We may mention another form of mining investment whieb has proved wonderfully profitable in the c,trrse of the par..' few years. we iefee to exploration and developasent sped"; cates. These syndicates are formed to explore certain golo fields, discover and open out mines, Ac. With undertaking, of this kind a small amount of capital only is required. Clainl can be purchased first hand from proipector-s or discoverer; and acquired by agents of the syndicate, and proved an developed to an extent suffieient to warrant the formation Is separate companies to work them. Now a Syndicate of this description will frequently acquire sufficient claims or grants for a dozen companies, the sale a a single group alone paying back the entire ca7tital two or three times over, so that in the event of a successful floaW tion of half a dozen companies, the snareholders in the Ori- ginal Syndicate would hive their capital returned meal times over in cash besides retaining a large interest in tbe various companies form d A preliminary Syudit•ate of tho description is no v ha course of negeciatien, not. for the Purs pose Of discovering gold claims, hut to acquire a number ub claims already found and proved. The country to which this ,Syndicate proposes to carry MI! Its first operations has been one of the richest geld produCer° in the world, but there has b-r n no rush or exeitementra: In the days of Caliiornian, Austratiin. South African as„ Westralian discoveries, for the simple reason that 1 has Ow' been advertised. Prospectors are quietly toning along# mining and crushing the ore and extracting the gold by yert crude and ancient methods and ,king good profir,s, 00 withstanding the p im.tiye procesies employ ed. he Projector of his Syndicate having been engaged in ftb,t mini3g industry in the country for several years, is WI! acquaint -d with the field, and has every confidence that whet these mines are taken up all w Aral lry modern* methods large and profitable returus must be made" modern, *methods Further particulars can be obtained of 1 ANDREWS &amp; AMPANY, MINING AGENTS AND ififOLDBRS. 105, LONDON WALL. LOND. IS, B.C. 23 Years' Experience in England, Atrica, and South America. Mining Shares bought and -oid at elosa Market PriCes• Correspondence invited. Telegraphic Address. •• OFICINA. LON vox." Bankers: BROWN, JANSEN &amp; Co.</p>
