        <p>WARRINGTON TE &amp;D ES AND LABOUR COUS CIL. The annual meeting of this Council was held at the Waggon and Horses Hotel on Tuesday even- ing. Mr. James Battersby occupied the chair, and there were also present—Messrs. A. Lang, T. B. Malkin, Oakden Morris,Sambrook, Whittaker,New- all, Nimmo, S. Virright, (LP., T. Barnes, J. Crimes, J. Jackson, G. Wilcox, J. Bennion, and W. Bullock (secretary). The Secretary's report was submitted to the meeting and approved. THE ELECTION OF OFFICERS. It was moved that Mr. BattersUy be re-elected chairman of the Council, and Mr. Lang was also proposed for this office., On the first show of hands the voting was equal but after the candi- dates had retired from toe room another vote was taken and Mr. Lang was elected. Mr. Morrie, the delegate of the cabinetmakers, was elected vice- president, and Mr. Oakden aii.l Ar. Bullock were re-elected to the office ..of trea.urer and secretary respectively. The follow,ng were elected on the committee in addition to the officers :—Messrs. Bat teraby, Malkin, Nim MO, Sambrook, and Jackson. THE JUBILEE OF THE CORPORATION. The CH AIRMAN said that some tune ago a email committee was appointed to wait upon the Town Clerk with respect to the jubilee in the Corpora- tion. The committee consisted of Mr. Malicia, Mr. Wright, the secretary and hulloed. They saw the Town Clerk by appointment and he gave them all the information he could on the ruatt•tr. He was not certain about the date, but it was expected that the celebrations would be on April 3rd, the day the Corporation attained its jubilee. The Town Clerk expected that the friendly socieries,trades societies, and all unions of that sort would take part in a procession. There would also Lie treats to aged people and young people, special medals in com- memoration of the event would be struck, and it was also expected that there would be a fireworks display. The Town theik was unable to give them any details of the arrangements, but he said he would bring the matter before the Mayor, and communicate further With the secretary of the Council. Ile (the Chairman) tioptd the C,)uncil would take up the work in earnest at the proper time, and show the people of Warrington that the trades unions were not a myth.—Mr. Malkin said the deputation was informed that the celebration would take place on the 3rd Apill, which was the .date of the incorporation of Lite borough. That happened next year on a Saturda3, which would be a very convenient day for tilem.—Mr. Wright said he understood that the celebrations would ex- t•-md over three days.—.lhe chairman hoped the delegates would bring the matter before their res- pective societies.</p>
